const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"London. A Room in the palace.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD, attended; JOHN OF GAUNT, with other NOBLES","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Old John of Gaunt, time-honoured Lancaster,","Hast thou, according to thy oath and band,","Brought hither Henry Hereford thy bold son,","Here to make good the boisterous late appeal,","Which then our leisure would not let us hear,","Against the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray?"],"id":2,"m":"Old John of Gaunt, honoured Duke of Lancaster, have you, as you swore and promised, brought your bold son Henry Hereford here to prove the angry accusation he made recently against the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray, which I had no time to hear then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["I have, my liege."],"id":3,"m":"I have, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Tell me, moreover, hast thou sounded him","If he appeal the Duke on ancient malice,","Or worthily, as a good subject should,","On some known ground of treachery in him?"],"id":4,"m":"Tell me also: have you found out from him whether he accuses the Duke out of some old grudge, or rightly, as a good subject should, on clear evidence of treason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["As near as I could sift him on that argument,","On some apparent danger seen in him","Aim'd at your Highness, no inveterate malice."],"id":5,"m":"As far as I could get it out of him on that point, it is because of some obvious danger he has seen threatening your Highness, not any long-held hatred."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Then call them to our presence: face to face","And frowning brow to brow, ourselves will hear","The accuser and the accused freely speak.","High-stomach'd are they both and full of ire,","In rage, deaf as the sea, hasty as fire."],"id":6,"m":"Then call them before me. Face to face and frowning brow to brow, I will hear the accuser and the accused speak freely. They are both proud and full of anger; in their rage they are as deaf as the sea and as hasty as fire."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Attendants, with BOLINGBROKE and MOWBRAY","id":7},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Many years of happy days befall","My gracious sovereign, my most loving liege!"],"id":8,"m":"May my gracious king, my most loving lord, have many years of happy days!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Each day still better other's happiness","Until the heavens, envying earth's good hap,","Add an immortal title to your crown!"],"id":9,"m":"May each day bring you more happiness than the last, until heaven, envying earth's good fortune, adds an immortal title to your crown!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["We thank you both; yet one but flatters us,","As well appeareth by the cause you come;","Namely, to appeal each other of high treason.","Cousin of Hereford, what dost thou object","Against the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray?"],"id":10,"m":"I thank you both. But one of you is only flattering me, as the reason you have come makes clear: to accuse each other of high treason. Cousin Hereford, what charge do you bring against the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["First,—heaven be the record to my speech!—","In the devotion of a subject's love,","Tendering the precious safety of my prince,","And free from other misbegotten hate,","Come I appellant to this princely presence.","Now, Thomas Mowbray, do I turn to thee,","And mark my greeting well; for what I speak","My body shall make good upon this earth,","Or my divine soul answer it in heaven.","Thou art a traitor and a miscreant;","Too good to be so and too bad to live,","Since the more fair and crystal is the sky,","The uglier seem the clouds that in it fly.","Once more, the more to aggravate the note,","With a foul traitor's name stuff I thy throat;","And wish, so please my sovereign, ere I move,","What my tongue speaks, my right drawn sword may prove."],"id":11,"m":"First, and may heaven witness what I say, I come here to accuse him before my prince out of a subject's loving duty, out of care for my king's precious safety, and free of any other, unworthy hatred. Now, Thomas Mowbray, I turn to you. Note well how I greet you, because what I say my body will prove here on earth, or my immortal soul will answer for it in heaven. You are a traitor and a villain, too noble to be one and too wicked to live. The clearer and brighter the sky, the uglier the clouds that fly across it look. And to stress the charge, I'll stuff the name of foul traitor down your throat once more. With my king's permission, before I leave here I want my drawn sword to prove what my tongue says."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Let not my cold words here accuse my zeal:","'Tis not the trial of a woman's war,","The bitter clamour of two eager tongues,","Can arbitrate this cause betwixt us twain;","The blood is hot that must be cool'd for this.","Yet can I not of such tame patience boast","As to be hush'd and nought at all to say.","First, the fair reverence of your highness curbs me","From giving reins and spurs to my free speech;","Which else would post until it had return'd","These terms of treason doubled down his throat.","Setting aside his high blood's royalty,","And let him be no kinsman to my liege,","I do defy him, and I spit at him,","Call him a slanderous coward and a villain:","Which to maintain, I would allow him odds","And meet him, were I tied to run afoot","Even to the frozen ridges of the Alps,","Or any other ground inhabitable,","Wherever Englishman durst set his foot.","Meantime let this defend my loyalty:","By all my hopes, most falsely doth he lie."],"id":12,"m":"Don't judge my loyalty by how coolly I speak. A shouting match like two women quarrelling, two bitter, eager tongues, can't settle this matter between us. Hot blood must be spilled and cooled to do that. But I can't boast of such tame patience as to keep quiet and say nothing at all. First, my respect for your Highness holds me back from giving my speech free rein; otherwise it would gallop on until it had driven these charges of treason back down his throat twice over. Setting aside his royal blood, and pretending he is no relation of my king, I defy him and spit at him. I call him a slandering coward and a villain. To prove it I would give him the advantage and meet him even if I had to run on foot to the frozen ridges of the Alps, or any other place where no one can live, anywhere an Englishman ever dared to set foot. Meanwhile, let this defend my loyalty: by all my hopes, he lies completely."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Pale trembling coward, there I throw my gage,","Disclaiming here the kindred of the king;","And lay aside my high blood's royalty,","Which fear, not reverence, makes thee to except:","If guilty dread have left thee so much strength","As to take up mine honour's pawn, then stoop:","By that, and all the rites of knighthood else,","Will I make good against thee, arm to arm,","What I have spoke or thou canst worst devise."],"id":13,"m":"Pale, trembling coward, there I throw down my glove. I give up any claim to be the king's relative and put aside my royal blood, which you use as an excuse out of fear, not respect. If guilty fear has left you strength enough to pick up the pledge of my honour, then bend down. By that glove, and by all the other rituals of knighthood, I will prove against you in single combat what I have said, or anything worse you can think up."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["I take it up; and by that sword I swear","Which gently laid my knighthood on my shoulder,","I'll answer thee in any fair degree,","Or chivalrous design of knightly trial:","And when I mount, alive may I not light","If I be traitor or unjustly fight!"],"id":14,"m":"I pick it up. And I swear by the sword that gently laid knighthood on my shoulder that I will answer you in any fair way or any form of knightly combat. And when I mount my horse, may I not get off it alive if I am a traitor or fight unjustly!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What doth our cousin lay to Mowbray's charge?","It must be great that can inherit us","So much as of a thought of ill in him."],"id":15,"m":"What does my cousin accuse Mowbray of? It must be something great to make me even suspect him of anything bad."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Look, what I speak, my life shall prove it true;","That Mowbray hath receiv'd eight thousand nobles","In name of lendings for your highness' soldiers,","The which he hath detain'd for lewd employments,","Like a false traitor and injurious villain.","Besides, I say and will in battle prove,","Or here, or elsewhere to the furthest verge","That ever was survey'd by English eye,","That all the treasons for these eighteen years","Complotted and contrived in this land,","Fetch from false Mowbray their first head and spring.","Further I say, and further will maintain","Upon his bad life to make all this good,","That he did plot the Duke of Gloucester's death,","Suggest his soon-believing adversaries,","And consequently, like a traitor coward,","Sluic'd out his innocent soul through streams of blood:","Which blood, like sacrificing Abel's, cries,","Even from the tongueless caverns of the earth,","To me for justice and rough chastisement;","And, by the glorious worth of my descent,","This arm shall do it, or this life be spent."],"id":16,"m":"Listen: what I say, my life will prove true. Mowbray has received eight thousand gold coins, supposedly as pay for your Highness's soldiers, and has kept them for wicked purposes, like a false traitor and harmful villain. Besides that, I say, and will prove in battle here or anywhere else as far as any English eye has ever seen, that all the treasons plotted and invented in this country in the last eighteen years started with false Mowbray. And I say further, and will prove it on his wicked life, that he plotted the Duke of Gloucester's death, stirred up the Duke's too-trusting enemies, and so, like a traitor and coward, let out his innocent soul in streams of blood. That blood, like the blood of Abel the sacrificer, cries out to me from the silent caves of the earth for justice and harsh punishment. And by my glorious ancestry, this arm will deliver it, or I will die trying."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["How high a pitch his resolution soars!","Thomas of Norfolk, what say'st thou to this?"],"id":17,"m":"How high his determination flies! Thomas of Norfolk, what do you say to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["O! let my sovereign turn away his face","And bid his ears a little while be deaf,","Till I have told this slander of his blood","How God and good men hate so foul a liar."],"id":18,"m":"Oh, let my king turn his face away and close his ears for a moment, until I have told this disgrace to his family how much God and good men hate such a foul liar."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Mowbray, impartial are our eyes and ears:","Were he my brother, nay, my kingdom's heir,—","As he is but my father's brother's son,—","Now, by my sceptre's awe I make a vow,","Such neighbour nearness to our sacred blood","Should nothing privilege him nor partialize","The unstooping firmness of my upright soul.","He is our subject, Mowbray; so art thou:","Free speech and fearless I to thee allow."],"id":19,"m":"Mowbray, my eyes and ears are fair to both sides. If he were my brother, or even heir to my kingdom, and he is only my uncle's son, I swear by my royal sceptre that such closeness to my sacred blood would not give him any privilege or bend my firm, upright judgment. He is my subject, Mowbray, and so are you. I allow you to speak freely and without fear."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Then, Bolingbroke, as low as to thy heart,","Through the false passage of thy throat, thou liest.","Three parts of that receipt I had for Calais","Disburs'd I duly to his highness' soldiers;","The other part reserv'd I by consent,","For that my sovereign liege was in my debt","Upon remainder of a dear account,","Since last I went to France to fetch his queen.","Now swallow down that lie. For Gloucester's death,","I slew him not; but to my own disgrace","Neglected my sworn duty in that case.","For you, my noble Lord of Lancaster,","The honourable father to my foe,","Once did I lay an ambush for your life,","A trespass that doth vex my grieved soul;","But ere I last receiv'd the sacrament","I did confess it, and exactly begg'd","Your Grace's pardon; and I hope I had it.","This is my fault: as for the rest appeal'd,","It issues from the rancour of a villain,","A recreant and most degenerate traitor;","Which in myself I boldly will defend,","And interchangeably hurl down my gage","Upon this overweening traitor's foot,","To prove myself a loyal gentleman","Even in the best blood chamber'd in his bosom.","In haste whereof, most heartily I pray","Your highness to assign our trial day."],"id":20,"m":"Then, Bolingbroke, you lie, right down your lying throat to your heart. Of the money I received for Calais, I duly paid three quarters to his Highness's soldiers. The rest I kept back with the King's agreement, because he owed me money left over from a large debt from when I last went to France to bring back his queen. So swallow that lie. As for Gloucester's death, I did not kill him; but to my shame I failed in my sworn duty in that matter. As for you, my noble Lord of Lancaster, honourable father of my enemy: I did once set an ambush to kill you, a crime that troubles my grieving soul. But before I last took communion I confessed it and begged your Grace's pardon, and I hope I received it. That is my fault. The rest of the charges come from the hatred of a villain, a coward and a most degenerate traitor. I will boldly defend myself against them, and in return I throw my glove down at this arrogant traitor's foot, to prove myself a loyal gentleman by spilling the best blood in his heart. To hurry that on, I beg your Highness with all my heart to set the day of our combat."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Wrath-kindled gentlemen, be rul'd by me;","Let's purge this choler without letting blood:","This we prescribe, though no physician;","Deep malice makes too deep incision:","Forget, forgive; conclude and be agreed,","Our doctors say this is no month to bleed.","Good uncle, let this end where it begun;","We'll calm the Duke of Norfolk, you your son."],"id":21,"m":"You angry gentlemen, do as I say. Let's get rid of this rage without shedding blood. This is my prescription, though I am no doctor: deep hatred cuts too deep. Forget, forgive, end it and agree; our doctors say this is not the month for bleeding. Good uncle, let this end where it began. I'll calm the Duke of Norfolk, you calm your son."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["To be a make-peace shall become my age:","Throw down, my son, the Duke of Norfolk's gage."],"id":22,"m":"Being a peacemaker suits my age. My son, throw down the Duke of Norfolk's glove."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And, Norfolk, throw down his."],"id":23,"m":"And, Norfolk, throw down his."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["When, Harry, when?","Obedience bids I should not bid again."],"id":24,"m":"Well, Harry, well? Obedience means I shouldn't have to ask you twice."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Norfolk, throw down; we bid;","There is no boot."],"id":25,"m":"Norfolk, throw it down. I order it. There's no use resisting."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Myself I throw, dread sovereign, at thy foot.","My life thou shalt command, but not my shame:","The one my duty owes; but my fair name,—","Despite of death, that lives upon my grave,—","To dark dishonour's use thou shalt not have.","I am disgrac'd, impeach'd, and baffled here;","Pierc'd to the soul with slander's venom'd spear,","The which no balm can cure but his heart-blood","Which breath'd this poison."],"id":26,"m":"I throw myself at your feet, my feared king. You may command my life, but not my shame. My life I owe to you as my duty; but my good name, which will live on my grave in spite of death, you will not have for dark dishonour. I am disgraced, accused and humiliated here, stabbed to the soul with the poisoned spear of slander. No medicine can cure it except the blood from the heart of the man who breathed out this poison."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Rage must be withstood:","Give me his gage: lions make leopards tame."],"id":27,"m":"Your anger must be held back. Give me his glove. Lions tame leopards."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Yea, but not change his spots: take but my shame,","And I resign my gage. My dear dear lord,","The purest treasure mortal times afford","Is spotless reputation; that away,","Men are but gilded loam or painted clay.","A jewel in a ten-times barr'd-up chest","Is a bold spirit in a loyal breast.","Mine honour is my life; both grow in one;","Take honour from me, and my life is done:","Then, dear my liege, mine honour let me try;","In that I live, and for that will I die."],"id":28,"m":"Yes, but they don't change their spots. Take away my shame and I'll give up the glove. My dear, dear lord, the purest treasure this life offers is a spotless reputation. Without it, men are just gilded mud or painted clay. A bold spirit in a loyal heart is a jewel in a chest locked ten times over. My honour is my life; the two grow together as one. Take my honour away and my life is over. So, my dear lord, let me put my honour to the test. I live for it, and I will die for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Cousin, throw down your gage: do you begin."],"id":29,"m":"Cousin, throw down your glove. You start."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["O! God defend my soul from such deep sin.","Shall I seem crest-fall'n in my father's sight,","Or with pale beggar-fear impeach my height","Before this outdar'd dastard? Ere my tongue","Shall wound my honour with such feeble wrong","Or sound so base a parle, my teeth shall tear","The slavish motive of recanting fear,","And spit it bleeding in his high disgrace,","Where shame doth harbour, even in Mowbray's face."],"id":30,"m":"Oh, God keep my soul from such a terrible sin! Should I look defeated in my father's sight, or lower my rank with cowardly fear in front of this beaten coward? Before my tongue wounds my honour with such weakness or calls such a shameful truce, my teeth will tear out the cowardly tongue that would take it back and spit it, bleeding, at the place where shame lives: in Mowbray's face."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit GAUNT","id":31},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["We were not born to sue, but to command:","Which since we cannot do to make you friends,","Be ready, as your lives shall answer it,","At Coventry, upon Saint Lambert's day:","There shall your swords and lances arbitrate","The swelling difference of your settled hate:","Since we can not atone you, we shall see","Justice design the victor's chivalry.","Lord Marshal, command our officers-at-arms","Be ready to direct these home alarms."],"id":32,"m":"I was not born to plead, but to command. Since I can't make you friends, be ready, on pain of death, at Coventry on St Lambert's Day. There your swords and lances will settle the swelling difference of your fixed hatred. Since I cannot reconcile you, we'll see justice declare the winner by knightly combat. Lord Marshal, order my heralds to be ready to manage this quarrel at home."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":33},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A room in the DUKE OF LANCASTER'S palace.","id":34},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GAUNT and DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","id":35},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Alas, the part I had in Woodstock's blood","Doth more solicit me than your exclaims,","To stir against the butchers of his life.","But since correction lieth in those hands","Which made the fault that we cannot correct,","Put we our quarrel to the will of heaven;","Who, when they see the hours ripe on earth,","Will rain hot vengeance on offenders' heads."],"id":36,"m":"Alas, being Woodstock's brother moves me more than your cries do to act against the butchers who killed him. But since the power to punish lies in the hands of the very man who committed the crime, which we cannot punish, let us leave our quarrel to the will of heaven. When heaven sees the time is ripe on earth, it will rain hot vengeance on the heads of the guilty."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","lines":["Finds brotherhood in thee no sharper spur?","Hath love in thy old blood no living fire?","Edward's seven sons, whereof thyself art one,","Were as seven vials of his sacred blood,","Or seven fair branches springing from one root:","Some of those seven are dried by nature's course,","Some of those branches by the Destinies cut;","But Thomas, my dear lord, my life, my Gloucester,","One vial full of Edward's sacred blood,","One flourishing branch of his most royal root,","Is crack'd, and all the precious liquor spilt;","Is hack'd down, and his summer leaves all vaded,","By envy's hand and murder's bloody axe.","Ah, Gaunt! his blood was thine: that bed, that womb,","That metal, that self-mould, that fashion'd thee,","Made him a man; and though thou liv'st and breath'st,","Yet art thou slain in him: thou dost consent","In some large measure to thy father's death","In that thou seest thy wretched brother die,","Who was the model of thy father's life.","Call it not patience, Gaunt; it is despair:","In suffering thus thy brother to be slaughter'd,","Thou showest the naked pathway to thy life,","Teaching stern murder how to butcher thee:","That which in mean men we entitle patience","Is pale cold cowardice in noble breasts.","What shall I say? To safeguard thine own life,","The best way is to venge my Gloucester's death."],"id":37,"m":"Doesn't being his brother spur you on more sharply than that? Is there no living fire of love in your old blood? Edward's seven sons, and you are one of them, were like seven bottles of his sacred blood, or seven fine branches growing from one root. Some of the seven have dried up naturally; some of the branches have been cut by fate. But Thomas, my dear husband, my life, my Gloucester, one bottle full of Edward's sacred blood, one flourishing branch of his royal root, has been cracked and all its precious liquid spilled, has been hacked down and all his summer leaves faded, by the hand of malice and the bloody axe of murder. Ah, Gaunt, his blood was yours! The same bed, the same womb, the same metal and the same mould that made you made him a man. Though you live and breathe, you are killed in him. You partly agree to your own father's death by watching your poor brother die, who was the image of your father's life. Don't call it patience, Gaunt; it is despair. By letting your brother be slaughtered like this, you show the open road to your own life and teach cruel murder how to butcher you. What we call patience in common men is pale, cold cowardice in noble ones. What more can I say? The best way to protect your own life is to avenge my Gloucester's death."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["God's is the quarrel; for God's substitute,","His deputy anointed in his sight,","Hath caus'd his death; the which if wrongfully,","Let heaven revenge, for I may never lift","An angry arm against his minister."],"id":38,"m":"This quarrel belongs to God. God's representative, his deputy anointed in his sight, caused Gloucester's death. If it was wrong, let heaven take revenge, because I can never raise an angry arm against God's minister."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","lines":["Where then, alas! may I complain myself?"],"id":39,"m":"Then who, alas, can I complain to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["To God, the widow's champion and defence."],"id":40,"m":"To God, the protector and defender of widows."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","lines":["Why then, I will. Farewell, old Gaunt.","Thou go'st to Coventry, there to behold","Our cousin Hereford and fell Mowbray fight:","O! sit my husband's wrongs on Hereford's spear,","That it may enter butcher Mowbray's breast.","Or, if misfortune miss the first career,","Be Mowbray's sins so heavy in his bosom","That they may break his foaming courser's back,","And throw the rider headlong in the lists,","A caitiff recreant to my cousin Hereford!","Farewell, old Gaunt: thy sometimes brother's wife","With her companion, Grief, must end her life."],"id":41,"m":"Then I will. Goodbye, old Gaunt. You are going to Coventry to watch our cousin Hereford and cruel Mowbray fight. Oh, may my husband's wrongs ride on Hereford's spear, so that it goes into butcher Mowbray's heart! Or if bad luck makes him miss at the first charge, may Mowbray's sins be so heavy in his heart that they break his foaming horse's back and throw the rider headlong into the arena, a cowardly wretch at my cousin Hereford's mercy! Goodbye, old Gaunt. Your dead brother's wife must end her life with her only companion, grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Sister, farewell; I must to Coventry.","As much good stay with thee as go with me!"],"id":42,"m":"Sister, goodbye. I must go to Coventry. May as much good stay with you as goes with me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","lines":["Yet one word more. Grief boundeth where it falls,","Not with the empty hollowness, but weight:","I take my leave before I have begun,","For sorrow ends not when it seemeth done.","Commend me to thy brother, Edmund York.","Lo! this is all: nay, yet depart not so;","Though this be all, do not so quickly go;","I shall remember more. Bid him—ah, what?—","With all good speed at Plashy visit me.","Alack! and what shall good old York there see","But empty lodgings and unfurnish'd walls,","Unpeopled offices, untrodden stones?","And what hear there for welcome but my groans?","Therefore commend me; let him not come there,","To seek out sorrow that dwells every where.","Desolate, desolate, will I hence and die:","The last leave of thee takes my weeping eye."],"id":43,"m":"One more word. Grief bounces back where it falls, not because it is hollow but because it is heavy. I'm saying goodbye before I've even begun, because sorrow doesn't end when it seems to be over. Give my regards to your brother, Edmund of York. There, that's all. No, don't leave yet; even if that's all, don't go so quickly. I'll remember more. Tell him... ah, what? To visit me at Plashy as soon as he can. Alas, and what will good old York see there but empty rooms and bare walls, deserted servants' quarters and stones nobody walks on? And what welcome will he hear there but my groans? So give him my regards, but don't let him come there looking for sorrow; it is everywhere. Desolate, desolate, I will go away and die. My weeping eye takes its last look at you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":44},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"Open Space, near Coventry. Lists set out, and a Throne. Heralds, &c., attending.","id":45},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Lord Marshal and AUMERLE","id":46},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["My Lord Aumerle, is Harry Hereford arm'd?"],"id":47,"m":"My Lord Aumerle, is Harry Hereford armed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Yea, at all points; and longs to enter in."],"id":48,"m":"Yes, completely, and he is eager to come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["The Duke of Norfolk, sprightfully and bold,","Stays but the summons of the appelant's trumpet."],"id":49,"m":"The Duke of Norfolk, full of spirit and courage, is only waiting for the challenger's trumpet to call him."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Why then, the champions are prepar'd, and stay","For nothing but his Majesty's approach."],"id":50,"m":"Then both champions are ready, and are waiting for nothing but his Majesty's arrival."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD, who takes his seat on his Throne; GAUNT, BUSHY, BAGOT, GREEN, and Others, who take their places. A trumpet is sounded, and answered by another trumpet within. Then enter MOWBRAY, in armour, defendant, preceeded by a Herald","id":51},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Marshal, demand of yonder champion","The cause of his arrival here in arms:","Ask him his name, and orderly proceed","To swear him in the justice of his cause."],"id":52,"m":"Marshal, ask that champion over there why he has come here armed. Ask him his name, and then, in due order, make him swear that his cause is just."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["In God's name and the king's, say who thou art,","And why thou comest thus knightly clad in arms,","Against what man thou comest, and what thy quarrel.","Speak truly, on thy knighthood and thy oath;","As so defend thee heaven and thy valour!"],"id":53,"m":"In the name of God and the King, say who you are, why you come here dressed in armour like a knight, whom you have come to fight and what your quarrel is. Speak truthfully, on your knighthood and your oath, and may heaven and your courage defend you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["My name is Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk,","Who hither come engaged by my oath,—","Which God defend a knight should violate!—","Both to defend my loyalty and truth","To God, my King, and my succeeding issue,","Against the Duke of Hereford that appeals me;","And, by the grace of God and this mine arm,","To prove him, in defending of myself,","A traitor to my God, my King, and me:","And as I truly fight, defend me heaven!"],"id":54,"m":"My name is Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk. I come here bound by my oath, which God forbid a knight should break, to defend my loyalty and faithfulness to God, my King and my descendants against the Duke of Hereford, who accuses me. And by the grace of God and this arm of mine, I come to prove, by defending myself, that he is a traitor to my God, my King and me. And as I fight honestly, may heaven defend me!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He takes his seat","id":55},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpet sounds. Enter BOLINGBROKE, appellant, in armour, preceeded by a Herald","id":56},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Marshal, ask yonder knight in arms,","Both who he is and why he cometh hither","Thus plated in habiliments of war;","And formally, according to our law,","Depose him in the justice of his cause."],"id":57,"m":"Marshal, ask that armed knight over there who he is and why he comes here dressed in armour for war. And formally, as our law requires, take his oath that his cause is just."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["What is thy name? and wherefore com'st thou hither","Before King Richard in his royal lists?","Against whom comest thou? and what's thy quarrel?","Speak like a true knight, so defend thee heaven!"],"id":58,"m":"What is your name? Why have you come here, before King Richard in his royal arena? Whom have you come to fight, and what is your quarrel? Speak like a true knight, and may heaven defend you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Harry of Hereford, Lancaster, and Derby,","Am I; who ready here do stand in arms,","To prove by God's grace and my body's valour,","In lists, on Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk,","That he's a traitor foul and dangerous,","To God of heaven, King Richard, and to me:","And as I truly fight, defend me heaven!"],"id":59,"m":"I am Harry of Hereford, Lancaster and Derby. I stand here armed and ready to prove in combat, by God's grace and my body's courage, against Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk, that he is a foul and dangerous traitor to God in heaven, to King Richard and to me. And as I fight honestly, may heaven defend me!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["On pain of death, no person be so bold","Or daring-hardy as to touch the lists,","Except the Marshal and such officers","Appointed to direct these fair designs."],"id":60,"m":"On pain of death, let no one be so bold or reckless as to touch the barriers of the arena, except the Marshal and the officers appointed to manage this honourable contest."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Lord Marshal, let me kiss my sovereign's hand,","And bow my knee before his Majesty:","For Mowbray and myself are like two men","That vow a long and weary pilgrimage;","Then let us take a ceremonious leave","And loving farewell of our several friends."],"id":61,"m":"Lord Marshal, let me kiss my king's hand and kneel before his Majesty. Mowbray and I are like two men who have vowed to go on a long and weary pilgrimage, so let us take a formal leave and say a loving goodbye to our friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["The appellant in all duty greets your highness,","And craves to kiss your hand and take his leave."],"id":62,"m":"The challenger respectfully greets your Highness and asks to kiss your hand and say goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["[Descends from his throne] We will descend and fold him in our arms.","Cousin of Hereford, as thy cause is right,","So be thy fortune in this royal fight!","Farewell, my blood; which if to-day thou shed,","Lament we may, but not revenge thee dead."],"id":63,"m":"I will come down and take him in my arms. Cousin Hereford, may your fortune in this royal fight be as good as your cause is just! Goodbye, my own blood. If you shed it today, I may mourn you, but I may not avenge your death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["O! let no noble eye profane a tear","For me, if I be gor'd with Mowbray's spear.","As confident as is the falcon's flight","Against a bird, do I with Mowbray fight.","My loving lord, I take my leave of you;","Of you, my noble cousin, Lord Aumerle;","Not sick, although I have to do with death,","But lusty, young, and cheerly drawing breath.","Lo! as at English feasts, so I regreet","The daintiest last, to make the end most sweet:","O thou, the earthly author of my blood,","Whose youthful spirit, in me regenerate,","Doth with a twofold vigour lift me up","To reach at victory above my head,","Add proof unto mine armour with thy prayers,","And with thy blessings steel my lance's point,","That it may enter Mowbray's waxen coat,","And furbish new the name of John a Gaunt,","Even in the lusty haviour of his son."],"id":64,"m":"Oh, let no noble eye waste a tear on me if I am pierced by Mowbray's spear. I fight Mowbray as confidently as a falcon flies at a bird. My loving lord, I say goodbye to you; and to you, my noble cousin, Lord Aumerle. I am not sick, though I am dealing with death, but strong, young and breathing cheerfully. Look, as at English feasts, I greet the sweetest dish last, to make the end the best. Oh, you, my father on earth, whose young spirit, reborn in me, lifts me up with double strength to reach for a victory above my head: strengthen my armour with your prayers, and harden my lance's point with your blessings, so that it pierces Mowbray's armour as if it were wax and polishes up the name of John of Gaunt again through the vigorous deeds of his son."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["God in thy good cause make thee prosperous!","Be swift like lightning in the execution;","And let thy blows, doubly redoubled,","Fall like amazing thunder on the casque","Of thy adverse pernicious enemy:","Rouse up thy youthful blood, be valiant, and live."],"id":65,"m":"May God make you succeed in your good cause! Be as quick as lightning in the fight, and let your blows, doubled and doubled again, fall like stunning thunder on the helmet of your destructive enemy. Stir up your young blood, be brave, and live."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Mine innocency and Saint George to thrive!"],"id":66,"m":"May my innocence and Saint George help me win!"},{"t":"sd","text":"He takes his seat","id":67},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["[Rising] However God or fortune cast my lot,","There lives or dies, true to King Richard's throne,","A loyal, just, and upright gentleman.","Never did captive with a freer heart","Cast off his chains of bondage and embrace","His golden uncontroll'd enfranchisement,","More than my dancing soul doth celebrate","This feast of battle with mine adversary.","Most mighty liege, and my companion peers,","Take from my mouth the wish of happy years.","As gentle and as jocund as to jest","Go I to fight: truth hath a quiet breast."],"id":68,"m":"However God or fortune decides my fate, there lives or dies here a loyal, just and honest gentleman, faithful to King Richard's throne. No prisoner ever threw off his chains and welcomed his golden, unlimited freedom with a happier heart than my dancing soul feels as it celebrates this feast of battle with my enemy. Most mighty lord, and my fellow noblemen, accept my wish that you may have many happy years. I go to fight as calmly and cheerfully as if I were going to a game. A clear conscience has a peaceful heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Farewell, my lord: securely I espy","Virtue with valour couched in thine eye.","Order the trial, Marshal, and begin."],"id":69,"m":"Goodbye, my lord. I can see clearly that goodness and courage lie together in your eyes. Organise the combat, Marshal, and begin."},{"t":"sd","text":"The KING and the Lords return to their seats","id":70},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["Harry of Hereford, Lancaster, and Derby,","Receive thy lance; and God defend the right!"],"id":71,"m":"Harry of Hereford, Lancaster and Derby, take your lance, and may God defend the right!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["[Rising] Strong as a tower in hope, I cry 'amen'."],"id":72,"m":"Strong as a tower in my hope, I say 'Amen'."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["[To an officer] Go bear this lance to Thomas,","Duke of Norfolk."],"id":73,"m":"Take this lance to Thomas, Duke of Norfolk."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST HERALD","lines":["Harry of Hereford, Lancaster, and Derby,","Stands here for God, his sovereign, and himself,","On pain to be found false and recreant,","To prove the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray,","A traitor to his God, his King, and him;","And dares him to set forward to the fight."],"id":74,"m":"Harry of Hereford, Lancaster and Derby stands here for God, his king and himself, ready to prove, on pain of being found a liar and a coward, that the Duke of Norfolk, Thomas Mowbray, is a traitor to his God, his King and himself. He dares him to come forward and fight."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND HERALD","lines":["Here standeth Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk,","On pain to be found false and recreant,","Both to defend himself, and to approve","Henry of Hereford, Lancaster, and Derby,","To God, his sovereign, and to him disloyal;","Courageously and with a free desire,","Attending but the signal to begin."],"id":75,"m":"Here stands Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk, ready, on pain of being found a liar and a coward, to defend himself and to prove that Henry of Hereford, Lancaster and Derby is disloyal to God, his king and him. He comes with courage and eagerness, waiting only for the signal to begin."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["Sound trumpets; and set forward, combatants."],"id":76,"m":"Sound the trumpets, and charge, combatants."},{"t":"sd","text":"A charge sounded","id":77},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","cont":1,"lines":["Stay, the King hath thrown his warder down."],"id":78,"m":"Stop! The King has thrown down his staff."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Let them lay by their helmets and their spears,","And both return back to their chairs again:","Withdraw with us; and let the trumpets sound","While we return these dukes what we decree."],"id":79,"m":"Let them put aside their helmets and spears, and both go back to their seats. Come away with me, and let the trumpets sound while I return to tell these dukes what I have decided."},{"t":"sd","text":"A long flourish","id":80},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["[To the Combatants] Draw near,","And list what with our council we have done.","For that our kingdom's earth should not be soil'd","With that dear blood which it hath fostered;","And for our eyes do hate the dire aspect","Of civil wounds plough'd up with neighbours' swords;","And for we think the eagle-winged pride","Of sky-aspiring and ambitious thoughts,","With rival-hating envy, set on you","To wake our peace, which in our country's cradle","Draws the sweet infant breath of gentle sleep;","Which so rous'd up with boist'rous untun'd drums,","With harsh-resounding trumpets' dreadful bray,","And grating shock of wrathful iron arms,","Might from our quiet confines fright fair peace","And make us wade even in our kindred's blood:","Therefore we banish you our territories:","You, cousin Hereford, upon pain of life,","Till twice five summers have enrich'd our fields","Shall not regreet our fair dominions,","But tread the stranger paths of banishment."],"id":81,"m":"Come closer, and listen to what my council and I have decided. Because the soil of my kingdom should not be stained with the precious blood it has brought up, and because my eyes hate the terrible sight of wounds of civil war, ploughed open by neighbours' swords, and because I think that the proud, high-flying ambition that aims at the sky, together with jealous rivalry, set you on to wake up peace, which lies in our country's cradle breathing the sweet breath of a sleeping baby; and that peace, woken by loud, tuneless drums, the harsh, dreadful blare of trumpets and the grating clash of angry weapons, might be frightened out of our quiet land and make us wade in the blood of our own relatives: for these reasons I banish you from my lands. You, cousin Hereford, on pain of death, shall not see my fair kingdom again until ten summers have ripened our fields, but shall walk the foreign roads of exile."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Your will be done. This must my comfort be,","That sun that warms you here shall shine on me;","And those his golden beams to you here lent","Shall point on me and gild my banishment."],"id":82,"m":"Your will be done. My comfort must be this: the same sun that warms you here will shine on me, and the golden beams it lends you here will fall on me and turn my exile to gold."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Norfolk, for thee remains a heavier doom,","Which I with some unwillingness pronounce:","The sly slow hours shall not determinate","The dateless limit of thy dear exile;","The hopeless word of 'never to return'","Breathe I against thee, upon pain of life."],"id":83,"m":"Norfolk, a heavier sentence remains for you, which I announce with some reluctance. The slow, creeping hours will not bring to an end your exile, which has no end date. I pronounce against you, on pain of death, the hopeless words 'never to return'."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["A heavy sentence, my most sovereign liege,","And all unlook'd for from your highness' mouth:","A dearer merit, not so deep a maim","As to be cast forth in the common air,","Have I deserved at your highness' hands.","The language I have learn'd these forty years,","My native English, now I must forgo;","And now my tongue's use is to me no more","Than an unstringed viol or a harp,","Or like a cunning instrument cas'd up","Or, being open, put into his hands","That knows no touch to tune the harmony:","Within my mouth you have engaol'd my tongue,","Doubly portcullis'd with my teeth and lips;","And dull, unfeeling, barren ignorance","Is made my gaoler to attend on me.","I am too old to fawn upon a nurse,","Too far in years to be a pupil now:","What is thy sentence, then, but speechless death,","Which robs my tongue from breathing native breath?"],"id":84,"m":"A heavy sentence, my most royal lord, and completely unexpected from your Highness's mouth. I have deserved a better reward from your Highness than such a deep wound as to be thrown out into the open world. Now I must give up the language I have learned for forty years, my native English. My tongue is now no more use to me than a viol or harp without strings, or a fine instrument shut in its case, or, if it is opened, put into the hands of someone who has no idea how to play it. You have imprisoned my tongue inside my mouth, behind the double gate of my teeth and lips, and dull, unfeeling, empty ignorance has been made my jailer. I am too old to start learning from a nurse again, too old to be a schoolboy now. So what is your sentence but a speechless death, since it stops my tongue speaking its native language?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["It boots thee not to be compassionate:","After our sentence plaining comes too late."],"id":85,"m":"It does you no good to feel sorry for yourself. Complaining comes too late after my sentence."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["Then thus I turn me from my country's light,","To dwell in solemn shades of endless night."],"id":86,"m":"Then I turn away from my country's light, to live in the solemn shadows of endless night."},{"t":"sd","text":"Retiring","id":87},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Return again, and take an oath with thee.","Lay on our royal sword your banish'd hands;","Swear by the duty that you owe to God,—","Our part therein we banish with yourselves—","To keep the oath that we administer:","You never shall, so help you truth and God!—","Embrace each other's love in banishment;","Nor never look upon each other's face;","Nor never write, regreet, nor reconcile","This louring tempest of your home-bred hate;","Nor never by advised purpose meet","To plot, contrive, or complot any ill","'Gainst us, our state, our subjects, or our land."],"id":88,"m":"Come back, and take an oath with you. Lay your banished hands on my royal sword. Swear by the duty you owe to God (the duty you owe me goes into exile with you) that you will keep the oath I now give you: that you will never, so help you truth and God, become friends in exile, nor ever look at each other's faces, nor ever write, greet each other or settle this dark storm of homegrown hatred, nor ever meet on purpose to plot, plan or scheme any harm against me, my government, my subjects or my country."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I swear."],"id":89,"m":"I swear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["And I, to keep all this."],"id":90,"m":"And I swear to keep all this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Norfolk, so far as to mine enemy:—","By this time, had the king permitted us,","One of our souls had wand'red in the air,","Banish'd this frail sepulchre of our flesh,","As now our flesh is banish'd from this land:","Confess thy treasons ere thou fly the realm;","Since thou hast far to go, bear not along","The clogging burden of a guilty soul."],"id":91,"m":"Norfolk, I'll say this much to you as my enemy. By now, if the king had let us, one of our souls would be wandering in the air, banished from the frail tomb of its body, just as our bodies are now banished from this country. Confess your treasons before you leave the kingdom. Since you have a long way to go, don't carry the dragging weight of a guilty soul with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MOWBRAY","lines":["No, Bolingbroke: if ever I were traitor,","My name be blotted from the book of life,","And I from heaven banish'd as from hence!","But what thou art, God, thou, and I, do know;","And all too soon, I fear, the king shall rue.","Farewell, my liege. Now no way can I stray;","Save back to England, all the world's my way."],"id":92,"m":"No, Bolingbroke. If I was ever a traitor, may my name be wiped out of the book of life, and may I be banished from heaven as I am from here! But God, you and I know what you are, and I fear the king will regret it all too soon. Goodbye, my lord. Now I can't lose my way: the whole world is my road, except the way back to England."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":93},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Uncle, even in the glasses of thine eyes","I see thy grieved heart: thy sad aspect","Hath from the number of his banish'd years","Pluck'd four away.— [To BOLINGBROKE] Six frozen winters spent,","Return with welcome home from banishment."],"id":94,"m":"Uncle, I can see your grieving heart in the mirrors of your eyes. Your sad face has taken four years away from his exile. Come home with a welcome from banishment when six frozen winters have passed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["How long a time lies in one little word!","Four lagging winters and four wanton springs","End in a word: such is the breath of kings."],"id":95,"m":"What a long time lies in one little word! Four slow winters and four playful springs ended in a word. That is the power of a king's breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["I thank my liege that in regard of me","He shortens four years of my son's exile;","But little vantage shall I reap thereby:","For, ere the six years that he hath to spend","Can change their moons and bring their times about,","My oil-dried lamp and time-bewasted light","Shall be extinct with age and endless night;","My inch of taper will be burnt and done,","And blindfold death not let me see my son."],"id":96,"m":"I thank my lord for shortening my son's exile by four years for my sake. But I won't get much benefit from it. Before the six years he has to spend abroad can change their moons and come round, my lamp, dried of oil, and my light, worn out by time, will be put out by age and endless night. My last inch of candle will burn down, and blindfolded death won't let me see my son."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Why, uncle, thou hast many years to live."],"id":97,"m":"Why, uncle, you have many years to live."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["But not a minute, king, that thou canst give:","Shorten my days thou canst with sullen sorrow,","And pluck nights from me, but not lend a morrow;","Thou can'st help time to furrow me with age,","But stop no wrinkle in his pilgrimage;","Thy word is current with him for my death,","But dead, thy kingdom cannot buy my breath."],"id":98,"m":"But not a minute, King, that you can give me. You can shorten my days with gloomy sorrow and steal nights from me, but you can't lend me one tomorrow. You can help time plough me with the lines of age, but you can't stop one wrinkle on its journey. Your word is good enough for time to bring my death; but once I'm dead, your whole kingdom cannot buy back my breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thy son is banish'd upon good advice,","Whereto thy tongue a party-verdict gave.","Why at our justice seem'st thou then to lower?"],"id":99,"m":"Your son was banished on good advice, and you yourself gave your vote for it. So why do you seem to frown at my justice?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Things sweet to taste prove in digestion sour.","You urg'd me as a judge; but I had rather","You would have bid me argue like a father.","O! had it been a stranger, not my child,","To smooth his fault I should have been more mild:","A partial slander sought I to avoid,","And in the sentence my own life destroy'd.","Alas! I look'd when some of you should say","I was too strict to make mine own away;","But you gave leave to my unwilling tongue","Against my will to do myself this wrong."],"id":100,"m":"Things that taste sweet turn sour when you digest them. You asked me to speak as a judge, but I would rather you had told me to argue like a father. Oh, if it had been a stranger and not my child, I would have been gentler and played down his fault. I tried to avoid being accused of favouring my son, and by my sentence I destroyed my own life. Alas, I kept waiting for one of you to say I was too strict in sending away my own son. But you allowed my unwilling tongue to do me this harm against my will."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Cousin, farewell; and, uncle, bid him so:","Six years we banish him, and he shall go."],"id":101,"m":"Cousin, goodbye; and, uncle, say goodbye to him. I banish him for six years, and he shall go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exit KING RICHARD and Train","id":102},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Cousin, farewell: what presence must not know,","From where you do remain let paper show."],"id":103,"m":"Cousin, goodbye. What we can't tell each other face to face, let your letters tell from wherever you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARSHAL","lines":["My lord, no leave take I; for I will ride,","As far as land will let me, by your side."],"id":104,"m":"My lord, I won't say goodbye yet, because I will ride beside you as far as the land goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["O! to what purpose dost thou hoard thy words,","That thou return'st no greeting to thy friends?"],"id":105,"m":"Oh, why are you saving up your words and not returning your friends' greetings?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I have too few to take my leave of you,","When the tongue's office should be prodigal","To breathe the abundant dolour of the heart."],"id":106,"m":"I have too few words to say goodbye to you, when my tongue should be pouring them out to express the overflowing grief of my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Thy grief is but thy absence for a time."],"id":107,"m":"Your grief is only that you'll be away for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Joy absent, grief is present for that time."],"id":108,"m":"With joy away, grief is here for all that while."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["What is six winters? They are quickly gone."],"id":109,"m":"What are six winters? They pass quickly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["To men in joy; but grief makes one hour ten."],"id":110,"m":"For happy men, yes. But grief turns one hour into ten."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Call it a travel that thou tak'st for pleasure."],"id":111,"m":"Think of it as a journey you are taking for pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My heart will sigh when I miscall it so,","Which finds it an enforced pilgrimage."],"id":112,"m":"My heart will sigh if I call it that, because it feels like a forced pilgrimage."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["The sullen passage of thy weary steps","Esteem as foil wherein thou art to set","The precious jewel of thy home return."],"id":113,"m":"Think of the gloomy journey of your weary steps as the setting in which you will place the precious jewel of your return home."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Nay, rather, every tedious stride I make","Will but remember me what a deal of world","I wander from the jewels that I love.","Must I not serve a long apprenticehood","To foreign passages, and in the end,","Having my freedom, boast of nothing else","But that I was a journeyman to grief?"],"id":114,"m":"No. Every dull step I take will just remind me how far I am wandering from the jewels I love. Won't I have to serve a long apprenticeship in foreign travel, and in the end, when I am free, be able to boast of nothing except that I was a trained worker in grief?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["All places that the eye of heaven visits","Are to a wise man ports and happy havens.","Teach thy necessity to reason thus;","There is no virtue like necessity.","Think not the king did banish thee,","But thou the king. Woe doth the heavier sit,","Where it perceives it is but faintly borne.","Go, say I sent thee forth to purchase honour,","And not the King exil'd thee; or suppose","Devouring pestilence hangs in our air,","And thou art flying to a fresher clime.","Look, what thy soul holds dear, imagine it","To lie that way thou go'st, not whence thou com'st.","Suppose the singing birds musicians,","The grass whereon thou tread'st the presence strew'd,","The flowers fair ladies, and thy steps no more","Than a delightful measure or a dance;","For gnarling sorrow hath less power to bite","The man that mocks at it and sets it light."],"id":115,"m":"To a wise man, every place the sun shines on is a port and a happy harbour. Teach yourself in your hardship to think like this: there is no virtue like accepting what you must. Don't think the King banished you; think that you banished the King. Sorrow sits more heavily on someone who bears it weakly. Go, and say I sent you away to win honour, and the King didn't exile you. Or imagine a deadly plague hanging in our air, and you are escaping to a healthier country. Whatever your soul holds dear, imagine it lies in the direction you are going, not the place you came from. Imagine the singing birds are musicians, the grass you walk on is a royal hall strewn with rushes, the flowers are beautiful ladies and your steps are just a graceful dance. Snarling sorrow has less power to bite a man who laughs at it and takes it lightly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["O! who can hold a fire in his hand","By thinking on the frosty Caucasus?","Or cloy the hungry edge of appetite","By bare imagination of a feast?","Or wallow naked in December snow","By thinking on fantastic summer's heat?","O, no! the apprehension of the good","Gives but the greater feeling to the worse:","Fell sorrow's tooth doth never rankle more","Than when it bites, but lanceth not the sore."],"id":116,"m":"Oh, who can hold fire in his hand by thinking of the frosty Caucasus mountains? Or satisfy the sharp edge of hunger just by imagining a feast? Or roll naked in December snow by thinking of imaginary summer heat? Oh, no! Thinking about good things only makes you feel the bad ones more. Sorrow's cruel tooth never hurts more than when it bites without opening the wound to let it heal."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Come, come, my son, I'll bring thee on thy way.","Had I thy youth and cause, I would not stay."],"id":117,"m":"Come, come, my son, I'll set you on your way. If I had your youth and your reason to go, I wouldn't stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Then, England's ground, farewell; sweet soil, adieu;","My mother, and my nurse, that bears me yet!","Where'er I wander, boast of this I can,","Though banish'd, yet a true-born Englishman."],"id":118,"m":"Then, ground of England, goodbye. Sweet soil, farewell, my mother and my nurse, who still carries me! Wherever I wander, I can boast of this: though banished, I am still a true-born Englishman."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":119},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"IV","loc":"London. A Room in the King's Castle","id":120},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD, BAGOT, and GREEN, at one door; AUMERLE at another","id":121},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["We did observe. Cousin Aumerle,","How far brought you high Hereford on his way?"],"id":122,"m":"We noticed. Cousin Aumerle, how far did you go with high and mighty Hereford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["I brought high Hereford, if you call him so,","But to the next highway, and there I left him."],"id":123,"m":"I took high Hereford, if you call him that, only as far as the next main road, and left him there."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And say, what store of parting tears were shed?"],"id":124,"m":"And tell me, how many tears were shed at parting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Faith, none for me; except the north-east wind,","Which then blew bitterly against our faces,","Awak'd the sleeping rheum, and so by chance","Did grace our hollow parting with a tear."],"id":125,"m":"Honestly, none from me, except that the north-east wind, which was blowing bitterly in our faces, woke up my watery eyes, and so by chance decorated our insincere parting with a tear."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What said our cousin when you parted with him?"],"id":126,"m":"What did our cousin say when you left him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["'Farewell:'","And, for my heart disdained that my tongue","Should so profane the word, that taught me craft","To counterfeit oppression of such grief","That words seem'd buried in my sorrow's grave.","Marry, would the word 'farewell' have lengthen'd hours","And added years to his short banishment,","He should have had a volume of farewells;","But since it would not, he had none of me."],"id":127,"m":"'Farewell.' And since my heart refused to let my tongue insult that word by saying it, it taught me the trick of pretending to be so overcome with grief that my words seemed buried in the grave of my sorrow. Honestly, if the word 'farewell' could have lengthened the hours and added years to his short exile, I would have given him a whole book of farewells. But since it couldn't, he got none from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["He is our cousin, cousin; but 'tis doubt,","When time shall call him home from banishment,","Whether our kinsman come to see his friends.","Ourself, and Bushy, Bagot here and Green,","Observ'd his courtship to the common people,","How he did seem to dive into their hearts","With humble and familiar courtesy,","What reverence he did throw away on slaves,","Wooing poor craftsmen with the craft of smiles","And patient underbearing of his fortune,","As 'twere to banish their affects with him.","Off goes his bonnet to an oyster-wench;","A brace of draymen bid God speed him well,","And had the tribute of his supple knee,","With 'Thanks, my countrymen, my loving friends';","As were our England in reversion his,","And he our subjects' next degree in hope."],"id":128,"m":"He is our cousin, cousin. But when time calls him home from exile, it's doubtful whether our relative will come back to see his friends. I myself, and Bushy, Bagot and Green here, watched him courting the common people: how he seemed to dive into their hearts with humble, friendly politeness, how much respect he wasted on lowly people, winning over poor workmen with crafty smiles and with the patient way he bore his bad luck, as if to take their love into exile with him. Off comes his cap to an oyster-seller. A couple of cart drivers wished him God speed, and got a bow of his bending knee and 'Thank you, my countrymen, my loving friends', as if our England would be his after me, and he were next in line in my subjects' hopes."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Well, he is gone; and with him go these thoughts.","Now for the rebels which stand out in Ireland;","Expedient manage must be made, my liege,","Ere further leisure yield them further means","For their advantage and your highness' loss."],"id":129,"m":"Well, he's gone, and these thoughts can go with him. Now for the rebels who are holding out in Ireland. Quick action must be taken, my lord, before any more delay gives them more chances to gain advantage and cause your Highness loss."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["We will ourself in person to this war.","And, for our coffers, with too great a court","And liberal largess, are grown somewhat light,","We are enforc'd to farm our royal realm;","The revenue whereof shall furnish us","For our affairs in hand. If that come short,","Our substitutes at home shall have blank charters;","Whereto, when they shall know what men are rich,","They shall subscribe them for large sums of gold,","And send them after to supply our wants;","For we will make for Ireland presently."],"id":130,"m":"I will go to this war in person. And because my treasury has got rather light from keeping too large a court and giving generous gifts, I am forced to lease out my royal kingdom for rent. The income from it will pay for the business in hand. If that is not enough, my deputies at home shall have blank documents. When they find out which men are rich, they will fill in those men's names for large sums of gold and send the money after me to supply what I need. I am leaving for Ireland at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BUSHY","id":131},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["Bushy, what news?"],"id":132,"m":"Bushy, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Old John of Gaunt is grievous sick, my lord,","Suddenly taken, and hath sent poste-haste","To entreat your Majesty to visit him."],"id":133,"m":"Old John of Gaunt is seriously ill, my lord. It came on suddenly, and he has sent in great haste to ask your Majesty to visit him."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Where lies he?"],"id":134,"m":"Where is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["At Ely House."],"id":135,"m":"At Ely House."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Now put it, God, in his physician's mind","To help him to his grave immediately!","The lining of his coffers shall make coats","To deck our soldiers for these Irish wars.","Come, gentlemen, let's all go visit him:","Pray God we may make haste, and come too late!"],"id":136,"m":"Now, God, put it into his doctor's mind to help him into his grave at once! The lining of his money chests will make coats for our soldiers in these Irish wars. Come, gentlemen, let's all go and visit him. Pray God we hurry and arrive too late!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Amen."],"id":137,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":138},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"London. An Apartment in Ely House.","id":139},{"t":"sd","text":"GAUNT on a couch; the DUKE OF YORK and Others standing by him","id":140},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Will the King come, that I may breathe my last","In wholesome counsel to his unstaid youth?"],"id":141,"m":"Will the King come, so that I can spend my last breath giving good advice to his reckless youth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Vex not yourself, nor strive not with your breath;","For all in vain comes counsel to his ear."],"id":142,"m":"Don't upset yourself or strain your breath. Advice is wasted on his ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["O! but they say the tongues of dying men","Enforce attention like deep harmony:","Where words are scarce, they are seldom spent in vain,","For they breathe truth that breathe their words in pain.","He that no more must say is listen'd more","Than they whom youth and ease have taught to glose;","More are men's ends mark'd than their lives before:","The setting sun, and music at the close,","As the last taste of sweets, is sweetest last,","Writ in remembrance more than things long past:","Though Richard my life's counsel would not hear,","My death's sad tale may yet undeaf his ear."],"id":143,"m":"Oh, but they say the words of dying men command attention like solemn music. When words are few, they are rarely wasted, because those who speak in pain speak the truth. A man who will soon say nothing more is listened to more than those whom youth and comfort have taught to flatter. People notice how men end more than how they lived before. The setting sun, and music as it closes, like the last taste of something sweet, are sweetest at the end and stay in the memory longer than things long past. Though Richard would not listen to my advice while I lived, my sad words as I die may still unblock his ears."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["No; it is stopp'd with other flattering sounds,","As praises of his state: then there are fond,","Lascivious metres, to whose venom sound","The open ear of youth doth always listen:","Report of fashions in proud Italy,","Whose manners still our tardy apish nation","Limps after in base imitation.","Where doth the world thrust forth a vanity,—","So it be new there's no respect how vile,—","That is not quickly buzz'd into his ears?","Then all too late comes counsel to be heard,","Where will doth mutiny with wit's regard.","Direct not him whose way himself will choose:","'Tis breath thou lack'st, and that breath wilt thou lose."],"id":144,"m":"No. His ears are stuffed with other, flattering sounds, like praise of his greatness. Then there are silly, lustful poems, whose poisonous sound young ears are always open to, and reports of the fashions in proud Italy, whose manners our slow, copycat nation limps after in cheap imitation. Where in the world is there a new vanity, and if it's new, nobody cares how vile it is, that isn't quickly buzzed into his ears? Advice comes much too late to be heard when desire rebels against reason. Don't try to steer someone who insists on choosing his own way. You are short of breath, and you will lose what breath you have."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Methinks I am a prophet new inspir'd,","And thus expiring do foretell of him:","His rash fierce blaze of riot cannot last,","For violent fires soon burn out themselves;","Small showers last long, but sudden storms are short;","He tires betimes that spurs too fast betimes;","With eager feeding food doth choke the feeder:","Light vanity, insatiate cormorant,","Consuming means, soon preys upon itself.","This royal throne of kings, this scepter'd isle,","This earth of majesty, this seat of Mars,","This other Eden, demi-paradise,","This fortress built by Nature for herself","Against infection and the hand of war,","This happy breed of men, this little world,","This precious stone set in the silver sea,","Which serves it in the office of a wall,","Or as a moat defensive to a house,","Against the envy of less happier lands;","This blessed plot, this earth, this realm, this England,","This nurse, this teeming womb of royal kings,","Fear'd by their breed, and famous by their birth,","Renowned for their deeds as far from home,—","For Christian service and true chivalry,—","As is the sepulchre in stubborn Jewry","Of the world's ransom, blessed Mary's Son:","This land of such dear souls, this dear, dear land,","Dear for her reputation through the world,","Is now leas'd out,—I die pronouncing it,—","Like to a tenement or pelting farm:","England, bound in with the triumphant sea,","Whose rocky shore beats back the envious siege","Of watery Neptune, is now bound in with shame,","With inky blots, and rotten parchment bonds:","That England, that was wont to conquer others,","Hath made a shameful conquest of itself.","Ah! would the scandal vanish with my life,","How happy then were my ensuing death."],"id":145,"m":"I feel I am a prophet newly inspired, and as I breathe my last I predict this about him: his reckless, fierce blaze of wild living cannot last, because violent fires soon burn themselves out. Light showers last a long time, but sudden storms are short. A man who spurs his horse too fast too early soon tires. Eating too greedily chokes the eater. Empty vanity, that greedy bird that is never full, soon eats itself once it has used up everything else. This royal throne of kings, this island ruled by a sceptre, this land of majesty, this home of the god of war, this second Eden, this half-paradise, this fortress that Nature built for herself against disease and the hand of war, this happy race of men, this little world, this precious stone set in the silver sea, which acts as a wall for it, or as a defensive moat around a house, against the envy of less fortunate countries; this blessed plot of ground, this earth, this kingdom, this England, this nurse, this fertile womb of royal kings, feared because of their ancestry and famous for their birth, renowned for their deeds as far from home, in Christian service and true knighthood, as the tomb, in stubborn Judea, of the world's saviour, blessed Mary's Son; this land of such dear souls, this dear, dear land, dear for its reputation throughout the world, is now leased out, and I die as I say it, like a rented house or a miserable farm. England, surrounded by the triumphant sea, whose rocky shore beats back the jealous attacks of the sea god Neptune, is now surrounded by shame, by inky blots and rotten paper contracts. That England, which used to conquer others, has made a shameful conquest of itself. Ah, if the disgrace would vanish when I die, how happy my coming death would be!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD and QUEEN; AUMERLE, BUSHY, GREEN, BAGOT, ROSS, and WILLOUGHBY","id":146},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The King is come: deal mildly with his youth;","For young hot colts, being rag'd, do rage the more."],"id":147,"m":"The King has come. Go gently with his youth, because hot young horses get wilder if you enrage them."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["How fares our noble uncle, Lancaster?"],"id":148,"m":"How is our noble uncle Lancaster?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What comfort, man? How is't with aged Gaunt?"],"id":149,"m":"How are you, man? How is old Gaunt?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["O! how that name befits my composition;","Old Gaunt, indeed; and gaunt in being old:","Within me grief hath kept a tedious fast;","And who abstains from meat that is not gaunt?","For sleeping England long time have I watch'd;","Watching breeds leanness, leanness is all gaunt.","The pleasure that some fathers feed upon","Is my strict fast, I mean my children's looks;","And therein fasting, hast thou made me gaunt.","Gaunt am I for the grave, gaunt as a grave,","Whose hollow womb inherits nought but bones."],"id":150,"m":"Oh, how well that name suits my condition! Old Gaunt indeed, and gaunt from being old. Grief has kept a long fast inside me, and who goes without food and isn't gaunt? I have kept watch over sleeping England for a long time. Staying awake makes you thin, and thinness is all gaunt. The pleasure some fathers feed on, I mean the sight of their children, I have been strictly denied. And by making me fast from that, you have made me gaunt. I am gaunt and ready for the grave, gaunt as a grave, whose hollow belly holds nothing but bones."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Can sick men play so nicely with their names?"],"id":151,"m":"Can sick men play so cleverly with their names?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["No, misery makes sport to mock itself:","Since thou dost seek to kill my name in me,","I mock my name, great king, to flatter thee."],"id":152,"m":"No, misery mocks itself for entertainment. Since you are trying to kill my name in my son, I mock my name, great King, to flatter you."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Should dying men flatter with those that live?"],"id":153,"m":"Should dying men flatter the living?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["No, no; men living flatter those that die."],"id":154,"m":"No, no; the living flatter the dying."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thou, now a-dying, sayest thou flatterest me."],"id":155,"m":"You, who are dying now, say you are flattering me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["O, no! thou diest, though I the sicker be."],"id":156,"m":"Oh, no! You are the one who is dying, though I am the sicker."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I am in health, I breathe, and see thee ill."],"id":157,"m":"I am healthy, I am breathing, and I see you are ill."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["Now, he that made me knows I see thee ill;","Ill in myself to see, and in thee seeing ill.","Thy death-bed is no lesser than thy land","Wherein thou liest in reputation sick:","And thou, too careless patient as thou art,","Committ'st thy anointed body to the cure","Of those physicians that first wounded thee:","A thousand flatterers sit within thy crown,","Whose compass is no bigger than thy head;","And yet, incaged in so small a verge,","The waste is no whit lesser than thy land.","O! had thy grandsire, with a prophet's eye,","Seen how his son's son should destroy his sons,","From forth thy reach he would have laid thy shame,","Deposing thee before thou wert possess'd,","Which art possess'd now to depose thyself.","Why, cousin, wert thou regent of the world,","It were a shame to let this land by lease;","But for thy world enjoying but this land,","Is it not more than shame to shame it so?","Landlord of England art thou now, not king:","Thy state of law is bondslave to the law,","And—"],"id":158,"m":"Now, God who made me knows I see you are ill. I am ill myself, and ill to see you doing wrong. Your deathbed is nothing less than your whole country, where your reputation lies sick. And you, careless patient that you are, hand your anointed body over to be cured by the same doctors who wounded you in the first place. A thousand flatterers sit inside your crown, whose circle is no bigger than your head. And yet, though they are caged in such a small space, the damage they do is as big as your whole country. Oh, if your grandfather had seen with a prophet's eye how his son's son would destroy his sons, he would have put your shame out of your reach by deposing you before you inherited, you who are now possessed by madness to depose yourself. Why, cousin, if you ruled the whole world, it would be shameful to lease out this land. But since this land is the only world you have, isn't it worse than shameful to shame it like this? You are England's landlord now, not its king. Your legal position makes you a slave to the law, and..."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And thou a lunatic lean-witted fool,","Presuming on an ague's privilege,","Dar'st with thy frozen admonition","Make pale our cheek, chasing the royal blood","With fury from his native residence.","Now by my seat's right royal majesty,","Wert thou not brother to great Edward's son,—","This tongue that runs so roundly in thy head","Should run thy head from thy unreverent shoulders."],"id":159,"m":"And you, a mad, half-witted fool, taking advantage of the privilege a fever gives you, dare to make my cheeks pale with your frozen warning, chasing the royal blood from its natural home with anger. Now, by the royal majesty of my throne, if you weren't the brother of great Edward's son, this tongue that runs so freely in your head would run your head right off your disrespectful shoulders."},{"t":"sp","who":"GAUNT","lines":["O! spare me not, my brother Edward's son,","For that I was his father Edward's son.","That blood already, like the pelican,","Hast thou tapp'd out, and drunkenly carous'd:","My brother Gloucester, plain well-meaning soul,—","Whom fair befall in heaven 'mongst happy souls!—","May be a precedent and witness good","That thou respect'st not spilling Edward's blood:","Join with the present sickness that I have;","And thy unkindness be like crooked age,","To crop at once a too-long withered flower.","Live in thy shame, but die not shame with thee!","These words hereafter thy tormentors be!","Convey me to my bed, then to my grave:","Love they to live that love and honour have."],"id":160,"m":"Oh, don't spare me, my brother Edward's son, just because I was his father Edward's son. Like the pelican chick that feeds on its mother's blood, you have already drained that blood and drunk it greedily. My brother Gloucester, a simple, well-meaning soul (may he be happy in heaven among blessed souls!), is a good example and proof that you don't care about spilling Edward's blood. Join forces with the sickness I have now, and let your cruelty, like bent old age, cut down at once a flower that has been withered too long. Live with your shame, but don't let your shame die with you! May these words torment you from now on! Carry me to my bed, then to my grave. Let those who have love and honour wish to live."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, bourne out by his Attendants","id":161},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And let them die that age and sullens have;","For both hast thou, and both become the grave."],"id":162,"m":"And let those who are old and sulky die. You have both, and both are fit for the grave."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I do beseech your Majesty, impute his words","To wayward sickliness and age in him:","He loves you, on my life, and holds you dear","As Harry Duke of Hereford, were he here."],"id":163,"m":"I beg your Majesty to put his words down to the bad temper of sickness and age. On my life, he loves you and holds you as dear as Harry Duke of Hereford, if he were here."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Right, you say true: as Hereford's love, so his;","As theirs, so mine; and all be as it is."],"id":164,"m":"Right, you're quite right. His love is like Hereford's, and mine is like theirs. And let everything be as it is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter NORTHUMBERLAND","id":165},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My liege, old Gaunt commends him to your Majesty."],"id":166,"m":"My lord, old Gaunt sends his respects to your Majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What says he?"],"id":167,"m":"What does he say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Nay, nothing; all is said:","His tongue is now a stringless instrument;","Words, life, and all, old Lancaster hath spent."],"id":168,"m":"No, nothing. Everything is said. His tongue is now an instrument without strings. Old Lancaster has used up his words, his life, everything."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Be York the next that must be bankrupt so!","Though death be poor, it ends a mortal woe."],"id":169,"m":"May York be the next to go bankrupt like that! Death may be poor, but it ends human misery."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["The ripest fruit first falls, and so doth he:","His time is spent; our pilgrimage must be.","So much for that. Now for our Irish wars.","We must supplant those rough rug-headed kerns,","Which live like venom where no venom else","But only they have privilege to live.","And for these great affairs do ask some charge,","Towards our assistance we do seize to us","The plate, coin, revenues, and moveables,","Whereof our uncle Gaunt did stand possess'd."],"id":170,"m":"The ripest fruit falls first, and so does he. His time is over; our journey through life must go on. So much for that. Now for our Irish wars. We must get rid of those rough, shaggy-haired Irish foot soldiers, who live like poison in a country where nothing else poisonous is allowed to live. And because these great matters cost money, to help pay for them I take for myself the silver plate, coins, income and possessions that belonged to our uncle Gaunt."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["How long shall I be patient? Ah! how long","Shall tender duty make me suffer wrong?","Not Gloucester's death, nor Hereford's banishment,","Nor Gaunt's rebukes, nor England's private wrongs,","Nor the prevention of poor Bolingbroke","About his marriage, nor my own disgrace,","Have ever made me sour my patient cheek,","Or bend one wrinkle on my sovereign's face.","I am the last of noble Edward's sons,","Of whom thy father, Prince of Wales, was first;","In war was never lion rag'd more fierce,","In peace was never gentle lamb more mild,","Than was that young and princely gentleman.","His face thou hast, for even so look'd he,","Accomplish'd with the number of thy hours;","But when he frown'd, it was against the French,","And not against his friends; his noble hand","Did win what he did spend, and spent not that","Which his triumphant father's hand had won:","His hands were guilty of no kindred's blood,","But bloody with the enemies of his kin.","O Richard! York is too far gone with grief,","Or else he never would compare between."],"id":171,"m":"How long shall I be patient? Ah, how long will my gentle sense of duty make me put up with wrong? Not Gloucester's death, not Hereford's exile, not Gaunt's humiliations, not England's private wrongs, not the way poor Bolingbroke's marriage abroad was blocked, not my own disgrace, has ever made me sour my patient face or frown even once at my king. I am the last of noble Edward's sons, of whom your father, the Prince of Wales, was the eldest. In war no lion ever raged more fiercely, and in peace no gentle lamb was ever milder, than that young prince. You have his face; he looked just like you when he was your age. But when he frowned, it was against the French, not against his friends. His noble hand won what he spent, and did not spend what his victorious father's hand had won. His hands were guilty of no relative's blood, but bloody with the blood of his family's enemies. Oh, Richard! York is too overcome with grief, or he would never make this comparison."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Why, uncle, what's the matter?"],"id":172,"m":"Why, uncle, what's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["O! my liege.","Pardon me, if you please; if not, I, pleas'd","Not to be pardon'd, am content withal.","Seek you to seize and gripe into your hands","The royalties and rights of banish'd Hereford?","Is not Gaunt dead, and doth not Hereford live?","Was not Gaunt just, and is not Harry true?","Did not the one deserve to have an heir?","Is not his heir a well-deserving son?","Take Hereford's rights away, and take from Time","His charters and his customary rights;","Let not to-morrow then ensue to-day;","Be not thyself; for how art thou a king","But by fair sequence and succession?","Now, afore God,—God forbid I say true!—","If you do wrongfully seize Hereford's rights,","Call in the letters-patents that he hath","By his attorneys-general to sue","His livery, and deny his offer'd homage,","You pluck a thousand dangers on your head,","You lose a thousand well-disposed hearts,","And prick my tender patience to those thoughts","Which honour and allegiance cannot think."],"id":173,"m":"Oh, my lord, forgive me if you want to. If not, I'm happy not to be forgiven. Are you trying to seize and grab into your hands the royal grants and rights of banished Hereford? Isn't Gaunt dead, and isn't Hereford alive? Wasn't Gaunt honourable, and isn't Harry loyal? Didn't the one deserve to have an heir? Isn't his heir a deserving son? Take away Hereford's rights, and you take away Time's legal charters and customary rights. Then don't let tomorrow follow today. Don't be yourself; for how are you a king except by proper order and succession? Now, before God, and God forbid I'm right, if you wrongfully seize Hereford's rights, cancel the royal letters that allow his lawyers to claim his inheritance, and refuse his offer of loyalty, you will pull a thousand dangers down on your head, lose a thousand well-meaning hearts, and push my gentle patience towards thoughts that a man of honour and loyalty cannot think."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Think what you will: we seize into our hands","His plate, his goods, his money, and his lands."],"id":174,"m":"Think what you like. I am taking his silver, his goods, his money and his lands into my hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I'll not be by the while: my liege, farewell:","What will ensue hereof there's none can tell;","But by bad courses may be understood","That their events can never fall out good."],"id":175,"m":"I won't be here while that happens. My lord, goodbye. No one can tell what will come of this; but we can understand from bad actions that their results can never turn out well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":176},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Go, Bushy, to the Earl of Wiltshire straight:","Bid him repair to us to Ely House","To see this business. To-morrow next","We will for Ireland; and 'tis time, I trow:","And we create, in absence of ourself,","Our Uncle York lord governor of England;","For he is just, and always lov'd us well.","Come on, our queen: to-morrow must we part;","Be merry, for our time of stay is short."],"id":177,"m":"Bushy, go to the Earl of Wiltshire at once. Tell him to come to me at Ely House to deal with this business. Tomorrow I leave for Ireland, and it's high time, I think. While I am away I make my uncle York governor of England, because he is fair and has always loved me. Come on, my queen. Tomorrow we must part. Be cheerful, because our time together is short."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt KING, QUEEN, BUSHY, AUMERLE, GREEN, and BAGOT","id":178},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Well, lords, the Duke of Lancaster is dead."],"id":179,"m":"Well, lords, the Duke of Lancaster is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["And living too; for now his son is Duke."],"id":180,"m":"And alive too, because now his son is Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["Barely in title, not in revenues."],"id":181,"m":"Only in title, not in income."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Richly in both, if justice had her right."],"id":182,"m":"Rich in both, if justice had its way."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["My heart is great; but it must break with silence,","Ere't be disburdened with a liberal tongue."],"id":183,"m":"My heart is full, but it must break in silence before I relieve it by speaking freely."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Nay, speak thy mind; and let him ne'er speak more","That speaks thy words again to do thee harm!"],"id":184,"m":"No, say what you think, and may anyone who repeats your words to harm you never speak again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["Tends that thou wouldst speak to the Duke of Hereford?","If it be so, out with it boldly, man;","Quick is mine ear to hear of good towards him."],"id":185,"m":"Is what you want to say about the Duke of Hereford? If so, out with it boldly, man. My ears are quick to hear good news about him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["No good at all that I can do for him,","Unless you call it good to pity him,","Bereft and gelded of his patrimony."],"id":186,"m":"There's no good I can do for him, unless you call it good to pity him, robbed and stripped of his inheritance."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Now, afore God, 'tis shame such wrongs are borne","In him, a royal prince, and many moe","Of noble blood in this declining land.","The king is not himself, but basely led","By flatterers; and what they will inform,","Merely in hate, 'gainst any of us all,","That will the king severely prosecute","'Gainst us, our lives, our children, and our heirs."],"id":187,"m":"Now, before God, it's shameful that such wrongs are done to him, a royal prince, and to many others of noble blood in this declining country. The king is not himself, but is shamefully led by flatterers; and whatever they tell him out of pure hatred against any of us, the king will harshly punish us for it, our lives, our children and our heirs."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["The commons hath he pill'd with grievous taxes,","And quite lost their hearts: the nobles hath he fin'd","For ancient quarrels and quite lost their hearts."],"id":188,"m":"He has stripped the common people with heavy taxes and lost their hearts completely. He has fined the nobles for old quarrels and lost their hearts completely too."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["And daily new exactions are devis'd;","As blanks, benevolences, and I wot not what:","But what, o' God's name, doth become of this?"],"id":189,"m":"And every day new ways of squeezing money are invented, like blank charters, forced 'gifts' and who knows what. But what, in God's name, happens to all this money?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Wars hath not wasted it, for warr'd he hath not,","But basely yielded upon compromise","That which his ancestors achiev'd with blows.","More hath he spent in peace than they in wars."],"id":190,"m":"Wars haven't used it up, because he hasn't fought any. Instead he has shamefully given up, by making deals, what his ancestors won with blows. He has spent more in peace than they did in war."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["The Earl of Wiltshire hath the realm in farm."],"id":191,"m":"The Earl of Wiltshire has the kingdom on lease."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["The King's grown bankrupt like a broken man."],"id":192,"m":"The King has gone bankrupt like a ruined man."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Reproach and dissolution hangeth over him."],"id":193,"m":"Disgrace and ruin are hanging over him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["He hath not money for these Irish wars,","His burdenous taxations notwithstanding,","But by the robbing of the banish'd Duke."],"id":194,"m":"In spite of his heavy taxes, he has no money for these Irish wars except by robbing the banished Duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["His noble kinsman: most degenerate king!","But, lords, we hear this fearful tempest sing,","Yet seek no shelter to avoid the storm;","We see the wind sit sore upon our sails,","And yet we strike not, but securely perish."],"id":195,"m":"His own noble relative. What a degenerate king! But, lords, we hear this terrible storm howling, and yet we look for no shelter from it. We see the wind pressing hard on our sails, and yet we don't lower them, but carelessly go to our deaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["We see the very wrack that we must suffer;","And unavoided is the danger now,","For suffering so the causes of our wrack."],"id":196,"m":"We can see the very shipwreck we are going to suffer, and the danger can't be avoided now, because we have put up with the causes of our wreck for so long."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Not so: even through the hollow eyes of death","I spy life peering; but I dare not say","How near the tidings of our comfort is."],"id":197,"m":"Not so. Even through the hollow eyes of death's skull I can see life peeping out; but I don't dare say how close the news of our rescue is."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["Nay, let us share thy thoughts as thou dost ours."],"id":198,"m":"No, share your thoughts with us, as you share ours."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["Be confident to speak, Northumberland:","We three are but thyself: and, speaking so,","Thy words are but as thoughts; therefore be bold."],"id":199,"m":"Speak with confidence, Northumberland. We three are the same as you, and so, said to us, your words are no more than thoughts. So be bold."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Then thus: I have from Le Port Blanc, a bay","In Brittany, receiv'd intelligence","That Harry Duke of Hereford, Rainold Lord Cobham,","That late broke from the Duke of Exeter,","His brother, Archbishop late of Canterbury,","Sir Thomas Erpingham, Sir John Ramston,","Sir John Norbery, Sir Robert Waterton, and Francis Quoint,","All these well furnish'd by the Duke of Britaine,","With eight tall ships, three thousand men of war,","Are making hither with all due expedience,","And shortly mean to touch our northern shore.","Perhaps they had ere this, but that they stay","The first departing of the king for Ireland.","If then we shall shake off our slavish yoke,","Imp out our drooping country's broken wing,","Redeem from broking pawn the blemish'd crown,","Wipe off the dust that hides our sceptre's gilt,","And make high majesty look like itself,","Away with me in post to Ravenspurgh;","But if you faint, as fearing to do so,","Stay and be secret, and myself will go."],"id":200,"m":"Then here it is. I have had news from Port Blanc, a bay in Brittany, that Harry Duke of Hereford, Rainold Lord Cobham, the son of the Earl of Arundel who recently escaped from the Duke of Exeter, his brother the former Archbishop of Canterbury, Sir Thomas Erpingham, Sir John Ramston, Sir John Norbery, Sir Robert Waterton and Francis Quoint, all well equipped by the Duke of Brittany with eight large ships and three thousand soldiers, are heading here as fast as they can and plan to land on our northern coast soon. Perhaps they would have landed already, but they are waiting for the king to leave for Ireland first. So if we are going to throw off our slavery, repair our drooping country's broken wing, buy back the stained crown from the pawnbroker, wipe off the dust that hides the gold of our sceptre and make royal majesty look like itself again, come with me at full speed to Ravenspurgh. But if you lose your nerve and are afraid to, stay here and keep it secret, and I will go myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["To horse, to horse! Urge doubts to them that fear."],"id":201,"m":"To horse, to horse! Raise your doubts with those who are afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["Hold out my horse, and I will first be there."],"id":202,"m":"If my horse holds out, I'll be there first."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":203},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"The Same. A Room in the Castle.","id":204},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter QUEEN, BUSHY, and BAGOT","id":205},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Madam, your Majesty is too much sad.","You promis'd, when you parted with the king,","To lay aside life-harming heaviness,","And entertain a cheerful disposition."],"id":206,"m":"Madam, your Majesty is much too sad. When you said goodbye to the king, you promised to put aside this sadness that harms your health and to keep cheerful."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["To please the King, I did; to please myself","I cannot do it; yet I know no cause","Why I should welcome such a guest as grief,","Save bidding farewell to so sweet a guest","As my sweet Richard: yet again methinks,","Some unborn sorrow, ripe in fortune's womb,","Is coming towards me, and my inward soul","With nothing trembles; at some thing it grieves","More than with parting from my lord the king."],"id":207,"m":"I promised to please the King. To please myself I cannot do it. And yet I know no reason why I should welcome such a guest as grief, except that I have said goodbye to such a sweet guest as my sweet Richard. And yet I feel that some sorrow not yet born, ripe in the womb of fortune, is coming towards me, and my inner soul trembles at nothing. It grieves at something more than parting from my husband the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Each substance of a grief hath twenty shadows,","Which shows like grief itself, but is not so;","For sorrow's eye, glazed with blinding tears,","Divides one thing entire to many objects;","Like perspectives which, rightly gaz'd upon,","Show nothing but confusion; ey'd awry,","Distinguish form: so your sweet Majesty,","Looking awry upon your lord's departure,","Find shapes of grief more than himself to wail;","Which, look'd on as it is, is nought but shadows","Of what it is not. Then, thrice-gracious Queen,","More than your lord's departure weep not: more's not seen;","Or if it be, 'tis with false sorrow's eye,","Which for things true weeps things imaginary."],"id":208,"m":"Every real grief casts twenty shadows that look like grief itself but are not. Sorrow's eye, glazed with blinding tears, splits one whole thing into many, like those trick pictures which show nothing but a blur when you look at them straight on, but show a clear shape when you look at them from the side. In the same way, your sweet Majesty, looking at your husband's departure from the wrong angle, finds more shapes of grief in it than the departure itself; and those, seen as they really are, are only shadows of what is not there. So, most gracious Queen, weep for nothing more than your husband's departure. There is nothing more to see; or if there is, it is seen with sorrow's false eye, which weeps for imaginary things as if they were real."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["It may be so; but yet my inward soul","Persuades me it is otherwise: howe'er it be,","I cannot but be sad, so heavy sad","As, though in thinking, on no thought I think,","Makes me with heavy nothing faint and shrink."],"id":209,"m":"That may be so; but my inner soul tells me it's otherwise. However it is, I can't help being sad, so heavily sad that, though I try to think of nothing, this heavy nothing makes me faint and shrink."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["'Tis nothing but conceit, my gracious lady."],"id":210,"m":"It's nothing but imagination, my gracious lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["'Tis nothing less: conceit is still deriv'd","From some forefather grief; mine is not so,","For nothing hath begot my something grief,","Or something hath the nothing that I grieve:","'Tis in reversion that I do possess;","But what it is, that is not yet known; what","I cannot name; 'tis nameless woe, I wot."],"id":211,"m":"It's anything but that. Imagination always comes from some earlier grief. Mine isn't like that, because nothing has given birth to my something grief, or else something has the nothing I grieve about. It's as if I own a grief that is still to come to me; but what it is, I don't yet know. I can't name it. It's a nameless sorrow, I'm sure."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter GREEN","id":212},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["God save your majesty! and well met, gentlemen:","I hope the King is not yet shipp'd for Ireland."],"id":213,"m":"God save your majesty! And good to see you, gentlemen. I hope the King hasn't sailed for Ireland yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Why hop'st thou so? 'Tis better hope he is,","For his designs crave haste, his haste good hope:","Then wherefore dost thou hope he is not shipp'd?"],"id":214,"m":"Why do you hope that? It's better to hope he has, because his plans need speed, and his speed gives us good hope. So why do you hope he hasn't sailed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["That he, our hope, might have retir'd his power,","And driven into despair an enemy's hope","Who strongly hath set footing in this land:","The banish'd Bolingbroke repeals himself,","And with uplifted arms is safe arriv'd","At Ravenspurgh."],"id":215,"m":"So that he, our hope, could have brought his army back and driven to despair the hopes of an enemy who has landed in force in this country. The banished Bolingbroke has called himself back from exile, and has arrived safely at Ravenspurgh with his weapons raised."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Now God in heaven forbid!"],"id":216,"m":"Now God in heaven forbid!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Ah! madam, 'tis too true; and that is worse,","The Lord Northumberland, his son young Henry Percy,","The Lords of Ross, Beaumond, and Willoughby,","With all their powerful friends, are fled to him."],"id":217,"m":"Ah, madam, it's all too true. And what is worse, Lord Northumberland, his son young Henry Percy, the Lords of Ross, Beaumond and Willoughby, with all their powerful friends, have gone over to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Why have you not proclaim'd Northumberland","And all the rest revolted faction traitors?"],"id":218,"m":"Why haven't you declared Northumberland and all the rest of the rebel group traitors?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["We have: whereupon the Earl of Worcester","Hath broken his staff, resign'd his stewardship,","And all the household servants fled with him","To Bolingbroke."],"id":219,"m":"We have. And so the Earl of Worcester has broken his staff of office, resigned as steward, and all the servants of the royal household have run off with him to Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["So, Green, thou art the midwife to my woe,","And Bolingbroke my sorrow's dismal heir:","Now hath my soul brought forth her prodigy,","And I, a gasping new-deliver'd mother,","Have woe to woe, sorrow to sorrow join'd."],"id":220,"m":"So, Green, you are the midwife of my sorrow, and Bolingbroke is the dreadful child of my sadness. Now my soul has given birth to its monster, and I, a gasping mother who has just delivered, have joined sorrow to sorrow and grief to grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Despair not, madam."],"id":221,"m":"Don't despair, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Who shall hinder me?","I will despair, and be at enmity","With cozening hope: he is a flatterer,","A parasite, a keeper-back of death,","Who gently would dissolve the bands of life,","Which false hope lingers in extremity."],"id":222,"m":"Who will stop me? I will despair and be the enemy of cheating hope. Hope is a flatterer, a hanger-on, something that holds back death, which would gently untie the bonds of life that false hope keeps dragging out at the very end."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK","id":223},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Here comes the Duke of York."],"id":224,"m":"Here comes the Duke of York."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["With signs of war about his aged neck:","O! full of careful business are his looks.","Uncle, for God's sake, speak comfortable words."],"id":225,"m":"With armour round his old neck. Oh, his face is full of worried business! Uncle, for God's sake, say something comforting."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Should I do so, I should belie my thoughts:","Comfort's in heaven; and we are on the earth,","Where nothing lives but crosses, cares, and grief.","Your husband, he is gone to save far off,","Whilst others come to make him lose at home.","Here am I left to underprop his land,","Who, weak with age, cannot support myself.","Now comes the sick hour that his surfeit made;","Now shall he try his friends that flatter'd him."],"id":226,"m":"If I did, I would be lying about my thoughts. Comfort is in heaven, and we are on earth, where there is nothing but trouble, worry and grief. Your husband has gone far away to save one land, while others come to make him lose this one at home. I am left here to prop up his country, when I am so weak with age I can't support myself. Now comes the sickness that his excesses have caused. Now he will find out what his flattering friends are worth."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant","id":227},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord, your son was gone before I came."],"id":228,"m":"My lord, your son had gone before I got there."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["He was? Why, so! go all which way it will!","The nobles they are fled, the commons they are cold,","And will, I fear, revolt on Hereford's side.","Sirrah, get thee to Plashy, to my sister Gloucester;","Bid her send me presently a thousand pound.","Hold, take my ring."],"id":229,"m":"He had? Well then, let everything go whichever way it likes! The nobles have run away, the common people are unenthusiastic and, I fear, will rebel on Hereford's side. You, go to Plashy, to my sister-in-law Gloucester. Tell her to send me a thousand pounds at once. Wait, take my ring."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord, I had forgot to tell your lordship:","To-day, as I came by, I called there;","But I shall grieve you to report the rest."],"id":230,"m":"My lord, I forgot to tell your lordship. Today I called there on my way here; but it will upset you to hear the rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["What is't, knave?"],"id":231,"m":"What is it, man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["An hour before I came the duchess died."],"id":232,"m":"An hour before I arrived, the duchess died."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["God for his mercy! what a tide of woes","Comes rushing on this woeful land at once!","I know not what to do: I would to God,—","So my untruth had not provok'd him to it,—","The king had cut off my head with my brother's.","What! are there no posts dispatch'd for Ireland?","How shall we do for money for these wars?","Come, sister,—cousin, I would say,—pray, pardon me.—","Go, fellow, get thee home; provide some carts,","And bring away the armour that is there."],"id":233,"m":"God have mercy! What a flood of sorrows comes rushing over this sad country all at once! I don't know what to do. I wish to God, as long as it wasn't disloyalty in me that provoked him, that the king had cut off my head along with my brother's. What! Haven't any messengers been sent to Ireland? How will we find money for these wars? Come, sister... cousin, I mean. Please forgive me. Go, man, go home, get some carts and bring away the armour that's there."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant","id":234},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Gentlemen, will you go muster men?","If I know how or which way to order these affairs","Thus disorderly thrust into my hands,","Never believe me. Both are my kinsmen:","T'one is my sovereign, whom both my oath","And duty bids defend; the other again","Is my kinsman, whom the king hath wrong'd,","Whom conscience and my kindred bids to right.","Well, somewhat we must do. Come, cousin,","I'll dispose of you. Gentlemen, go muster up your men,","And meet me presently at Berkeley Castle.","I should to Plashy too:","But time will not permit. All is uneven,","And everything is left at six and seven."],"id":235,"m":"Gentlemen, will you go and gather troops? If I know how or which way to manage these affairs that have been shoved into my hands in such a mess, never believe me again. Both are my relatives. One is my king, whom my oath and my duty both tell me to defend. The other is also my relative, whom the king has wronged, and whom my conscience and family feeling tell me to put right. Well, we must do something. Come, cousin, I'll find a place for you. Gentlemen, go and gather your men and meet me straight away at Berkeley Castle. I ought to go to Plashy too, but there isn't time. Everything is uneven, and everything is in total confusion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt YORK and QUEEN","id":236},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["The wind sits fair for news to go to Ireland,","But none returns. For us to levy power","Proportionable to the enemy","Is all unpossible."],"id":237,"m":"The wind is good for sending news to Ireland, but no news comes back. For us to raise an army as big as the enemy's is completely impossible."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Besides, our nearness to the king in love","Is near the hate of those love not the king."],"id":238,"m":"Besides, the King's love for us makes us close to the hatred of those who don't love the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["And that is the wavering commons; for their love","Lies in their purses; and whoso empties them,","By so much fills their hearts with deadly hate."],"id":239,"m":"And that means the unreliable common people, because their love lies in their purses, and whoever empties them fills their hearts with deadly hatred just as much."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Wherein the king stands generally condemn'd."],"id":240,"m":"And that's why the king is hated by almost everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["If judgment lie in them, then so do we,","Because we ever have been near the king."],"id":241,"m":"If they are to be the judges, then so are we condemned, because we have always been close to the king."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Well, I will for refuge straight to Bristol Castle.","The Earl of Wiltshire is already there."],"id":242,"m":"Well, I'm going straight to Bristol Castle for safety. The Earl of Wiltshire is already there."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Thither will I with you; for little office","Will the hateful commons perform for us,","Except like curs to tear us all to pieces.","Will you go along with us?"],"id":243,"m":"I'll go there with you, because the hateful common people won't do much for us except tear us all to pieces like dogs. Will you come with us?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["No; I will to Ireland to his Majesty.","Farewell: If heart's presages be not vain,","We three here part that ne'er shall meet again."],"id":244,"m":"No. I will go to Ireland to his Majesty. Goodbye. If my heart's forebodings are right, we three are parting here and will never meet again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["That's as York thrives to beat back Bolingbroke."],"id":245,"m":"That depends on whether York succeeds in beating back Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["Alas, poor Duke! the task he undertakes","Is numb'ring sands and drinking oceans dry:","Where one on his side fights, thousands will fly.","Farewell at once; for once, for all, and ever."],"id":246,"m":"Alas, poor Duke! The task he is taking on is like counting the sands and drinking the oceans dry. For every one who fights on his side, thousands will run away. Goodbye now, once and for all, and for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["Well, we may meet again."],"id":247,"m":"Well, we may meet again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["I fear me, never."],"id":248,"m":"I'm afraid never."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":249},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"The Wolds in Gloucestershire.","id":250},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BOLINGBROKE and NORTHUMBERLAND, with Forces","id":251},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["How far is it, my lord, to Berkeley now?"],"id":252,"m":"How far is it to Berkeley now, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Believe me, noble lord,","I am a stranger here in Gloucestershire.","These high wild hills and rough uneven ways","Draws out our miles, and makes them wearisome;","And yet your fair discourse hath been as sugar,","Making the hard way sweet and delectable.","But I bethink me what a weary way","From Ravenspurgh to Cotswold will be found","In Ross and Willoughby, wanting your company,","Which, I protest, hath very much beguil'd","The tediousness and process of my travel.","But theirs is sweeten'd with the hope to have","The present benefit which I possess;","And hope to joy is little less in joy","Than hope enjoy'd: by this the weary lords","Shall make their way seem short, as mine hath done","By sight of what I have, your noble company."],"id":253,"m":"Believe me, noble lord, I don't know my way around Gloucestershire. These high, wild hills and rough, bumpy roads stretch out our miles and make them tiring. And yet your pleasant conversation has been like sugar, making the hard road sweet and delightful. But I think what a weary road Ross and Willoughby will find it from Ravenspurgh to the Cotswolds without your company, which, I swear, has made the dullness and length of my journey pass much more quickly. But their journey is sweetened by the hope of having the benefit I have now; and hoping for joy is not much less joyful than joy itself. In this way the weary lords will make their road seem short, as mine has been made short by having what I have: your noble company."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Of much less value is my company","Than your good words. But who comes here?"],"id":254,"m":"My company is worth much less than your kind words. But who is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HARRY PERCY","id":255},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["It is my son, young Harry Percy,","Sent from my brother Worcester, whencesoever.","Harry, how fares your uncle?"],"id":256,"m":"It's my son, young Harry Percy, sent from my brother Worcester, wherever he is. Harry, how is your uncle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["I had thought, my lord, to have learn'd his health of you."],"id":257,"m":"I thought I would find out how he is from you, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Why, is he not with the Queen?"],"id":258,"m":"Why, isn't he with the Queen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["No, my good lord; he hath forsook the court,","Broken his staff of office, and dispers'd","The household of the King."],"id":259,"m":"No, my good lord. He has left the court, broken his staff of office and dismissed the King's household."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["What was his reason?","He was not so resolv'd when last we spake together."],"id":260,"m":"Why did he do that? He hadn't decided to when we last spoke."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["Because your lordship was proclaimed traitor.","But he, my lord, is gone to Ravenspurgh,","To offer service to the Duke of Hereford;","And sent me over by Berkeley, to discover","What power the Duke of York had levied there;","Then with directions to repair to Ravenspurgh."],"id":261,"m":"Because your lordship was declared a traitor. But he has gone to Ravenspurgh, my lord, to offer his service to the Duke of Hereford. He sent me by way of Berkeley to find out how many troops the Duke of York had raised there, with orders to come on to Ravenspurgh afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Have you forgot the Duke of Hereford, boy?"],"id":262,"m":"Have you forgotten the Duke of Hereford, boy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["No, my good lord; for that is not forgot","Which ne'er I did remember; to my knowledge,","I never in my life did look on him."],"id":263,"m":"No, my good lord, because you can't forget what you never remembered. As far as I know, I have never set eyes on him in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Then learn to know him now; this is the duke."],"id":264,"m":"Then get to know him now. This is the duke."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["My gracious lord, I tender you my service,","Such as it is, being tender, raw, and young,","Which elder days shall ripen, and confirm","To more approved service and desert."],"id":265,"m":"My gracious lord, I offer you my service, such as it is, young, raw and untried. Older age will ripen it and strengthen it into more proven service and greater worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I thank thee, gentle Percy; and be sure","I count myself in nothing else so happy","As in a soul remembering my good friends;","And as my fortune ripens with thy love,","It shall be still thy true love's recompense.","My heart this covenant makes, my hand thus seals it."],"id":266,"m":"Thank you, noble Percy. And be sure that nothing makes me happier than having a soul that remembers my good friends. As my fortune ripens with your love, it will always reward your true love. My heart makes this promise, and my hand seals it like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["How far is it to Berkeley? And what stir","Keeps good old York there with his men of war?"],"id":267,"m":"How far is it to Berkeley? And what forces is good old York keeping there with his soldiers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["There stands the castle, by yon tuft of trees,","Mann'd with three hundred men, as I have heard;","And in it are the Lords of York, Berkeley, and Seymour;","None else of name and noble estimate."],"id":268,"m":"There's the castle, by that clump of trees, defended by three hundred men, as I've heard. Inside are the Lords York, Berkeley and Seymour, and nobody else of high rank and importance."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Ross and WILLOUGHBY","id":269},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Here come the Lords of Ross and Willoughby,","Bloody with spurring, fiery-red with haste."],"id":270,"m":"Here come the Lords Ross and Willoughby, bloody from spurring their horses and red-faced with haste."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Welcome, my lords. I wot your love pursues","A banish'd traitor; all my treasury","Is yet but unfelt thanks, which, more enrich'd,","Shall be your love and labour's recompense."],"id":271,"m":"Welcome, my lords. I know your love follows a banished traitor. All the treasure I have yet is thanks you can't touch; but when I am richer, it will reward your love and your efforts."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["Your presence makes us rich, most noble lord."],"id":272,"m":"Your presence makes us rich, most noble lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["And far surmounts our labour to attain it."],"id":273,"m":"And far outweighs the effort we made to reach it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Evermore thanks, the exchequer of the poor;","Which, till my infant fortune comes to years,","Stands for my bounty. But who comes here?"],"id":274,"m":"Thanks again and always, the only treasury of the poor. Until my infant fortune grows up, thanks must stand for my generosity. But who is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BERKELEY","id":275},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["It is my Lord of Berkeley, as I guess."],"id":276,"m":"It's my Lord Berkeley, I think."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERKELEY","lines":["My Lord of Hereford, my message is to you."],"id":277,"m":"My Lord of Hereford, I have a message for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My lord, my answer is—to Lancaster;","And I am come to seek that name in England;","And I must find that title in your tongue","Before I make reply to aught you say."],"id":278,"m":"My lord, I answer only to the name Lancaster. I have come to look for that name in England, and I must hear that title from your tongue before I reply to anything you say."},{"t":"sp","who":"BERKELEY","lines":["Mistake me not, my lord; 'tis not my meaning","To raze one title of your honour out:","To you, my lord, I come, what lord you will,","From the most gracious regent of this land,","The Duke of York, to know what pricks you on","To take advantage of the absent time,","And fright our native peace with self-borne arms."],"id":279,"m":"Don't misunderstand me, my lord. I don't mean to scratch out one title of your honour. I come to you, my lord, whatever lord you like, from the most gracious regent of this country, the Duke of York, to find out what drives you to take advantage of the King's absence and frighten our peaceful country with weapons you carry on your own authority."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK, attended","id":280},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I shall not need transport my words by you;","Here comes his Grace in person.","My noble uncle!"],"id":281,"m":"I won't need you to carry my words; here comes his Grace in person. My noble uncle!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels","id":282},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Show me thy humble heart, and not thy knee,","Whose duty is deceivable and false."],"id":283,"m":"Show me a humble heart, not a bent knee, whose show of duty is deceptive and false."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My gracious uncle—"],"id":284,"m":"My gracious uncle..."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Tut, tut!","Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle:","I am no traitor's uncle; and that word 'grace'","In an ungracious mouth is but profane.","Why have those banish'd and forbidden legs","Dar'd once to touch a dust of England's ground?","But then more 'why?' why have they dar'd to march","So many miles upon her peaceful bosom,","Frighting her pale-fac'd villages with war","And ostentation of despised arms?","Com'st thou because the anointed king is hence?","Why, foolish boy, the king is left behind,","And in my loyal bosom lies his power.","Were I but now lord of such hot youth","As when brave Gaunt, thy father, and myself","Rescued the Black Prince, that young Mars of men,","From forth the ranks of many thousand French,","O! then how quickly should this arm of mine,","Now prisoner to the palsy, chastise thee","And minister correction to thy fault!"],"id":285,"m":"Tut, tut! Don't 'grace' me, and don't 'uncle' me. I am no traitor's uncle, and that word 'grace' is an insult to God in a mouth without grace. Why have those banished, forbidden legs dared to touch even one speck of English dust? And more 'why': why have they dared to march so many miles over her peaceful breast, frightening her pale-faced villages with war and a display of hateful weapons? Have you come because the anointed king is away? Why, foolish boy, the king is left behind here, and his power lies in my loyal heart. If I were now as young and hot-blooded as when brave Gaunt, your father, and I rescued the Black Prince, that young god of war among men, from the ranks of many thousand Frenchmen, oh, how quickly this arm of mine, now trembling with palsy, would punish you and correct your fault!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My gracious uncle, let me know my fault:","On what condition stands it and wherein?"],"id":286,"m":"My gracious uncle, tell me what my fault is. What kind of fault is it, and where does it lie?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Even in condition of the worst degree,","In gross rebellion and detested treason:","Thou art a banish'd man, and here art come","Before the expiration of thy time,","In braving arms against thy sovereign."],"id":287,"m":"It is a fault of the very worst kind: open rebellion and hateful treason. You are a banished man, and you have come here before the end of your sentence, defying your king with weapons."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["As I was banish'd, I was banish'd Hereford;","But as I come, I come for Lancaster.","And, noble uncle, I beseech your Grace","Look on my wrongs with an indifferent eye:","You are my father, for methinks in you","I see old Gaunt alive: O! then, my father,","Will you permit that I shall stand condemn'd","A wandering vagabond; my rights and royalties","Pluck'd from my arms perforce, and given away","To upstart unthrifts? Wherefore was I born?","If that my cousin king be King in England,","It must be granted I am Duke of Lancaster.","You have a son, Aumerle, my noble cousin;","Had you first died, and he been thus trod down,","He should have found his uncle Gaunt a father","To rouse his wrongs and chase them to the bay.","I am denied to sue my livery here,","And yet my letters-patents give me leave.","My father's goods are all distrain'd and sold;","And these and all are all amiss employ'd.","What would you have me do? I am a subject,","And challenge law: attorneys are denied me;","And therefore personally I lay my claim","To my inheritance of free descent."],"id":288,"m":"When I was banished, it was Hereford who was banished; but I come back as Lancaster. And, noble uncle, I beg your Grace to look at the wrongs done to me with an impartial eye. You are my father, because I think I see old Gaunt alive in you. Oh, then, my father, will you let me be condemned to be a wandering beggar, my rights and titles snatched from my arms by force and given away to upstart spendthrifts? What was I born for? If my cousin is King of England, it must follow that I am Duke of Lancaster. You have a son, Aumerle, my noble cousin. If you had died first and he had been trampled on like this, he would have found a father in his uncle Gaunt, to rouse up his wrongs and hunt them down. I am not allowed to claim my inheritance here, even though my royal letters give me the right. My father's goods have all been seized and sold, and all of it has been wrongly used. What do you want me to do? I am a subject and I appeal to the law. I am refused lawyers, and so I am claiming my lawful inheritance in person."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["The noble Duke hath been too much abus'd."],"id":289,"m":"The noble Duke has been very badly treated."},{"t":"sp","who":"ROSS","lines":["It stands your Grace upon to do him right."],"id":290,"m":"Your Grace has a duty to see that he gets justice."},{"t":"sp","who":"WILLOUGHBY","lines":["Base men by his endowments are made great."],"id":291,"m":"Worthless men have been made great with his property."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["My lords of England, let me tell you this:","I have had feeling of my cousin's wrongs,","And labour'd all I could to do him right;","But in this kind to come, in braving arms,","Be his own carver and cut out his way,","To find out right with wrong, it may not be;","And you that do abet him in this kind","Cherish rebellion, and are rebels all."],"id":292,"m":"My lords of England, let me tell you this. I have felt for my nephew's wrongs and worked as hard as I could to put them right. But to come like this, in defiant arms, carving out his own way and looking for right by doing wrong, cannot be allowed. And you who help him like this are encouraging rebellion, and you are all rebels."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["The noble Duke hath sworn his coming is","But for his own; and for the right of that","We all have strongly sworn to give him aid;","And let him never see joy that breaks that oath!"],"id":293,"m":"The noble Duke has sworn that he has come only for what is his own; and we have all solemnly sworn to help him get his rights. May anyone who breaks that oath never know joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Well, well, I see the issue of these arms:","I cannot mend it, I must needs confess,","Because my power is weak and all ill left;","But if I could, by him that gave me life,","I would attach you all and make you stoop","Unto the sovereign mercy of the king;","But since I cannot, be it known unto you","I do remain as neuter. So, fare you well;","Unless you please to enter in the castle,","And there repose you for this night."],"id":294,"m":"Well, well, I see how this armed rising will end. I must admit I can't stop it, because my army is weak and has been badly left. But if I could, by God who gave me life, I would arrest you all and make you bow to the royal mercy of the king. But since I can't, I tell you that I will stay neutral. So, goodbye, unless you would like to come into the castle and rest there for the night."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["An offer, uncle, that we will accept:","But we must win your Grace to go with us","To Bristol Castle, which they say is held","By Bushy, Bagot, and their complices,","The caterpillars of the commonwealth,","Which I have sworn to weed and pluck away."],"id":295,"m":"An offer, uncle, that we accept. But we must persuade your Grace to go with us to Bristol Castle, which they say is held by Bushy, Bagot and their accomplices, the caterpillars eating up the country, which I have sworn to weed out and pull up."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["It may be I will go with you; but yet I'll pause,","For I am loath to break our country's laws.","Nor friends nor foes, to me welcome you are.","Things past redress are now with me past care."],"id":296,"m":"I may go with you; but I'll think about it first, because I don't want to break our country's laws. You are welcome to me neither as friends nor as enemies. Things that can't be put right are now past my worrying about."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":297},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"A camp in Wales.","id":298},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EARL OF SALISBURY and a CAPTAIN","id":299},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["My Lord of Salisbury, we have stay'd ten days","And hardly kept our countrymen together,","And yet we hear no tidings from the King;","Therefore we will disperse ourselves: farewell."],"id":300,"m":"My Lord Salisbury, we have waited ten days and only just kept our men together, and still we hear no news from the King. So we will break up and go home. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Stay yet another day, thou trusty Welshman;","The King reposeth all his confidence in thee."],"id":301,"m":"Stay one more day, trusty Welshman. The King puts all his trust in you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPTAIN","lines":["'Tis thought the king is dead; we will not stay.","The bay trees in our country are all wither'd,","And meteors fright the fixed stars of heaven;","The pale-fac'd moon looks bloody on the earth","And lean-look'd prophets whisper fearful change;","Rich men look sad, and ruffians dance and leap,","The one in fear to lose what they enjoy,","The other to enjoy by rage and war.","These signs forerun the death or fall of kings.","Farewell: our countrymen are gone and fled,","As well assur'd Richard their king is dead."],"id":302,"m":"People think the king is dead. We won't stay. The bay trees in our country have all withered, and comets frighten the fixed stars in heaven. The pale moon looks blood-red on the earth, and thin-faced prophets whisper about terrifying changes. Rich men look sad, and thugs dance and jump about, the rich because they are afraid of losing what they have, the thugs because they hope to gain from violence and war. These signs come before the death or fall of kings. Goodbye. Our countrymen have gone and run off, sure that Richard their king is dead."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":303},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Ah, Richard! with the eyes of heavy mind,","I see thy glory like a shooting star","Fall to the base earth from the firmament.","The sun sets weeping in the lowly west,","Witnessing storms to come, woe, and unrest.","Thy friends are fled, to wait upon thy foes,","And crossly to thy good all fortune goes."],"id":304,"m":"Ah, Richard! With the eyes of a heavy heart I see your glory fall like a shooting star from the sky to the lowly earth. Your sun sets weeping in the humble west, foretelling storms, sorrow and trouble to come. Your friends have run off to serve your enemies, and all fortune goes against you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":305},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Bristol. BOLINGBROKE'S camp.","id":306},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BOLINGBROKE, YORK, NORTHUMBERLAND, HENRY PERCY, WILLOUGHBY, ROSS; Officers behind, with BUSHY and GREEN, prisoners","id":307},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Bring forth these men.","Bushy and Green, I will not vex your souls—","Since presently your souls must part your bodies—","With too much urging your pernicious lives,","For 'twere no charity; yet, to wash your blood","From off my hands, here in the view of men","I will unfold some causes of your deaths.","You have misled a prince, a royal king,","A happy gentleman in blood and lineaments,","By you unhappied and disfigur'd clean;","You have in manner with your sinful hours","Made a divorce betwixt his queen and him,","Broke the possession of a royal bed,","And stain'd the beauty of a fair queen's cheeks","With tears drawn from her eyes by your foul wrongs.","Myself, a prince by fortune of my birth,","Near to the King in blood, and near in love","Till you did make him misinterpret me,","Have stoop'd my neck under your injuries,","And sigh'd my English breath in foreign clouds,","Eating the bitter bread of banishment;","Whilst you have fed upon my signories,","Dispark'd my parks and felled my forest woods,","From my own windows torn my household coat,","Raz'd out my impress, leaving me no sign","Save men's opinions and my living blood","To show the world I am a gentleman.","This and much more, much more than twice all this,","Condemns you to the death. See them deliver'd over","To execution and the hand of death."],"id":308,"m":"Bring these men out. Bushy and Green, I won't torment your souls, since they must leave your bodies very soon, by dwelling too much on your wicked lives; that would be unkind. But to wash your blood off my hands, I will set out here, in front of everyone, some of the reasons for your deaths. You have misled a prince, a royal king, a fortunate gentleman in birth and looks, whom you made unfortunate and completely spoiled. With your sinful pastimes you have more or less divorced him from his queen, broken up a royal marriage bed and stained the beauty of a fair queen's cheeks with tears your foul wrongs drew from her eyes. I myself, a prince by birth, close to the King in blood and close in love until you made him misjudge me, have bent my neck under the wrongs you did me and sighed my English breath into foreign clouds, eating the bitter bread of exile. Meanwhile you have fed on my estates, opened up my private parks and cut down my forests, torn my coat of arms out of my own windows and scratched out my emblem, leaving me no sign except people's opinion and my living blood to show the world I am a gentleman. This, and much more, much more than twice all this, condemns you to death. See that they are handed over for execution and the hand of death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BUSHY","lines":["More welcome is the stroke of death to me","Than Bolingbroke to England. Lords, farewell."],"id":309,"m":"The stroke of death is more welcome to me than Bolingbroke is to England. Lords, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREEN","lines":["My comfort is that heaven will take our souls,","And plague injustice with the pains of hell."],"id":310,"m":"My comfort is that heaven will take our souls and punish injustice with the pains of hell."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My Lord Northumberland, see them dispatch'd."],"id":311,"m":"My Lord Northumberland, see that they are executed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt NORTHUMBERLAND, and Others, with BUSHY and GREEN","id":312},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","cont":1,"lines":["Uncle, you say the Queen is at your house;","For God's sake, fairly let her be entreated:","Tell her I send to her my kind commends;","Take special care my greetings be deliver'd."],"id":313,"m":"Uncle, you say the Queen is at your house. For God's sake, see that she is treated well. Tell her I send her my kind regards. Take special care that my greetings are delivered."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["A gentleman of mine I have dispatch'd","With letters of your love to her at large."],"id":314,"m":"I have sent one of my gentlemen with a letter setting out your kind feelings towards her in full."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Thanks, gentle uncle. Come, lords, away,","To fight with Glendower and his complices.","Awhile to work, and after holiday."],"id":315,"m":"Thank you, kind uncle. Come, lords, let's go to fight Glendower and his allies. A little work now, and a holiday afterwards."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":316},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The coast of Wales. A castle in view.","id":317},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish: drums and trumpets. Enter KING RICHARD, the BISHOP OF CARLISLE, AUMERLE, and soldiers","id":318},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Barkloughly Castle call they this at hand?"],"id":319,"m":"Is this the castle nearby they call Barkloughly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Yea, my lord. How brooks your Grace the air","After your late tossing on the breaking seas?"],"id":320,"m":"Yes, my lord. How does your Grace like the air after being tossed about on the rough seas?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Needs must I like it well: I weep for joy","To stand upon my kingdom once again.","Dear earth, I do salute thee with my hand,","Though rebels wound thee with their horses' hoofs:","As a long-parted mother with her child","Plays fondly with her tears and smiles in meeting,","So weeping-smiling greet I thee, my earth,","And do thee favours with my royal hands.","Feed not thy sovereign's foe, my gentle earth,","Nor with thy sweets comfort his ravenous sense;","But let thy spiders, that suck up thy venom,","And heavy-gaited toads lie in their way,","Doing annoyance to the treacherous feet","Which with usurping steps do trample thee.","Yield stinging nettles to mine enemies;","And when they from thy bosom pluck a flower,","Guard it, I pray thee, with a lurking adder","Whose double tongue may with a mortal touch","Throw death upon thy sovereign's enemies.","Mock not my senseless conjuration, lords.","This earth shall have a feeling, and these stones","Prove armed soldiers, ere her native king","Shall falter under foul rebellion's arms."],"id":321,"m":"I must like it very much. I weep for joy to stand on my kingdom once again. Dear earth, I greet you with my hand, though rebels wound you with their horses' hooves. Like a mother long separated from her child, who weeps and smiles tenderly when they meet, I greet you, my earth, weeping and smiling, and touch you lovingly with my royal hands. Don't feed your king's enemy, my gentle earth, and don't comfort his greedy appetite with your sweet things. Let your spiders, that suck up your poison, and slow, heavy toads lie in their path to trouble the treacherous feet that trample you with usurping steps. Grow stinging nettles for my enemies; and when they pick a flower from your breast, guard it, I beg you, with a hidden adder, whose forked tongue can throw death on your king's enemies with a deadly touch. Don't laugh at my appeal to things that can't feel, lords. This earth will come to have feelings, and these stones will turn into armed soldiers, before her rightful king is brought down by the weapons of foul rebellion."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["Fear not, my lord; that Power that made you king","Hath power to keep you king in spite of all.","The means that heaven yields must be embrac'd","And not neglected; else, if heaven would,","And we will not, heaven's offer we refuse,","The proffer'd means of succour and redress."],"id":322,"m":"Don't be afraid, my lord. The Power that made you king has the power to keep you king in spite of everything. But we must take hold of the means heaven gives us and not neglect them. Otherwise, if heaven is willing and we are not, we refuse what heaven offers, the means of help and rescue it holds out to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["He means, my lord, that we are too remiss;","Whilst Bolingbroke, through our security,","Grows strong and great in substance and in friends."],"id":323,"m":"He means, my lord, that we are too slack, while Bolingbroke grows strong and great in resources and friends because we feel too safe."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Discomfortable cousin! know'st thou not","That when the searching eye of heaven is hid,","Behind the globe, that lights the lower world,","Then thieves and robbers range abroad unseen","In murders and in outrage boldly here;","But when from under this terrestrial ball","He fires the proud tops of the eastern pines","And darts his light through every guilty hole,","Then murders, treasons, and detested sins,","The cloak of night being pluck'd from off their backs,","Stand bare and naked, trembling at themselves?","So when this thief, this traitor, Bolingbroke,","Who all this while hath revell'd in the night,","Whilst we were wandering with the Antipodes,","Shall see us rising in our throne, the east,","His treasons will sit blushing in his face,","Not able to endure the sight of day,","But self-affrighted tremble at his sin.","Not all the water in the rough rude sea","Can wash the balm off from an anointed king;","The breath of worldly men cannot depose","The deputy elected by the Lord.","For every man that Bolingbroke hath press'd","To lift shrewd steel against our golden crown,","God for his Richard hath in heavenly pay","A glorious angel: then, if angels fight,","Weak men must fall, for heaven still guards the right."],"id":324,"m":"Gloomy cousin! Don't you know that when the searching eye of heaven, the sun, is hidden behind the earth and lights the world below, thieves and robbers wander about unseen, boldly committing murder and outrage here? But when he rises from under the earth, sets fire to the proud tops of the eastern pine trees and shoots his light into every guilty hole, then murders, treasons and hateful sins stand bare and naked, with the cloak of night pulled off their backs, trembling at themselves. So when this thief, this traitor Bolingbroke, who has been partying in the night all this time while we were wandering on the other side of the world, sees us rising on our throne in the east, his treasons will blush in his face. He will not be able to bear the sight of day, but will tremble, terrified, at his own sin. Not all the water in the rough, wild sea can wash the holy oil off an anointed king. The breath of mere earthly men cannot depose the deputy chosen by the Lord. For every man Bolingbroke has forced to raise his sharp steel against our golden crown, God has a glorious angel in heaven's pay for his Richard. Then if angels fight, weak men must fall, because heaven always guards the right."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SALISBURY","id":325},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome, my lord. How far off lies your power?"],"id":326,"m":"Welcome, my lord. How far away is your army?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SALISBURY","lines":["Nor near nor farther off, my gracious lord,","Than this weak arm: discomfort guides my tongue","And bids me speak of nothing but despair.","One day too late, I fear me, noble lord,","Hath clouded all thy happy days on earth.","O! call back yesterday, bid time return,","And thou shalt have twelve thousand fighting men!","To-day, to-day, unhappy day, too late,","O'erthrows thy joys, friends, fortune, and thy state;","For all the Welshmen, hearing thou wert dead,","Are gone to Bolingbroke, dispers'd, and fled."],"id":327,"m":"No nearer and no further, my gracious lord, than this weak arm. Despair guides my tongue and tells me to speak of nothing but hopelessness. I fear, noble lord, that one day's delay has clouded all your happy days on earth. Oh, call back yesterday, tell time to turn back, and you will have twelve thousand fighting men! Today, today, this unlucky day, too late, overthrows your joys, friends, fortune and power; because all the Welshmen, hearing you were dead, have gone over to Bolingbroke, or scattered and run away."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Comfort, my liege! why looks your Grace so pale?"],"id":328,"m":"Courage, my lord! Why does your Grace look so pale?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["But now, the blood of twenty thousand men","Did triumph in my face, and they are fled;","And till so much blood thither come again","Have I not reason to look pale and dead?","All souls that will be safe, fly from my side;","For time hath set a blot upon my pride."],"id":329,"m":"Just now the blood of twenty thousand men glowed triumphantly in my face, and now they have run away. Until that much blood comes back there, don't I have good reason to look pale and dead? Let everyone who wants to be safe run from my side, because time has stained my pride."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Comfort, my liege! remember who you are."],"id":330,"m":"Courage, my lord! Remember who you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I had forgot myself. Am I not king?","Awake, thou coward majesty! thou sleepest.","Is not the king's name twenty thousand names?","Arm, arm, my name! a puny subject strikes","At thy great glory. Look not to the ground,","Ye favourites of a king; are we not high?","High be our thoughts. I know my uncle York","Hath power enough to serve our turn. But who comes here?"],"id":331,"m":"I had forgotten myself. Am I not king? Wake up, you cowardly majesty! You are asleep. Isn't the king's name worth twenty thousand names? Arm yourself, arm yourself, my name! A puny subject is striking at your great glory. Don't look at the ground, you favourites of a king. Aren't we high? Let our thoughts be high. I know my uncle York has enough troops to do what we need. But who comes here?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SIR STEPHEN SCROOP","id":332},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["More health and happiness betide my liege","Than can my care-tun'd tongue deliver him!"],"id":333,"m":"May more health and happiness come to my lord than my tongue, tuned to sad news, can bring him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Mine ear is open and my heart prepar'd:","The worst is worldly loss thou canst unfold.","Say, is my kingdom lost? Why, 'twas my care,","And what loss is it to be rid of care?","Strives Bolingbroke to be as great as we?","Greater he shall not be: if he serve God","We'll serve him too, and be his fellow so:","Revolt our subjects? That we cannot mend;","They break their faith to God as well as us:","Cry woe, destruction, ruin, loss, decay;","The worst is death, and death will have his day."],"id":334,"m":"My ear is open and my heart is ready. The worst you can tell me is a worldly loss. Tell me, is my kingdom lost? Well, it was my burden of care, and what loss is it to be rid of care? Is Bolingbroke trying to be as great as I am? He shall not be greater. If he serves God, I will serve God too, and so be his equal. Are my subjects rebelling? I can't put that right; they break their promises to God as well as to me. Cry woe, destruction, ruin, loss, decay. The worst is death, and death will have his day."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Glad am I that your highness is so arm'd","To bear the tidings of calamity.","Like an unseasonable stormy day","Which makes the silver rivers drown their shores,","As if the world were all dissolv'd to tears,","So high above his limits swells the rage","Of Bolingbroke, covering your fearful land","With hard bright steel and hearts harder than steel.","White-beards have arm'd their thin and hairless scalps","Against thy majesty; and boys, with women's voices,","Strive to speak big, and clap their female joints","In stiff unwieldy arms against thy crown;","Thy very beadsmen learn to bend their bows","Of double-fatal yew against thy state;","Yea, distaff-women manage rusty bills","Against thy seat: both young and old rebel,","And all goes worse than I have power to tell."],"id":335,"m":"I am glad your highness is so well prepared to hear news of disaster. Like a stormy day out of season, that makes the silver rivers flood their banks as if the world had melted into tears, Bolingbroke's rage swells high above its limits, covering your frightened land with hard, bright steel and hearts harder than steel. White-bearded old men have put helmets on their thin, bald heads to fight your majesty. Boys with high voices try to speak like men and squeeze their soft limbs into stiff, heavy armour against your crown. Even the old men paid to pray for you learn to bend their bows of deadly yew against your rule. Even women who spin wool take up rusty spears against your throne. Young and old rebel, and everything is going worse than I can tell."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Too well, too well thou tell'st a tale so ill.","Where is the Earl of Wiltshire? Where is Bagot?","What is become of Bushy? Where is Green?","That they have let the dangerous enemy","Measure our confines with such peaceful steps?","If we prevail, their heads shall pay for it.","I warrant they have made peace with Bolingbroke."],"id":336,"m":"You tell such a bad story far too well. Where is the Earl of Wiltshire? Where is Bagot? What has happened to Bushy? Where is Green? How have they let the dangerous enemy pace out our country so peacefully? If we win, their heads will pay for it. I bet they have made peace with Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Peace have they made with him indeed, my lord."],"id":337,"m":"They have made peace with him indeed, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["O villains, vipers, damn'd without redemption!","Dogs, easily won to fawn on any man!","Snakes, in my heart-blood warm'd, that sting my heart!","Three Judases, each one thrice worse than Judas!","Would they make peace? Terrible hell make war","Upon their spotted souls for this offence!"],"id":338,"m":"Oh, villains, snakes, damned beyond saving! Dogs, easily won over to fawn on anyone! Snakes, warmed in my own heart's blood, who sting my heart! Three Judases, each one three times worse than Judas! Would they make peace? May terrible hell make war on their stained souls for this crime!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Sweet love, I see, changing his property,","Turns to the sourest and most deadly hate.","Again uncurse their souls; their peace is made","With heads, and not with hands: those whom you curse","Have felt the worst of death's destroying wound","And lie full low, grav'd in the hollow ground."],"id":339,"m":"I see sweet love, when it changes its nature, turns into the sourest and deadliest hate. Take back your curses on their souls. They made their peace with their heads, not their hands. The men you curse have felt the worst of death's destroying wound and lie low, buried in the hollow ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Is Bushy, Green, and the Earl of Wiltshire dead?"],"id":340,"m":"Are Bushy, Green and the Earl of Wiltshire dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Ay, all of them at Bristol lost their heads."],"id":341,"m":"Yes, they all lost their heads at Bristol."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Where is the Duke my father with his power?"],"id":342,"m":"Where is the Duke my father with his army?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["No matter where. Of comfort no man speak:","Let's talk of graves, of worms, and epitaphs;","Make dust our paper, and with rainy eyes","Write sorrow on the bosom of the earth.","Let's choose executors and talk of wills;","And yet not so—for what can we bequeath","Save our deposed bodies to the ground?","Our lands, our lives, and all are Bolingbroke's.","And nothing can we call our own but death,","And that small model of the barren earth","Which serves as paste and cover to our bones.","For God's sake let us sit upon the ground","And tell sad stories of the death of kings:","How some have been deposed, some slain in war,","Some haunted by the ghosts they have depos'd,","Some poison'd by their wives, some sleeping kill'd;","All murder'd: for within the hollow crown","That rounds the mortal temples of a king","Keeps Death his court; and there the antick sits,","Scoffing his state and grinning at his pomp;","Allowing him a breath, a little scene,","To monarchize, be fear'd, and kill with looks,","Infusing him with self and vain conceit","As if this flesh which walls about our life","Were brass impregnable; and, humour'd thus,","Comes at the last, and with a little pin","Bores through his castle wall, and farewell, king!","Cover your heads, and mock not flesh and blood","With solemn reverence: throw away respect,","Tradition, form, and ceremonious duty;","For you have but mistook me all this while:","I live with bread like you, feel want,","Taste grief, need friends: subjected thus,","How can you say to me I am a king?"],"id":343,"m":"It doesn't matter where. Let nobody speak of comfort. Let's talk about graves, worms and epitaphs. Let's make the dust our paper and write sorrow on the breast of the earth with our rainy eyes. Let's choose executors and talk about wills. And yet, no: what can we leave except our deposed bodies to the ground? Our lands, our lives and everything belong to Bolingbroke, and we can call nothing our own except death and that small patch of barren earth that covers our bones like pastry over a pie. For God's sake, let's sit on the ground and tell sad stories about the deaths of kings: how some have been deposed, some killed in war, some haunted by the ghosts of those they deposed, some poisoned by their wives, some killed in their sleep. All murdered. Because inside the hollow crown that circles a king's mortal head, Death holds his court. There the clown sits, mocking the king's greatness and grinning at his ceremony, allowing him a breath, a short scene, to play the monarch, be feared and kill with a look, filling him with selfish, empty ideas, as if the flesh that walls in our life were unbreakable brass. And when he has amused himself like this, Death comes at last, and with a little pin he bores through the castle wall, and goodbye, king! Put your hats back on, and don't mock flesh and blood with solemn respect. Throw away respect, tradition, ceremony and formal duty, because you have misunderstood me all this time. I live on bread like you, feel need, taste grief, need friends. Since I am a subject to all these, how can you tell me I am a king?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["My lord, wise men ne'er sit and wail their woes,","But presently prevent the ways to wail.","To fear the foe, since fear oppresseth strength,","Gives, in your weakness, strength unto your foe,","And so your follies fight against yourself.","Fear and be slain; no worse can come to fight;","And fight and die is death destroying death;","Where fearing dying pays death servile breath."],"id":344,"m":"My lord, wise men never sit and moan about their troubles, but act at once to stop the causes of moaning. Fearing the enemy, since fear weakens strength, gives your enemy strength from your weakness, and so your foolishness fights against you. Be afraid, and you will be killed; nothing worse can come from fighting. To fight and die is to destroy death by death, while to die fearing death is to pay death the breath of a slave."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["My father hath a power; inquire of him,","And learn to make a body of a limb."],"id":345,"m":"My father has an army. Ask about him, and learn to make a whole body out of one limb."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thou chid'st me well. Proud Bolingbroke, I come","To change blows with thee for our day of doom.","This ague fit of fear is over-blown;","An easy task it is to win our own.—","Say, Scroop, where lies our uncle with his power?","Speak sweetly, man, although thy looks be sour."],"id":346,"m":"You scold me rightly. Proud Bolingbroke, I am coming to exchange blows with you and settle our fate. This shivering fit of fear has passed. It is an easy task to win back what is ours. Tell me, Scroop, where is our uncle with his army? Speak sweetly, man, even though you look sour."},{"t":"sp","who":"SCROOP","lines":["Men judge by the complexion of the sky","The state in inclination of the day;","So may you by my dull and heavy eye,","My tongue hath but a heavier tale to say.","I play the torturer, by small and small","To lengthen out the worst that must be spoken:","Your uncle York is join'd with Bolingbroke;","And all your northern castles yielded up,","And all your southern gentlemen in arms","Upon his party."],"id":347,"m":"Men judge what kind of day it will be by the colour of the sky. So you can judge by my dull, heavy eyes that my tongue only has a heavier story to tell. I am playing the torturer, dragging out bit by bit the worst news, which must be told. Your uncle York has joined Bolingbroke, all your northern castles have surrendered and all your southern gentlemen have taken up arms on his side."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thou hast said enough.","[To AUMERLE] Beshrew thee, cousin, which didst lead me forth","Of that sweet way I was in to despair!","What say you now? What comfort have we now?","By heaven, I'll hate him everlastingly","That bids me be of comfort any more.","Go to Flint Castle; there I'll pine away;","A king, woe's slave, shall kingly woe obey.","That power I have, discharge; and let them go","To ear the land that hath some hope to grow,","For I have none. Let no man speak again","To alter this, for counsel is but vain."],"id":348,"m":"You have said enough. Curse you, cousin, for leading me away from the sweet road to despair I was on! What do you say now? What comfort do we have now? By heaven, I will hate for ever anyone who tells me to take comfort again. Go to Flint Castle; I'll waste away there. A king who is sorrow's slave will obey royal sorrow. Dismiss the army I have, and let them go and plough land that has some hope of growing something, because I have none. Let no one speak again to change this, because advice is useless."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["My liege, one word."],"id":349,"m":"My lord, one word."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["He does me double wrong","That wounds me with the flatteries of his tongue.","Discharge my followers; let them hence away,","From Richard's night to Bolingbroke's fair day."],"id":350,"m":"Anyone who wounds me with the flattery of his tongue does me a double wrong. Dismiss my followers. Let them go away from Richard's night to Bolingbroke's bright day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":351},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Wales. Before Flint Castle.","id":352},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, with drum and colours, BOLINGBROKE and Forces; YORK, NORTHUMBERLAND, and Others","id":353},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["So that by this intelligence we learn","The Welshmen are dispers'd; and Salisbury","Is gone to meet the king, who lately landed","With some few private friends upon this coast."],"id":354,"m":"So from this report we learn that the Welshmen have scattered, and Salisbury has gone to meet the king, who landed recently on this coast with a few close friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["The news is very fair and good, my lord.","Richard not far from hence hath hid his head."],"id":355,"m":"The news is very good, my lord. Richard has hidden his head not far from here."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["It would beseem the Lord Northumberland","To say 'King Richard': alack the heavy day","When such a sacred king should hide his head!"],"id":356,"m":"It would be more fitting for Lord Northumberland to say 'King Richard'. Alas, what a sad day, when such a sacred king has to hide his head!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Your Grace mistakes; only to be brief,","Left I his title out."],"id":357,"m":"Your Grace misunderstands. I left out his title only to be brief."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["The time hath been,","Would you have been so brief with him, he would","Have been so brief with you to shorten you,","For taking so the head, your whole head's length."],"id":358,"m":"There was a time when, if you had been so brief with him, he would have been brief with you and shortened you by a whole head's length for taking such liberties."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Mistake not, uncle, further than you should."],"id":359,"m":"Don't take this the wrong way, uncle, any more than you should."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Take not, good cousin, further than you should,","Lest you mistake. The heavens are o'er our heads."],"id":360,"m":"And don't you take more than you should, good nephew, or you may take the wrong way. The heavens are above our heads."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I know it, uncle; and oppose not myself","Against their will. But who comes here?"],"id":361,"m":"I know it, uncle, and I don't set myself against their will. But who is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HENRY PERCY","id":362},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome, Harry: what, will not this castle yield?"],"id":363,"m":"Welcome, Harry. What, won't this castle surrender?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["The castle royally is mann'd, my lord,","Against thy entrance."],"id":364,"m":"The castle is royally defended against you, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Royally!","Why, it contains no king?"],"id":365,"m":"Royally! Why, there's no king in it, is there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["Yes, my good lord,","It doth contain a king; King Richard lies","Within the limits of yon lime and stone;","And with him are the Lord Aumerle, Lord Salisbury,","Sir Stephen Scroop, besides a clergyman","Of holy reverence; who, I cannot learn."],"id":366,"m":"Yes, my good lord, there is a king inside. King Richard is within those walls of lime and stone over there, and with him are Lord Aumerle, Lord Salisbury, Sir Stephen Scroop and a holy clergyman; I can't find out who."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["O! belike it is the Bishop of Carlisle."],"id":367,"m":"Oh, it's probably the Bishop of Carlisle."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["[To NORTHUMBERLAND] Noble lord,","Go to the rude ribs of that ancient castle;","Through brazen trumpet send the breath of parley","Into his ruin'd ears, and thus deliver:","Henry Bolingbroke","On both his knees doth kiss King Richard's hand,","And sends allegiance and true faith of heart","To his most royal person; hither come","Even at his feet to lay my arms and power,","Provided that my banishment repeal'd","And lands restor'd again be freely granted;","If not, I'll use the advantage of my power","And lay the summer's dust with showers of blood","Rain'd from the wounds of slaughtered Englishmen;","The which, how far off from the mind of Bolingbroke","It is, such crimson tempest should bedrench","The fresh green lap of fair King Richard's land,","My stooping duty tenderly shall show.","Go, signify as much, while here we march","Upon the grassy carpet of this plain.","Let's march without the noise of threat'ning drum,","That from this castle's totter'd battlements","Our fair appointments may be well perus'd.","Methinks King Richard and myself should meet","With no less terror than the elements","Of fire and water, when their thund'ring shock","At meeting tears the cloudy cheeks of heaven.","Be he the fire, I'll be the yielding water;","The rage be his, whilst on the earth I rain","My waters; on the earth, and not on him.","March on, and mark King Richard how he looks."],"id":368,"m":"Noble lord, go to the rough walls of that ancient castle. Send the breath of a request to talk through a brass trumpet into its broken ears, and deliver this message: Henry Bolingbroke kisses King Richard's hand on both knees and sends loyalty and true faith of heart to his most royal person. I have come here to lay my weapons and my army at his feet, on condition that my banishment is cancelled and my lands are given back freely. If not, I will use the advantage of my strength and settle the summer's dust with showers of blood rained from the wounds of slaughtered Englishmen. My humble kneeling will gently show how far it is from Bolingbroke's mind that such a crimson storm should soak the fresh green lap of fair King Richard's land. Go and tell him this, while we march here on the grassy carpet of this plain. Let's march without the threatening noise of drums, so that our fine equipment can be clearly seen from the castle's crumbling walls. I think King Richard and I should meet with no less terror than the elements of fire and water, when their thundering clash tears the cloudy cheeks of the sky. Let him be the fire, and I'll be the water that gives way. Let the rage be his, while I rain my waters on the earth; on the earth, not on him. March on, and watch how King Richard looks."},{"t":"sd","text":"A Parley sounded, and answered by a Trumpet within. Flourish. Enter on the Walls, the KING, the BISHOP OF CARLISLE, AUMERLE, SCROOP, and SALISBURY","id":369},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["See, see, King Richard doth himself appear,","As doth the blushing discontented sun","From out the fiery portal of the east,","When he perceives the envious clouds are bent","To dim his glory and to stain the track","Of his bright passage to the occident."],"id":370,"m":"Look, look, King Richard appears himself, like the blushing, unhappy sun coming out of the fiery gateway of the east, when he sees that jealous clouds are set on dimming his glory and staining the path of his bright journey to the west."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Yet he looks like a king: behold, his eye,","As bright as is the eagle's, lightens forth","Controlling majesty: alack, alack, for woe,","That any harm should stain so fair a show!"],"id":371,"m":"And yet he looks like a king. Look, his eye, as bright as an eagle's, flashes out commanding majesty. Alas, alas, how sad that any harm should stain such a fine sight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["[To NORTHUMBERLAND] We are amaz'd; and thus long have we stood","To watch the fearful bending of thy knee,","Because we thought ourself thy lawful king;","And if we be, how dare thy joints forget","To pay their awful duty to our presence?","If we be not, show us the hand of God","That hath dismiss'd us from our stewardship;","For well we know no hand of blood and bone","Can gripe the sacred handle of our sceptre,","Unless he do profane, steal, or usurp.","And though you think that all, as you have done,","Have torn their souls by turning them from us,","And we are barren and bereft of friends,","Yet know, my master, God omnipotent,","Is mustering in his clouds on our behalf","Armies of pestilence; and they shall strike","Your children yet unborn and unbegot,","That lift your vassal hands against my head","And threat the glory of my precious crown.","Tell Bolingbroke,—for yond methinks he stands,—","That every stride he makes upon my land","Is dangerous treason; he is come to open","The purple testament of bleeding war;","But ere the crown he looks for live in peace,","Ten thousand bloody crowns of mothers' sons","Shall ill become the flower of England's face,","Change the complexion of her maid-pale peace","To scarlet indignation, and bedew","Her pastures' grass with faithful English blood."],"id":372,"m":"I am astonished. I have stood here this long waiting to see you bend your knee in fear, because I thought I was your lawful king. And if I am, how dare your joints forget to pay their respectful duty to my presence? If I am not, show me the hand of God that has dismissed me from my office, because I know well that no hand of flesh and blood can grip the sacred handle of my sceptre without committing sacrilege, theft or usurpation. And though you think that everyone has torn their souls, as you have, by turning them away from me, and that I am left bare and without friends, know this: my master, almighty God, is gathering armies of plague in his clouds on my behalf. They will strike your children, not yet born or even conceived, because you lift your servants' hands against my head and threaten the glory of my precious crown. Tell Bolingbroke, for I think that's him standing there, that every step he takes on my land is dangerous treason. He has come to open the blood-red will of bleeding war. But before the crown he is after can live in peace, ten thousand bloody heads of mothers' sons will spoil the flower of England's face, change the pale, maiden colour of her peace to scarlet anger, and wet the grass of her fields with loyal English blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["The King of Heaven forbid our lord the King","Should so with civil and uncivil arms","Be rush'd upon! Thy thrice noble cousin,","Harry Bolingbroke, doth humbly kiss thy hand;","And by the honourable tomb he swears","That stands upon your royal grandsire's bones,","And by the royalties of both your bloods,","Currents that spring from one most gracious head,","And by the buried hand of warlike Gaunt,","And by the worth and honour of himself,","Comprising all that may be sworn or said,","His coming hither hath no further scope","Than for his lineal royalties, and to beg","Enfranchisement immediate on his knees;","Which on thy royal party granted once,","His glittering arms he will commend to rust,","His barbed steeds to stables, and his heart","To faithful service of your Majesty.","This swears he, as he is a prince, is just;","And as I am a gentleman I credit him."],"id":373,"m":"The King of Heaven forbid that our lord the King should be attacked like this with weapons of civil war! Your thrice-noble cousin, Harry Bolingbroke, humbly kisses your hand. He swears by the honourable tomb that stands over your royal grandfather's bones, by the royal blood of you both, two streams springing from one most gracious source, by the buried hand of warlike Gaunt, and by his own worth and honour, which covers everything that can be sworn or said, that his only purpose in coming here is to claim his inherited titles and to beg on his knees to have his rights restored at once. Once your Majesty grants that, he will leave his glittering weapons to rust, his armoured horses to their stables and his heart to faithful service of your Majesty. He swears this is fair, on his word as a prince; and on my word as a gentleman, I believe him."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Northumberland, say, thus the king returns:","His noble cousin is right welcome hither;","And all the number of his fair demands","Shall be accomplish'd without contradiction.","With all the gracious utterance thou hast","Speak to his gentle hearing kind commends."],"id":374,"m":"Northumberland, tell him that this is the king's reply: his noble cousin is very welcome here, and all his reasonable demands will be met without argument. With all the courteous words you have, give him my kind regards."},{"t":"sd","text":"NORTHUMBERLAND retires to BOLINGBROKE","id":375},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["[To AUMERLE] We do debase ourselves, cousin, do we not,","To look so poorly and to speak so fair?","Shall we call back Northumberland, and send","Defiance to the traitor, and so die?"],"id":376,"m":"Cousin, aren't we lowering ourselves by looking so poor and speaking so politely? Shall we call Northumberland back, send defiance to the traitor and so die?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["No, good my lord; let's fight with gentle words","Till time lend friends, and friends their helpful swords."],"id":377,"m":"No, my good lord. Let's fight with gentle words until time gives us friends, and friends give us their helpful swords."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["O God, O God! that e'er this tongue of mine","That laid the sentence of dread banishment","On yond proud man should take it off again","With words of sooth! O! that I were as great","As is my grief, or lesser than my name,","Or that I could forget what I have been,","Or not remember what I must be now.","Swell'st thou, proud heart? I'll give thee scope to beat,","Since foes have scope to beat both thee and me."],"id":378,"m":"O God, O God, that this tongue of mine, which passed the terrible sentence of exile on that proud man over there, should ever take it off again with flattering words! Oh, I wish I were as great as my grief, or less than my title, or that I could forget what I have been, or not remember what I must be now! Are you swelling, proud heart? I'll give you room to beat, since my enemies have room to beat both you and me."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Northumberland comes back from Bolingbroke."],"id":379,"m":"Northumberland is coming back from Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["What must the King do now? Must he submit?","The king shall do it: must he be depos'd?","The king shall be contented: must he lose","The name of king? A God's name, let it go:","I'll give my jewels for a set of beads,","My gorgeous palace for a hermitage,","My gay apparel for an almsman's gown,","My figur'd goblets for a dish of wood,","My sceptre for a palmer's walking-staff,","My subjects for a pair of carved saints,","And my large kingdom for a little grave,","A little little grave, an obscure grave;","Or I'll be buried in the king's highway,","Some way of common trade, where subjects' feet","May hourly trample on their sovereign's head;","For on my heart they tread now whilst I live;","And buried once, why not upon my head?","Aumerle, thou weep'st, my tender-hearted cousin!","We'll make foul weather with despised tears;","Our sighs and they shall lodge the summer corn","And make a dearth in this revolting land.","Or shall we play the wantons with our woes","And make some pretty match with shedding tears?","As thus: to drop them still upon one place","Till they have fretted us a pair of graves","Within the earth; and, there inlaid: 'There lies","Two kinsmen digg'd their graves with weeping eyes.'","Would not this ill do well? Well, well, I see","I talk but idly, and you laugh at me.","Most mighty prince, my Lord Northumberland,","What says King Bolingbroke? will his Majesty","Give Richard leave to live till Richard die?","You make a leg, and Bolingbroke says ay."],"id":380,"m":"What must the King do now? Must he give in? The king will do it. Must he be deposed? The king will be content. Must he lose the name of king? In God's name, let it go. I'll give my jewels for a string of prayer beads, my splendid palace for a hermit's cell, my fine clothes for a beggar's gown, my engraved goblets for a wooden bowl, my sceptre for a pilgrim's walking stick, my subjects for a pair of carved saints, and my large kingdom for a little grave, a little, little grave, an unknown grave. Or I'll be buried in the king's highway, some busy road, where subjects' feet can trample on their king's head every hour; because they tread on my heart now while I'm alive, so once I'm buried, why not on my head? Aumerle, you're crying, my tender-hearted cousin! We'll make bad weather with our despised tears. Our sighs and tears together will flatten the summer corn and cause a famine in this rebellious country. Or shall we play games with our sorrows and have a pretty contest in shedding tears? Like this: we drop them on the same spot until they have worn a pair of graves for us in the earth, with this carved there: 'Here lie two relatives who dug their graves with weeping eyes.' Wouldn't this misery work well? Well, well, I see I'm just talking nonsense, and you are laughing at me. Most mighty prince, my Lord Northumberland, what does King Bolingbroke say? Will his Majesty give Richard permission to live until Richard dies? You bow, and that means Bolingbroke says yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My lord, in the base court he doth attend","To speak with you; may it please you to come down?"],"id":381,"m":"My lord, he is waiting in the lower courtyard to speak with you. Would you be pleased to come down?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Down, down I come; like glist'ring Phaethon,","Wanting the manage of unruly jades.","In the base court? Base court, where kings grow base,","To come at traitors' calls, and do them grace.","In the base court? Come down? Down, court! down, king!","For night-owls shriek where mounting larks should sing."],"id":382,"m":"Down, down I come, like glittering Phaethon, unable to control his wild horses. In the lower courtyard? The low court, where kings become low, coming when traitors call and doing them honour. In the lower courtyard? Come down? Down, court! Down, king! For night owls shriek where rising larks should sing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt from above","id":383},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["What says his Majesty?"],"id":384,"m":"What does his Majesty say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Sorrow and grief of heart","Makes him speak fondly, like a frantic man;","Yet he is come."],"id":385,"m":"Sorrow and grief of heart make him talk foolishly, like a madman. But he is coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD, and his attendants","id":386},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Stand all apart,","And show fair duty to his Majesty. [Kneeling]","My gracious lord,—"],"id":387,"m":"Everyone stand back and show proper respect to his Majesty. My gracious lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Fair cousin, you debase your princely knee","To make the base earth proud with kissing it:","Me rather had my heart might feel your love","Than my unpleas'd eye see your courtesy.","Up, cousin, up; your heart is up, I know,","Thus high at least, although your knee be low."],"id":388,"m":"Fair cousin, you lower your princely knee by making the low earth proud by kissing it. I would rather my heart could feel your love than my displeased eye see your politeness. Up, cousin, up. I know your heart is up, at least this high, though your knee is low."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["My gracious lord, I come but for mine own."],"id":389,"m":"My gracious lord, I have come only for what is mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Your own is yours, and I am yours, and all."],"id":390,"m":"What is yours is yours, and I am yours, and everything is."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["So far be mine, my most redoubted lord,","As my true service shall deserve your love."],"id":391,"m":"Let me have only as much, my most feared lord, as my true service deserves from your love."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Well you deserve: they well deserve to have","That know the strong'st and surest way to get.","Uncle, give me your hand: nay, dry your eyes:","Tears show their love, but want their remedies.","Cousin, I am too young to be your father,","Though you are old enough to be my heir.","What you will have, I'll give, and willing too;","For do we must what force will have us do.","Set on towards London. Cousin, is it so?"],"id":392,"m":"You deserve it well. Those who know the strongest and surest way to get things deserve to have them. Uncle, give me your hand. No, dry your eyes. Tears show love, but they can't cure anything. Cousin, I am too young to be your father, though you are old enough to be my heir. What you want, I will give you, and willingly too, because we must do what force makes us do. Set off towards London. Cousin, is that right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Yea, my good lord."],"id":393,"m":"Yes, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Then I must not say no."],"id":394,"m":"Then I must not say no."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Exeunt","id":395},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Langley. The DUKE OF YORK's garden.","id":396},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the QUEEN and two Ladies","id":397},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What sport shall we devise here in this garden","To drive away the heavy thought of care?"],"id":398,"m":"What game can we think up here in this garden to drive away heavy, worried thoughts?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Madam, we'll play at bowls."],"id":399,"m":"Madam, let's play bowls."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["'Twill make me think the world is full of rubs","And that my fortune runs against the bias."],"id":400,"m":"It will make me think the world is full of obstacles, and my fortune runs off course."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Madam, we'll dance."],"id":401,"m":"Madam, let's dance."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["My legs can keep no measure in delight,","When my poor heart no measure keeps in grief:","Therefore no dancing, girl; some other sport."],"id":402,"m":"My legs can't keep time with delight when my poor heart keeps no limit in its grief. So no dancing, girl. Some other game."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Madam, we'll tell tales."],"id":403,"m":"Madam, let's tell stories."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Of sorrow or of joy?"],"id":404,"m":"About sorrow or joy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Of either, madam."],"id":405,"m":"Either, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Of neither, girl:","For if of joy, being altogether wanting,","It doth remember me the more of sorrow;","Or if of grief, being altogether had,","It adds more sorrow to my want of joy;","For what I have I need not to repeat,","And what I want it boots not to complain."],"id":406,"m":"Neither, girl. If they are about joy, which I have none of, they will only remind me more of sorrow. If they are about grief, which I have plenty of, they will add more sorrow to my lack of joy. What I have, I don't need to repeat; and what I lack, it does no good to complain about."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["Madam, I'll sing."],"id":407,"m":"Madam, I'll sing."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["'Tis well that thou hast cause;","But thou shouldst please me better wouldst thou weep."],"id":408,"m":"It's good that you have reason to; but you would please me more if you would cry."},{"t":"sp","who":"LADY","lines":["I could weep, madam, would it do you good."],"id":409,"m":"I could cry, madam, if it would do you good."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And I could sing, would weeping do me good,","And never borrow any tear of thee.","But stay, here come the gardeners.","Let's step into the shadow of these trees.","My wretchedness unto a row of pins,","They will talk of state, for every one doth so","Against a change: woe is forerun with woe."],"id":410,"m":"And I could sing, if crying would do me any good, and never need to borrow a tear from you. But wait, here come the gardeners. Let's step into the shade of these trees. I'll bet my misery against a row of pins that they'll talk about politics; everyone does when a change is coming. Sorrow is always announced by sorrow."},{"t":"sd","text":"QUEEN and Ladies retire","id":411},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Gardener and two Servants","id":412},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["Go, bind thou up yon dangling apricocks,","Which, like unruly children, make their sire","Stoop with oppression of their prodigal weight:","Give some supportance to the bending twigs.","Go thou, and like an executioner","Cut off the heads of too fast growing sprays","That look too lofty in our commonwealth:","All must be even in our government.","You thus employ'd, I will go root away","The noisome weeds which without profit suck","The soil's fertility from wholesome flowers."],"id":413,"m":"You, go and tie up those dangling apricots over there, which, like unruly children, make their father bend under their heavy, wasteful weight. Give some support to the bending branches. You go, and like an executioner cut off the heads of the shoots that grow too fast and look too high in our little state. Everything must be level in our government. While you do that, I will go and dig up the harmful weeds that uselessly suck the soil's goodness away from the healthy flowers."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Why should we in the compass of a pale","Keep law and form and due proportion,","Showing, as in a model, our firm estate,","When our sea-walled garden, the whole land,","Is full of weeds; her fairest flowers chok'd up,","Her fruit trees all unprun'd, her hedges ruin'd,","Her knots disordered, and her wholesome herbs","Swarming with caterpillars?"],"id":414,"m":"Why should we keep law, order and proper balance inside the fence of this garden, showing a model of a well-run state, when our sea-walled garden, the whole country, is full of weeds, her finest flowers choked, her fruit trees unpruned, her hedges ruined, her flower beds in a mess and her healthy herbs crawling with caterpillars?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["Hold thy peace.","He that hath suffer'd this disorder'd spring","Hath now himself met with the fall of leaf;","The weeds which his broad-spreading leaves did shelter,","That seem'd in eating him to hold him up,","Are pluck'd up root and all by Bolingbroke;","I mean the Earl of Wiltshire, Bushy, Green."],"id":415,"m":"Be quiet. The man who allowed this disordered spring has now met his own autumn. The weeds that his wide leaves sheltered, which seemed to hold him up while they were eating him, have been pulled up, roots and all, by Bolingbroke. I mean the Earl of Wiltshire, Bushy and Green."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["What! are they dead?"],"id":416,"m":"What! Are they dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["They are; and Bolingbroke","Hath seiz'd the wasteful King. O! what pity is it","That he had not so trimm'd and dress'd his land","As we this garden! We at time of year","Do wound the bark, the skin of our fruit trees,","Lest, being over-proud in sap and blood,","With too much riches it confound itself:","Had he done so to great and growing men,","They might have liv'd to bear, and he to taste","Their fruits of duty: superfluous branches","We lop away, that bearing boughs may live:","Had he done so, himself had borne the crown,","Which waste of idle hours hath quite thrown down."],"id":417,"m":"They are, and Bolingbroke has captured the wasteful King. Oh, what a pity it is that he didn't trim and tend his land as we do this garden! At the right time of year we cut the bark, the skin of our fruit trees, in case they get too full of sap and blood and destroy themselves with too much richness. If he had done that to great men who were growing too powerful, they might have lived to bear fruit, and he to taste their fruits of loyalty. We cut off unnecessary branches so that fruit-bearing boughs can live. If he had done that, he would still be wearing the crown, which the waste of idle hours has completely thrown down."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["What! think you the king shall be depos'd?"],"id":418,"m":"What! Do you think the king will be deposed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["Depress'd he is already, and depos'd","'Tis doubt he will be: letters came last night","To a dear friend of the good Duke of York's","That tell black tidings."],"id":419,"m":"He's brought low already, and it's likely he will be deposed. Letters came last night to a close friend of the good Duke of York with dark news."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["O! I am press'd to death through want of speaking!"],"id":420,"m":"Oh, I am being crushed to death because I can't speak!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Coming forward","id":421},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["Thou, old Adam's likeness, set to dress this garden,","How dares thy harsh rude tongue sound this unpleasing news?","What Eve, what serpent, hath suggested thee","To make a second fall of cursed man?","Why dost thou say King Richard is depos'd?","Dar'st thou, thou little better thing than earth,","Divine his downfall? Say, where, when, and how,","Cam'st thou by this ill tidings? Speak, thou wretch."],"id":422,"m":"You, made in the image of old Adam, put here to look after this garden, how dare your harsh, rough tongue tell this unpleasant news? What Eve, what serpent, has tempted you to make cursed mankind fall a second time? Why do you say King Richard is deposed? Do you dare, you thing little better than dirt, to foretell his downfall? Tell me, where, when and how did you hear this bad news? Speak, you wretch."},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["Pardon me, madam: little joy have I","To breathe this news; yet what I say is true.","King Richard, he is in the mighty hold","Of Bolingbroke: their fortunes both are weigh'd.","In your lord's scale is nothing but himself,","And some few vanities that make him light;","But in the balance of great Bolingbroke,","Besides himself, are all the English peers,","And with that odds he weighs King Richard down.","Post you to London, and you will find it so;","I speak no more than every one doth know."],"id":423,"m":"Forgive me, madam. I take little pleasure in telling this news, but what I say is true. King Richard is in Bolingbroke's powerful grip. Their fortunes have been weighed. In your husband's scale there is nothing but himself and a few vanities that make him light. But in great Bolingbroke's scale, besides himself, there are all the English lords, and with that advantage he weighs King Richard down. Hurry to London and you will find it is true. I'm saying no more than everyone knows."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Nimble mischance, that art so light of foot,","Doth not thy embassage belong to me,","And am I last that knows it? O! thou thinkest","To serve me last, that I may longest keep","Thy sorrow in my breast. Come, ladies, go,","To meet at London London's king in woe.","What was I born to this, that my sad look","Should grace the triumph of great Bolingbroke?","Gardener, for telling me these news of woe,","Pray God the plants thou graft'st may never grow!"],"id":424,"m":"Quick misfortune, so light on your feet, isn't your message meant for me, and am I the last to know it? Oh, you think you'll serve me last so that I keep your sorrow in my heart the longest. Come, ladies, let's go and meet London's king, sorrowful in London. Was I born for this, so that my sad face should decorate great Bolingbroke's triumph? Gardener, for telling me this sad news, I pray God that the plants you graft may never grow!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt QUEEN and Ladies","id":425},{"t":"sp","who":"GARDENER","lines":["Poor Queen, so that thy state might be no worse,","I would my skill were subject to thy curse.","Here did she fall a tear; here in this place","I'll set a bank of rue, sour herb of grace.","Rue, even for ruth, here shortly shall be seen,","In the remembrance of a weeping queen."],"id":426,"m":"Poor Queen, if it would keep your situation from getting worse, I'd be happy for my skill to suffer your curse. Here she dropped a tear. Here in this place I'll plant a bed of rue, the bitter herb of grace. Rue, standing for pity, will soon be seen here in memory of a weeping queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":427},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Westminster Hall.","id":428},{"t":"sd","text":"The Lords spiritual on the right side of the throne; the Lords temporal on the left; the Commons below. Enter BOLINGBROKE, AUMERLE, SURREY, NORTHUMBERLAND, HENRY PERCY, FITZWATER, another Lord, the BISHOP OF CARLISLE, the ABBOT OF WESTMINSTER, and attendants. OFFICERS behind, with BAGOT","id":429},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Call forth Bagot.","Now, Bagot, freely speak thy mind;","What thou dost know of noble Gloucester's death;","Who wrought it with the King, and who perform'd","The bloody office of his timeless end."],"id":430,"m":"Bring Bagot forward. Now, Bagot, speak freely. Tell us what you know about noble Gloucester's death: who worked with the King to bring it about, and who carried out the bloody job of killing him before his time."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["Then set before my face the Lord Aumerle."],"id":431,"m":"Then put the Lord Aumerle in front of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Cousin, stand forth, and look upon that man."],"id":432,"m":"Cousin, step forward and look at that man."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAGOT","lines":["My Lord Aumerle, I know your daring tongue","Scorns to unsay what once it hath deliver'd.","In that dead time when Gloucester's death was plotted","I heard you say 'Is not my arm of length,","That reacheth from the restful English Court","As far as Calais, to mine uncle's head?'","Amongst much other talk that very time","I heard you say that you had rather refuse","The offer of an hundred thousand crowns","Than Bolingbroke's return to England;","Adding withal, how blest this land would be","In this your cousin's death."],"id":433,"m":"My Lord Aumerle, I know your bold tongue is too proud to take back what it has once said. At that dark time when Gloucester's death was being plotted, I heard you say, 'Isn't my arm long enough to reach from the peaceful English court as far as Calais, to my uncle's head?' In a lot of other talk at that very time, I heard you say you would rather turn down a hundred thousand crowns than see Bolingbroke come back to England, and you added how blessed this country would be if your cousin died."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Princes, and noble lords,","What answer shall I make to this base man?","Shall I so much dishonour my fair stars","On equal terms to give him chastisement?","Either I must, or have mine honour soil'd","With the attainder of his slanderous lips.","There is my gage, the manual seal of death","That marks thee out for hell: I say thou liest,","And will maintain what thou hast said is false","In thy heart-blood, through being all too base","To stain the temper of my knightly sword."],"id":434,"m":"Princes and noble lords, what answer shall I give this lowborn man? Shall I so dishonour my noble birth as to fight him as an equal? Either I must, or let my honour be stained by the accusation of his slandering lips. There is my glove, the hand-sealed warrant of death that marks you out for hell. I say you lie, and I will prove that what you have said is false with your heart's blood, though it is much too lowly to stain my knightly sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Bagot, forbear; thou shalt not take it up."],"id":435,"m":"Bagot, stop. You shall not pick it up."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Excepting one, I would he were the best","In all this presence that hath mov'd me so."],"id":436,"m":"Except for one man, I wish the one who has provoked me like this were the noblest man here."},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["If that thy valour stand on sympathies,","There is my gage, Aumerle, in gage to thine:","By that fair sun which shows me where thou stand'st,","I heard thee say, and vauntingly thou spak'st it,","That thou wert cause of noble Gloucester's death.","If thou deny'st it twenty times, thou liest;","And I will turn thy falsehood to thy heart,","Where it was forged, with my rapier's point."],"id":437,"m":"If your courage needs an equal in rank, there is my glove, Aumerle, as a challenge to yours. By that bright sun that shows me where you stand, I heard you say, and you said it boastfully, that you caused noble Gloucester's death. If you deny it twenty times, you lie; and I will drive your lie back into your heart, where it was made, with the point of my sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Thou darest not, coward, live to see that day."],"id":438,"m":"You don't dare live to see that day, coward."},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["Now, by my soul, I would it were this hour."],"id":439,"m":"Now, by my soul, I wish it were this very hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Fitzwater, thou art damn'd to hell for this."],"id":440,"m":"Fitzwater, you are damned to hell for this."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["Aumerle, thou liest; his honour is as true","In this appeal as thou art an unjust;","And that thou art so, there I throw my gage,","To prove it on thee to the extremest point","Of mortal breathing: seize it if thou dar'st."],"id":441,"m":"Aumerle, you lie. His honour is as true in this accusation as you are false; and to prove you are, there I throw my glove, to prove it on you to the last breath of life. Pick it up if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["And if I do not, may my hands rot off","And never brandish more revengeful steel","Over the glittering helmet of my foe!"],"id":442,"m":"And if I don't, may my hands rot off and never again swing a revengeful sword over the glittering helmet of my enemy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANOTHER LORD","lines":["I task the earth to the like, forsworn Aumerle;","And spur thee on with full as many lies","As may be halloa'd in thy treacherous ear","From sun to sun: there is my honour's pawn;","Engage it to the trial if thou dar'st."],"id":443,"m":"I load the earth with the same challenge, perjured Aumerle, and spur you on with as many accusations of lying as can be shouted into your treacherous ear from sunrise to sunset. There is the pledge of my honour. Take it up for the trial if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Who sets me else? By heaven, I'll throw at all:","I have a thousand spirits in one breast","To answer twenty thousand such as you."],"id":444,"m":"Who else challenges me? By heaven, I'll take on everyone. I have a thousand spirits in one heart to answer twenty thousand men like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["My Lord Fitzwater, I do remember well","The very time Aumerle and you did talk."],"id":445,"m":"My Lord Fitzwater, I remember very well the exact time Aumerle and you talked."},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["'Tis very true: you were in presence then,","And you can witness with me this is true."],"id":446,"m":"That's quite true. You were there then, and you can back me up that this is true."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["As false, by heaven, as heaven itself is true."],"id":447,"m":"It is as false, by heaven, as heaven itself is true."},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["Surrey, thou liest."],"id":448,"m":"Surrey, you lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"SURREY","lines":["Dishonourable boy!","That lie shall lie so heavy on my sword","That it shall render vengeance and revenge","Till thou the lie-giver and that lie do lie","In earth as quiet as thy father's skull.","In proof whereof, there is my honour's pawn;","Engage it to the trial if thou dar'st."],"id":449,"m":"Dishonourable boy! That lie will lie so heavily on my sword that it will pay back vengeance and revenge until you, the giver of the lie, and your lie lie in the earth as quiet as your father's skull. To prove it, there is the pledge of my honour. Take it up for the trial if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["How fondly dost thou spur a forward horse!","If I dare eat, or drink, or breathe, or live,","I dare meet Surrey in a wilderness,","And spit upon him, whilst I say he lies,","And lies, and lies: there is my bond of faith","To tie thee to my strong correction.","As I intend to thrive in this new world,","Aumerle is guilty of my true appeal:","Besides, I heard the banish'd Norfolk say","That thou, Aumerle, didst send two of thy men","To execute the noble duke at Calais."],"id":450,"m":"How foolishly you spur on a horse that is already eager! If I dare to eat or drink or breathe or live, I dare to meet Surrey in a wilderness and spit on him while I say he lies, and lies, and lies. There is my pledge of honour to bind you to the harsh punishment I'll give you. As I hope to prosper in this new world, Aumerle is guilty of what I truly accuse him of. Besides, I heard the banished Norfolk say that you, Aumerle, sent two of your men to kill the noble duke at Calais."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Some honest Christian trust me with a gage.","That Norfolk lies, here do I throw down this,","If he may be repeal'd to try his honour."],"id":451,"m":"Will some honest Christian lend me a glove? I throw this down to prove that Norfolk lies, if he can be called back to defend his honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["These differences shall all rest under gage","Till Norfolk be repeal'd: repeal'd he shall be","And, though mine enemy, restor'd again","To all his lands and signories; when he is return'd,","Against Aumerle we will enforce his trial."],"id":452,"m":"These quarrels will all remain under challenge until Norfolk is called back. He shall be called back and, though he is my enemy, given back all his lands and estates. When he has returned, we will make him face Aumerle in trial by combat."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["That honourable day shall ne'er be seen.","Many a time hath banish'd Norfolk fought","For Jesu Christ in glorious Christian field,","Streaming the ensign of the Christian cross","Against black pagans, Turks, and Saracens;","And, toil'd with works of war, retir'd himself","To Italy; and there, at Venice, gave","His body to that pleasant country's earth,","And his pure soul unto his captain, Christ,","Under whose colours he had fought so long."],"id":453,"m":"That honourable day will never be seen. Banished Norfolk fought many times for Jesus Christ on glorious Christian battlefields, flying the banner of the Christian cross against black pagans, Turks and Saracens. Then, worn out by the labours of war, he retired to Italy, and there, at Venice, he gave his body to that pleasant country's earth and his pure soul to his captain, Christ, under whose flag he had fought for so long."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Why, Bishop, is Norfolk dead?"],"id":454,"m":"Why, Bishop, is Norfolk dead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["As surely as I live, my lord."],"id":455,"m":"As surely as I am alive, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Sweet peace conduct his sweet soul to the bosom","Of good old Abraham! Lords appellants,","Your differences shall all rest under gage","Till we assign you to your days of trial"],"id":456,"m":"May sweet peace lead his sweet soul to the arms of good old Abraham in heaven! Lords who have made these accusations, your quarrels will all remain under challenge until we give you days for your trials."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK, attended","id":457},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Great Duke of Lancaster, I come to thee","From plume-pluck'd Richard; who with willing soul","Adopts thee heir, and his high sceptre yields","To the possession of thy royal hand.","Ascend his throne, descending now from him;","And long live Henry, of that name the fourth!"],"id":458,"m":"Great Duke of Lancaster, I come to you from Richard, stripped of his feathers, who willingly adopts you as his heir and hands over his high sceptre to your royal hand. Climb up to his throne, which he is now climbing down from; and long live Henry, the fourth king of that name!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["In God's name, I'll ascend the regal throne."],"id":459,"m":"In God's name, I'll climb up to the royal throne."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["Marry, God forbid!","Worst in this royal presence may I speak,","Yet best beseeming me to speak the truth.","Would God that any in this noble presence","Were enough noble to be upright judge","Of noble Richard! Then true noblesse would","Learn him forbearance from so foul a wrong.","What subject can give sentence on his king?","And who sits here that is not Richard's subject?","Thieves are not judg'd but they are by to hear,","Although apparent guilt be seen in them;","And shall the figure of God's majesty,","His captain, steward, deputy elect,","Anointed, crowned, planted many years,","Be judg'd by subject and inferior breath,","And he himself not present? O! forfend it, God,","That in a Christian climate souls refin'd","Should show so heinous, black, obscene a deed!","I speak to subjects, and a subject speaks,","Stirr'd up by God, thus boldly for his king.","My Lord of Hereford here, whom you call king,","Is a foul traitor to proud Hereford's king;","And if you crown him, let me prophesy,","The blood of English shall manure the ground","And future ages groan for this foul act;","Peace shall go sleep with Turks and infidels,","And in this seat of peace tumultuous wars","Shall kin with kin and kind with kind confound;","Disorder, horror, fear, and mutiny,","Shall here inhabit, and this land be call'd","The field of Golgotha and dead men's skulls.","O! if you raise this house against this house,","It will the woefullest division prove","That ever fell upon this cursed earth.","Prevent it, resist it, let it not be so,","Lest child, child's children, cry against you 'woe!'"],"id":460,"m":"God forbid! I may be the lowest in this royal company to speak, but I am the most fitting one to speak the truth. I wish to God anyone in this noble company were noble enough to be a fair judge of noble Richard! Then true nobility would teach him to hold back from such a foul wrong. What subject can pass sentence on his king? And who sits here who is not Richard's subject? Thieves are not judged unless they are there to hear it, even when their guilt is obvious. And shall the image of God's majesty, his captain, steward and chosen deputy, anointed, crowned and established for many years, be judged by the voices of his subjects and inferiors, without even being present himself? Oh, God forbid that in a Christian country refined souls should commit such a hateful, black, disgusting deed! I am speaking to subjects, and a subject is speaking, stirred up by God to speak out like this for his king. My Lord of Hereford here, whom you call king, is a foul traitor to proud Hereford's king. And if you crown him, let me prophesy: English blood will fertilise the ground, and future ages will groan because of this foul act. Peace will go to sleep among Turks and unbelievers, and in this home of peace, violent wars will set relatives against relatives and countrymen against countrymen. Disorder, horror, fear and rebellion will live here, and this country will be called Golgotha, the field of dead men's skulls. Oh, if you raise this royal house against this house, it will be the most terrible division that ever fell on this cursed earth. Prevent it, resist it, don't let it happen, or your children and your children's children will cry 'woe' against you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Well have you argued, sir; and, for your pains,","Of capital treason we arrest you here.","My Lord of Westminster, be it your charge","To keep him safely till his day of trial.","May it please you, lords, to grant the commons' suit?"],"id":461,"m":"You have argued well, sir; and for your trouble, we arrest you here for high treason. My Lord of Westminster, I put you in charge of keeping him safe until his trial. Lords, will you grant the request of the Commons?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Fetch hither Richard, that in common view","He may surrender; so we shall proceed","Without suspicion."],"id":462,"m":"Bring Richard here, so that he can surrender in front of everyone. That way we can go ahead without anyone suspecting us."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I will be his conduct."],"id":463,"m":"I will escort him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":464},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Lords, you that here are under our arrest,","Procure your sureties for your days of answer.","Little are we beholding to your love,","And little look'd for at your helping hands."],"id":465,"m":"Lords, those of you who are under arrest here, find people to guarantee you will appear on your trial days. We owe little to your love, and we expected little help from your hands."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter YORK, with KING RICHARD, and OFFICERS bearing the Crown, &c","id":466},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Alack! why am I sent for to a king","Before I have shook off the regal thoughts","Wherewith I reign'd? I hardly yet have learn'd","To insinuate, flatter, bow, and bend my knee.","Give sorrow leave awhile to tutor me","To this submission. Yet I well remember","The favours of these men: were they not mine?","Did they not sometime cry 'All hail!' to me?","So Judas did to Christ: but he, in twelve,","Found truth in all but one; I, in twelve thousand, none.","God save the King! Will no man say, amen?","Am I both priest and clerk? Well then, amen.","God save the King! although I be not he;","And yet, amen, if heaven do think him me.","To do what service am I sent for hither?"],"id":467,"m":"Alas, why am I sent for to come to a king before I have shaken off the royal thoughts I reigned with? I have hardly learned yet how to creep, flatter, bow and bend my knee. Give sorrow a little time to teach me this submission. And yet I remember these men's faces well. Weren't they mine? Didn't they once cry 'All hail!' to me? So Judas did to Christ. But Christ, out of twelve, found all loyal but one; I, out of twelve thousand, found none. God save the King! Won't anyone say amen? Am I both the priest and the clerk who answers him? Well then, amen. God save the King, although I am not him; and yet, amen, if heaven thinks he is me. What service have I been sent for to do here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["To do that office of thine own good will","Which tired majesty did make thee offer,","The resignation of thy state and crown","To Henry Bolingbroke."],"id":468,"m":"To carry out, of your own free will, what your weary majesty made you offer: to hand over your royal power and crown to Henry Bolingbroke."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Give me the crown. Here, cousin, seize the crown.","Here, cousin,","On this side my hand, and on that side thine.","Now is this golden crown like a deep well","That owes two buckets, filling one another;","The emptier ever dancing in the air,","The other down, unseen, and full of water.","That bucket down and full of tears am I,","Drinking my griefs, whilst you mount up on high."],"id":469,"m":"Give me the crown. Here, cousin, take hold of the crown. Here, cousin: my hand on this side, and yours on that. Now this golden crown is like a deep well with two buckets, filling each other in turn, the emptier one always dancing in the air, the other one down below, unseen and full of water. I am that bucket, down and full of tears, drinking my griefs while you rise up high."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I thought you had been willing to resign."],"id":470,"m":"I thought you were willing to give up the crown."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["My crown I am; but still my griefs are mine.","You may my glories and my state depose,","But not my griefs; still am I king of those."],"id":471,"m":"My crown I am; but my griefs are still mine. You may depose my glory and my royal power, but not my griefs. I am still king of those."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Part of your cares you give me with your crown."],"id":472,"m":"With your crown you give me part of your cares."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Your cares set up do not pluck my cares down.","My care is loss of care, by old care done;","Your care is gain of care, by new care won.","The cares I give I have, though given away;","They tend the crown, yet still with me they stay."],"id":473,"m":"Your cares being raised up don't take my cares down. My care is the loss of care, caused by my old carelessness. Your care is the gain of care, won by new effort. The cares I give I still have, though I give them away. They wait on the crown, and yet they stay with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Are you contented to resign the crown?"],"id":474,"m":"Are you willing to give up the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ay, no; no, ay; for I must nothing be;","Therefore no no, for I resign to thee.","Now mark me how I will undo myself:","I give this heavy weight from off my head,","And this unwieldy sceptre from my hand,","The pride of kingly sway from out my heart;","With mine own tears I wash away my balm,","With mine own hands I give away my crown,","With mine own tongue deny my sacred state,","With mine own breath release all duteous rites:","All pomp and majesty I do forswear;","My manors, rents, revenues, I forgo;","My acts, decrees, and statutes, I deny:","God pardon all oaths that are broke to me!","God keep all vows unbroke are made to thee!","Make me, that nothing have, with nothing griev'd,","And thou with all pleas'd, that hast all achiev'd!","Long mayst thou live in Richard's seat to sit,","And soon lie Richard in an earthly pit!","God save King Henry, unking'd Richard says,","And send him many years of sunshine days!","What more remains?"],"id":475,"m":"Yes, no; no, yes. Because I must be nothing, I can't say no; I give it up to you. Now watch how I will undo myself. I take this heavy weight off my head and this clumsy sceptre out of my hand, and the pride of royal power out of my heart. With my own tears I wash off my holy oil. With my own hands I give away my crown. With my own tongue I deny my sacred rank. With my own breath I release everyone from their duties. I give up all pomp and majesty. I give up my estates, rents and income. I cancel my acts, decrees and laws. God forgive all the oaths that are broken to me! God keep unbroken all the vows that are made to you! Make me, who have nothing, not grieve about having nothing, and make you, who have gained everything, pleased with everything! Long may you live to sit on Richard's throne, and may Richard soon lie in a hole in the earth! God save King Henry, says Richard, no longer king, and send him many years of sunny days! What else is there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["[Offering a paper] No more, but that you read","These accusations, and these grievous crimes","Committed by your person and your followers","Against the state and profit of this land;","That, by confessing them, the souls of men","May deem that you are worthily depos'd."],"id":476,"m":"Nothing more, except that you read out these charges and the serious crimes committed by you and your followers against the state and welfare of this country, so that by your confessing them people will believe you have been rightly deposed."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Must I do so? And must I ravel out","My weav'd-up follies? Gentle Northumberland,","If thy offences were upon record,","Would it not shame thee in so fair a troop","To read a lecture of them? If thou wouldst,","There shouldst thou find one heinous article,","Containing the deposing of a king","And cracking the strong warrant of an oath,","Mark'd with a blot, damn'd in the book of heaven.","Nay, all of you that stand and look upon me","Whilst that my wretchedness doth bait myself,","Though some of you, with Pilate, wash your hands,","Showing an outward pity; yet you Pilates","Have here deliver'd me to my sour cross,","And water cannot wash away your sin."],"id":477,"m":"Must I do that? Must I unpick the woven pattern of my follies? Gentle Northumberland, if your crimes were written down, wouldn't you be ashamed to read them out in front of such a fine company? If you did, you would find there one terrible charge: the deposing of a king and the breaking of the strong bond of an oath, marked with a black stain and damned in the book of heaven. No, all of you who stand looking at me while my misery torments me, though some of you wash your hands like Pilate, showing pity on the outside, you Pilates have still handed me over to my bitter cross, and water cannot wash away your sin."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My lord, dispatch; read o'er these articles."],"id":478,"m":"My lord, hurry. Read through these charges."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Mine eyes are full of tears; I cannot see:","And yet salt water blinds them not so much","But they can see a sort of traitors here.","Nay, if I turn mine eyes upon myself,","I find myself a traitor with the rest;","For I have given here my soul's consent","T'undeck the pompous body of a king;","Made glory base, and sovereignty a slave,","Proud majesty a subject, state a peasant."],"id":479,"m":"My eyes are full of tears; I can't see. And yet the salt water doesn't blind them so much that they can't see a pack of traitors here. No, if I turn my eyes on myself, I find I am a traitor along with the rest, because I have given my soul's agreement here to strip the splendid body of a king, to make glory lowly and royal power a slave, proud majesty a subject and kingship a peasant."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My lord,—"],"id":480,"m":"My lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["No lord of thine, thou haught insulting man,","Nor no man's lord; I have no name, no title,","No, not that name was given me at the font,","But 'tis usurp'd: alack the heavy day!","That I have worn so many winters out,","And know not now what name to call myself!","O! that I were a mockery king of snow,","Standing before the sun of Bolingbroke","To melt myself away in water-drops!","Good king, great king,—and yet not greatly good,","An if my word be sterling yet in England,","Let it command a mirror hither straight,","That it may show me what a face I have,","Since it is bankrupt of his majesty."],"id":481,"m":"I'm no lord of yours, you proud, insulting man, and no man's lord. I have no name, no title; no, not even the name I was given at my christening, because that has been taken from me. Alas, what a sad day, that I have lived through so many winters and don't know now what name to call myself! Oh, I wish I were a snowman king, standing in front of the sun of Bolingbroke to melt myself away in drops of water! Good king, great king, though not greatly good, if my word is still worth anything in England, let it order a mirror to be brought here at once, so that it can show me what my face looks like now that it has lost its majesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Go some of you and fetch a looking-glass."],"id":482,"m":"Some of you go and fetch a mirror."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit an Attendant","id":483},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["Read o'er this paper while the glass doth come."],"id":484,"m":"Read through this paper while the mirror is coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Fiend! thou torments me ere I come to hell."],"id":485,"m":"Devil! You torment me before I even get to hell."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Urge it no more, my Lord Northumberland."],"id":486,"m":"Don't press it any further, my Lord Northumberland."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["The Commons will not then be satisfied."],"id":487,"m":"Then the Commons won't be satisfied."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["They shall be satisfied; I'll read enough,","When I do see the very book indeed","Where all my sins are writ, and that's myself."],"id":488,"m":"They will be satisfied. I'll read enough when I see the real book where all my sins are written, and that's myself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Attendant, with glass","id":489},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["Give me that glass, and therein will I read.","No deeper wrinkles yet? Hath sorrow struck","So many blows upon this face of mine","And made no deeper wounds? O flatt'ring glass!","Like to my followers in prosperity,","Thou dost beguile me. Was this face the face","That every day under his household roof","Did keep ten thousand men? Was this the face","That like the sun did make beholders wink?","Is this the face which fac'd so many follies","That was at last out-fac'd by Bolingbroke?","A brittle glory shineth in this face:","As brittle as the glory is the face;"],"id":490,"m":"Give me that mirror, and I will read in it. No deeper wrinkles yet? Has sorrow struck so many blows on this face of mine and made no deeper wounds? Oh, flattering mirror! You deceive me, like my followers when I was doing well. Was this the face that every day fed ten thousand men under its roof? Was this the face that, like the sun, made people who looked at it blink? Is this the face that put a bold face on so many follies, and was at last outfaced by Bolingbroke? A fragile glory shines in this face. The face is as fragile as the glory."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dashes the glass against the ground","id":491},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["For there it is, crack'd in a hundred shivers.","Mark, silent king, the moral of this sport,","How soon my sorrow hath destroy'd my face."],"id":492,"m":"Because there it is, cracked into a hundred pieces. Notice, silent king, the moral of this game: how quickly my sorrow has destroyed my face."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["The shadow of your sorrow hath destroy'd","The shadow of your face."],"id":493,"m":"The shadow of your sorrow has destroyed the shadow of your face."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Say that again.","The shadow of my sorrow! Ha! let's see:","'Tis very true: my grief lies all within;","And these external manner of laments","Are merely shadows to the unseen grief","That swells with silence in the tortur'd soul.","There lies the substance: and I thank thee, king,","For thy great bounty, that not only givest","Me cause to wail, but teachest me the way","How to lament the cause. I'll beg one boon,","And then be gone and trouble you no more.","Shall I obtain it?"],"id":494,"m":"Say that again. The shadow of my sorrow! Ha! Let's see. It's very true. My grief is all inside, and these outward ways of mourning are only shadows of the unseen grief that swells in silence in my tortured soul. That's where the real thing is. And I thank you, king, for your great generosity, which not only gives me reason to weep but teaches me how to mourn that reason. I'll ask one favour, and then go and trouble you no more. Shall I have it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Name it, fair cousin."],"id":495,"m":"Name it, fair cousin."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["'Fair cousin'! I am greater than a king;","For when I was a king, my flatterers","Were then but subjects; being now a subject,","I have a king here to my flatterer.","Being so great, I have no need to beg."],"id":496,"m":"'Fair cousin'! I am greater than a king. When I was a king, my flatterers were only subjects. Now that I am a subject, I have a king here as my flatterer. Being so great, I have no need to beg."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Yet ask."],"id":497,"m":"Ask anyway."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["And shall I have?"],"id":498,"m":"And will I get it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["You shall."],"id":499,"m":"You will."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Then give me leave to go."],"id":500,"m":"Then let me go."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Whither?"],"id":501,"m":"Where to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Whither you will, so I were from your sights."],"id":502,"m":"Wherever you like, as long as I am out of your sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Go, some of you convey him to the Tower."],"id":503,"m":"Some of you, take him to the Tower."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["O, good! convey? conveyers are you all,","That rise thus nimbly by a true king's fall."],"id":504,"m":"Oh, good! 'Take'? You are all takers, thieves who rise so quickly through a true king's fall."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt KING RICHARD and Guard","id":505},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["On Wednesday next we solemnly set down","Our coronation: lords, prepare yourselves."],"id":506,"m":"We formally set our coronation for next Wednesday. Lords, get yourselves ready."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but the BISHOP OF CARLISLE, the ABBOT OF WESTMINSTER, and AUMERLE","id":507},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBOT","lines":["A woeful pageant have we here beheld."],"id":508,"m":"We have seen a sad show here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CARLISLE","lines":["The woe's to come; the children yet unborn","Shall feel this day as sharp to them as thorn."],"id":509,"m":"The sorrow is still to come. Children not yet born will feel this day as sharply as a thorn."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["You holy clergymen, is there no plot","To rid the realm of this pernicious blot?"],"id":510,"m":"You holy clergymen, is there no plan to rid the country of this destructive stain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ABBOT","lines":["My lord,","Before I freely speak my mind herein,","You shall not only take the sacrament","To bury mine intents, but also to effect","Whatever I shall happen to devise.","I see your brows are full of discontent,","Your hearts of sorrow, and your eyes of tears:","Come home with me to supper; I will lay","A plot shall show us all a merry day."],"id":511,"m":"My lord, before I speak my mind freely on this, you must take communion and swear not only to keep my intentions secret, but also to carry out whatever I decide. I can see your brows are full of discontent, your hearts full of sorrow and your eyes full of tears. Come home to supper with me. I will set out a plan that will bring us all a happy day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":512},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"London. A street leading to the Tower.","id":513},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the QUEEN and ladies","id":514},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["This way the King will come; this is the way","To Julius Caesar's ill-erected tower,","To whose flint bosom my condemned lord","Is doom'd a prisoner by proud Bolingbroke.","Here let us rest, if this rebellious earth","Have any resting for her true King's queen."],"id":515,"m":"The King will come this way. This is the way to the Tower that Julius Caesar built for evil, where proud Bolingbroke has sentenced my condemned husband to be a prisoner in its flint-hard heart. Let's rest here, if this rebellious earth has any resting place for her true King's queen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD and Guard","id":516},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["But soft, but see, or rather do not see,","My fair rose wither; yet look up, behold,","That you in pity may dissolve to dew,","And wash him fresh again with true-love tears.","Ah! thou, the model where old Troy did stand;","Thou map of honour, thou King Richard's tomb,","And not King Richard; thou most beauteous inn,","Why should hard-favour'd grief be lodg'd in thee,","When triumph is become an alehouse guest?"],"id":517,"m":"But wait, look, or rather don't look, at my fair rose withering. And yet look up, see him, so that you melt into dew out of pity and wash him fresh again with tears of true love. Ah, you, the ruin where old Troy once stood, the outline of honour, King Richard's tomb and not King Richard himself, you most beautiful inn, why should ugly grief be lodged in you, when triumph has become a guest at a common alehouse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Join not with grief, fair woman, do not so,","To make my end too sudden: learn, good soul,","To think our former state a happy dream;","From which awak'd, the truth of what we are","Shows us but this. I am sworn brother, sweet,","To grim Necessity; and he and I","Will keep a league till death. Hie thee to France,","And cloister thee in some religious house:","Our holy lives must win a new world's crown,","Which our profane hours here have thrown down."],"id":518,"m":"Don't join forces with grief, fair woman, don't, to make my end too sudden. Learn, good soul, to think of our former greatness as a happy dream. Now that we have woken from it, the truth of what we are shows us only this. I am sworn brother, sweet, to grim Necessity, and he and I will stay allied until death. Hurry to France and shut yourself away in some convent. Our holy lives must win a crown in the next world, which our wicked hours here have thrown down."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["What! is my Richard both in shape and mind","Transform'd and weaken'd! Hath Bolingbroke depos'd","Thine intellect? Hath he been in thy heart?","The lion dying thrusteth forth his paw","And wounds the earth, if nothing else, with rage","To be o'erpower'd; and wilt thou, pupil-like,","Take the correction mildly, kiss the rod,","And fawn on rage with base humility,","Which art a lion and the king of beasts?"],"id":519,"m":"What! Has my Richard been changed and weakened in both body and mind? Has Bolingbroke deposed your mind too? Has he been inside your heart? The lion, when he is dying, thrusts out his paw and wounds the earth, if nothing else, in rage at being overpowered. And will you, like a schoolboy, take your punishment meekly, kiss the cane and fawn on the anger against you with low humility, when you are a lion and the king of beasts?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["A king of beasts, indeed; if aught but beasts,","I had been still a happy king of men.","Good sometimes queen, prepare thee hence for France.","Think I am dead, and that even here thou tak'st,","As from my death-bed, thy last living leave.","In winter's tedious nights sit by the fire","With good old folks, and let them tell thee tales","Of woeful ages long ago betid;","And ere thou bid good night, to quit their griefs","Tell thou the lamentable tale of me,","And send the hearers weeping to their beds;","For why, the senseless brands will sympathize","The heavy accent of thy moving tongue,","And in compassion weep the fire out;","And some will mourn in ashes, some coal-black,","For the deposing of a rightful king."],"id":520,"m":"King of beasts indeed. If they had been anything but beasts, I would still be a happy king of men. Good woman, once my queen, get ready to leave for France. Think I am dead, and that even here you are taking your last leave of me, as if at my deathbed. On long winter nights, sit by the fire with good old people and let them tell you stories of sad times long ago. And before you say good night, to pay them back for their sad stories, tell them the sad story of me, and send your listeners weeping to their beds. Because even the senseless logs will feel for the heavy tone of your moving voice and weep the fire out in pity, and some will mourn in ashes, some in coal-black, for the deposing of a rightful king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter NORTHUMBERLAND, attended","id":521},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My lord, the mind of Bolingbroke is chang'd;","You must to Pomfret, not unto the Tower.","And, madam, there is order ta'en for you:","With all swift speed you must away to France."],"id":522,"m":"My lord, Bolingbroke has changed his mind. You must go to Pomfret, not to the Tower. And, madam, arrangements have been made for you: you must leave for France as fast as possible."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Northumberland, thou ladder wherewithal","The mounting Bolingbroke ascends my throne,","The time shall not be many hours of age","More than it is, ere foul sin gathering head","Shall break into corruption. Thou shalt think,","Though he divide the realm and give thee half","It is too little, helping him to all;","And he shall think that thou, which know'st the way","To plant unrightful kings, wilt know again,","Being ne'er so little urg'd, another way","To pluck him headlong from the usurped throne.","The love of wicked men converts to fear;","That fear to hate; and hate turns one or both","To worthy danger and deserved death."],"id":523,"m":"Northumberland, you ladder that the climbing Bolingbroke uses to reach my throne, it won't be many hours older than it is now before foul sin comes to a head and bursts like a boil. You will think, even if he divides the kingdom and gives you half, that it is too little, since you helped him get all of it. And he will think that you, who know how to set up kings who have no right, will know another way, with very little pushing, to pull him headlong off the throne he stole. The love of wicked men turns to fear, that fear to hate, and hate leads one or both of them to the danger and death they deserve."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["My guilt be on my head, and there an end.","Take leave, and part; for you must part forthwith."],"id":524,"m":"Let my guilt be on my own head, and that's the end of it. Say goodbye and part, because you must part immediately."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Doubly divorc'd! Bad men, ye violate","A twofold marriage; 'twixt my crown and me,","And then betwixt me and my married wife.","Let me unkiss the oath 'twixt thee and me;","And yet not so, for with a kiss 'twas made.","Part us, Northumberland: I towards the north,","Where shivering cold and sickness pines the clime;","My wife to France, from whence set forth in pomp,","She came adorned hither like sweet May,","Sent back like Hallowmas or short'st of day."],"id":525,"m":"Divorced twice over! Wicked men, you break two marriages: between my crown and me, and then between me and my wife. Let me unkiss the vow between you and me. And yet no, because it was made with a kiss. Separate us, Northumberland: me to the north, where shivering cold and sickness waste the land; my wife to France, where she came from in splendour, decked out like sweet May, and is sent back like dark November, the shortest day of the year."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["And must we be divided? Must we part?"],"id":526,"m":"And must we be divided? Must we part?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Ay, hand from hand, my love, and heart from heart."],"id":527,"m":"Yes, hand from hand, my love, and heart from heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Banish us both, and send the king with me."],"id":528,"m":"Banish us both, and send the king with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["That were some love, but little policy."],"id":529,"m":"That would be loving, but not good politics."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Then whither he goes, thither let me go."],"id":530,"m":"Then wherever he goes, let me go there too."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["So two, together weeping, make one woe.","Weep thou for me in France, I for thee here;","Better far off than near, be ne'er the near.","Go, count thy way with sighs; I mine with groans."],"id":531,"m":"Then two, weeping together, make one sorrow. You weep for me in France, and I for you here. It is better to be far apart than near if we can never be any nearer. Go, and measure your road with sighs; I'll measure mine with groans."},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["So longest way shall have the longest moans."],"id":532,"m":"Then the longest road will have the longest moans."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Twice for one step I'll groan, the way being short,","And piece the way out with a heavy heart.","Come, come, in wooing sorrow let's be brief,","Since, wedding it, there is such length in grief.","One kiss shall stop our mouths, and dumbly part;","Thus give I mine, and thus take I thy heart."],"id":533,"m":"I'll groan twice for every step, since my road is short, and make it longer with a heavy heart. Come, come, let's be brief in courting sorrow, since once we're married to it, grief lasts so long. One kiss will stop our mouths, and we'll part in silence. Like this I give you mine, and like this I take your heart."},{"t":"sd","text":"They kiss","id":534},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","lines":["Give me mine own again; 'twere no good part","To take on me to keep and kill thy heart."],"id":535,"m":"Give me my own back. It would be a bad thing to take your heart to keep and then kill it with grief."},{"t":"sd","text":"They kiss again","id":536},{"t":"sp","who":"QUEEN","cont":1,"lines":["So, now I have mine own again, be gone.","That I may strive to kill it with a groan."],"id":537,"m":"There, now I have my own again. Go, so that I can try to kill it with a groan."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["We make woe wanton with this fond delay:","Once more, adieu; the rest let sorrow say."],"id":538,"m":"We play with sorrow by this foolish delay. Once more, goodbye. Let sorrow say the rest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":539},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A room in the DUKE OF YORK's palace.","id":540},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK and his DUCHESS","id":541},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["My Lord, you told me you would tell the rest,","When weeping made you break the story off,","Of our two cousins' coming into London."],"id":542,"m":"My lord, you told me you would tell me the rest of the story of our two nephews' arrival in London, when crying made you break off."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Where did I leave?"],"id":543,"m":"Where did I stop?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["At that sad stop, my lord,","Where rude misgoverned hands from windows' tops","Threw dust and rubbish on King Richard's head."],"id":544,"m":"At that sad point, my lord, where rough, unruly hands at the tops of windows threw dust and rubbish on King Richard's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Then, as I said, the Duke, great Bolingbroke,","Mounted upon a hot and fiery steed","Which his aspiring rider seem'd to know,","With slow but stately pace kept on his course,","Whilst all tongues cried 'God save thee, Bolingbroke!'","You would have thought the very windows spake,","So many greedy looks of young and old","Through casements darted their desiring eyes","Upon his visage; and that all the walls","With painted imagery had said at once","'Jesu preserve thee! Welcome, Bolingbroke!'","Whilst he, from the one side to the other turning,","Bareheaded, lower than his proud steed's neck,","Bespake them thus, 'I thank you, countrymen:'","And thus still doing, thus he pass'd along."],"id":545,"m":"Then, as I said, the Duke, great Bolingbroke, riding a hot, fiery horse that seemed to know its ambitious rider, kept on his way at a slow but stately pace, while everyone shouted, 'God save you, Bolingbroke!' You would have thought the windows themselves were speaking, so many eager eyes of young and old darted their longing looks through them at his face; and that all the walls, hung with painted cloths, were saying at once, 'Jesus protect you! Welcome, Bolingbroke!' Meanwhile he, turning from one side to the other, with his head bare and bowed lower than his proud horse's neck, spoke to them like this: 'Thank you, countrymen.' And doing this all the way, he passed along."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Alack, poor Richard! where rode he the whilst?"],"id":546,"m":"Alas, poor Richard! Where was he riding all this time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["As in a theatre, the eyes of men","After a well-grac'd actor leaves the stage","Are idly bent on him that enters next,","Thinking his prattle to be tedious;","Even so, or with much more contempt, men's eyes","Did scowl on Richard: no man cried 'God save him;'","No joyful tongue gave him his welcome home;","But dust was thrown upon his sacred head,","Which with such gentle sorrow he shook off,","His face still combating with tears and smiles,","The badges of his grief and patience,","That had not God, for some strong purpose, steel'd","The hearts of men, they must perforce have melted,","And barbarism itself have pitied him.","But heaven hath a hand in these events,","To whose high will we bound our calm contents.","To Bolingbroke are we sworn subjects now,","Whose state and honour I for aye allow."],"id":547,"m":"As in a theatre, when a popular actor leaves the stage, people's eyes turn carelessly to the one who comes on next, thinking his chatter boring; in the same way, or with much more contempt, people's eyes scowled at Richard. No one shouted 'God save him'. No joyful voice welcomed him home. Instead dust was thrown on his sacred head, which he shook off with such gentle sorrow, his face struggling between tears and smiles, the signs of his grief and his patience, that if God had not hardened men's hearts for some strong purpose, they would have had to melt, and even savages would have pitied him. But heaven has a hand in these events, and we limit our wishes calmly to its high will. We are sworn subjects of Bolingbroke now, and I accept his power and honour for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Here comes my son Aumerle."],"id":548,"m":"Here comes my son Aumerle."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Aumerle that was;","But that is lost for being Richard's friend,","And madam, you must call him Rutland now.","I am in Parliament pledge for his truth","And lasting fealty to the new-made king."],"id":549,"m":"Aumerle he was; but he has lost that title for being Richard's friend, and, madam, you must call him Rutland now. I have pledged myself in Parliament as guarantee of his loyalty and lasting faithfulness to the new king."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter AUMERLE","id":550},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Welcome, my son: who are the violets now","That strew the green lap of the new come spring?"],"id":551,"m":"Welcome, my son. Who are the violets now that cover the green lap of the newly arrived spring?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Madam, I know not, nor I greatly care not.","God knows I had as lief be none as one."],"id":552,"m":"Madam, I don't know, and I don't much care. God knows I'd as soon not be one as be one."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Well, bear you well in this new spring of time,","Lest you be cropp'd before you come to prime.","What news from Oxford? hold those justs and triumphs?"],"id":553,"m":"Well, behave yourself well in this new spring of time, or you may be cut down before you come into flower. What news from Oxford? Are those tournaments and celebrations still going ahead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["For aught I know, my lord, they do."],"id":554,"m":"As far as I know, my lord, they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["You will be there, I know."],"id":555,"m":"You will be there, I know."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["If God prevent not, I purpose so."],"id":556,"m":"If God doesn't prevent it, I intend to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["What seal is that hangs without thy bosom?","Yea, look'st thou pale? Let me see the writing."],"id":557,"m":"What seal is that hanging outside your coat? What, are you going pale? Let me see the writing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["My lord, 'tis nothing."],"id":558,"m":"My lord, it's nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["No matter, then, who see it.","I will be satisfied; let me see the writing."],"id":559,"m":"Then it doesn't matter who sees it. I will be satisfied. Let me see the writing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["I do beseech your Grace to pardon me;","It is a matter of small consequence","Which for some reasons I would not have seen."],"id":560,"m":"I beg your Grace to excuse me. It's something unimportant that, for certain reasons, I don't want anyone to see."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Which for some reasons, sir, I mean to see.","I fear, I fear—"],"id":561,"m":"And for certain reasons, sir, I mean to see it. I fear, I fear..."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["What should you fear?","'Tis nothing but some bond that he is ent'red into","For gay apparel 'gainst the triumph day."],"id":562,"m":"What should you be afraid of? It's only some IOU he has signed for fine clothes for the day of the tournament."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Bound to himself! What doth he with a bond","That he is bound to? Wife, thou art a fool.","Boy, let me see the writing."],"id":563,"m":"Owed to himself? What is he doing with an IOU that he holds himself? Wife, you're a fool. Boy, let me see the writing."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["I do beseech you, pardon me; I may not show it."],"id":564,"m":"I beg you, excuse me. I can't show it."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["I will be satisfied; let me see it, I say."],"id":565,"m":"I will be satisfied. Let me see it, I say."},{"t":"sd","text":"Snatches it and reads","id":566},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Treason, foul treason! Villain! traitor! slave!"],"id":567,"m":"Treason, foul treason! Villain! Traitor! Wretch!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["What is the matter, my lord?"],"id":568,"m":"What's the matter, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Ho! who is within there?"],"id":569,"m":"Hey! Who's in there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant","id":570},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Saddle my horse.","God for his mercy! what treachery is here!"],"id":571,"m":"Saddle my horse. God have mercy! What treachery is this!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Why, what is it, my lord?"],"id":572,"m":"Why, what is it, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Give me my boots, I say; saddle my horse.","Now, by mine honour, by my life, my troth,","I will appeach the villain."],"id":573,"m":"Give me my boots, I tell you. Saddle my horse. Now, by my honour, by my life, by my faith, I will denounce the villain."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant","id":574},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["What is the matter?"],"id":575,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Peace, foolish woman."],"id":576,"m":"Quiet, foolish woman."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["I will not peace. What is the matter, Aumerle?"],"id":577,"m":"I won't be quiet. What's the matter, Aumerle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Good mother, be content; it is no more","Than my poor life must answer."],"id":578,"m":"Good mother, calm down. It's nothing more than my poor life will have to pay for."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Thy life answer!"],"id":579,"m":"Your life pay for it!"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Bring me my boots. I will unto the King."],"id":580,"m":"Bring me my boots. I'm going to the King."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Servant with boots","id":581},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Strike him, Aumerle. Poor boy, thou art amaz'd."],"id":582,"m":"Hit him, Aumerle. Poor boy, you're stunned."},{"t":"sd","text":"To Servant","id":583},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Hence, villain! never more come in my sight."],"id":584,"m":"Get out, villain! Never come into my sight again."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant","id":585},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Give me my boots, I say."],"id":586,"m":"Give me my boots, I tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Why, York, what wilt thou do?","Wilt thou not hide the trespass of thine own?","Have we more sons? or are we like to have?","Is not my teeming date drunk up with time?","And wilt thou pluck my fair son from mine age","And rob me of a happy mother's name?","Is he not like thee? Is he not thine own?"],"id":587,"m":"Why, York, what are you going to do? Won't you hide your own son's crime? Do we have any more sons, or are we likely to? Hasn't time used up my years for having children? And will you snatch my fair son away from me in my old age and rob me of the name of happy mother? Isn't he like you? Isn't he your own?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Thou fond mad woman,","Wilt thou conceal this dark conspiracy?","A dozen of them here have ta'en the sacrament,","And interchangeably set down their hands","To kill the King at Oxford."],"id":588,"m":"You foolish, mad woman, do you want to hide this dark conspiracy? A dozen of them have taken communion and signed their names to each other's copies to kill the King at Oxford."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["He shall be none;","We'll keep him here: then what is that to him?"],"id":589,"m":"He won't be one of them. We'll keep him here. Then what has it got to do with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Away, fond woman! were he twenty times my son","I would appeach him."],"id":590,"m":"Away, foolish woman! If he were my son twenty times over, I would denounce him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Hadst thou groan'd for him","As I have done, thou'dst be more pitiful.","But now I know thy mind: thou dost suspect","That I have been disloyal to thy bed","And that he is a bastard, not thy son:","Sweet York, sweet husband, be not of that mind.","He is as like thee as a man may be","Not like to me, or any of my kin,","And yet I love him."],"id":591,"m":"If you had groaned in labour for him as I did, you would have more pity. But now I know what you think. You suspect that I have been unfaithful to you, and that he is a bastard, not your son. Sweet York, sweet husband, don't think that. He is as like you as any man can be, not like me or any of my family. And yet I love him."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Make way, unruly woman!"],"id":592,"m":"Get out of my way, unruly woman!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":593},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["After, Aumerle! Mount thee upon his horse;","Spur post, and get before him to the king,","And beg thy pardon ere he do accuse thee.","I'll not be long behind; though I be old,","I doubt not but to ride as fast as York:","And never will I rise up from the ground","Till Bolingbroke have pardon'd thee. Away! be gone."],"id":594,"m":"After him, Aumerle! Get on his horse, spur it as fast as you can and get to the king before him, and beg for your pardon before he accuses you. I won't be far behind. Though I'm old, I'm sure I can ride as fast as York. And I will never get up off the ground until Bolingbroke has pardoned you. Go! Away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":595},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Windsor. A room in the Castle.","id":596},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BOLINGBROKE as King, HENRY PERCY, and other LORDS","id":597},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Can no man tell me of my unthrifty son?","'Tis full three months since I did see him last.","If any plague hang over us, 'tis he.","I would to God, my lords, he might be found.","Inquire at London, 'mongst the taverns there,","For there, they say, he daily doth frequent","With unrestrained loose companions,","Even such, they say, as stand in narrow lanes","And beat our watch and rob our passengers;","Which he, young wanton and effeminate boy,","Takes on the point of honour to support","So dissolute a crew."],"id":598,"m":"Can nobody tell me anything about my wild son? It's three whole months since I last saw him. If any disaster is hanging over us, it's him. I wish to God, my lords, he could be found. Ask in London, among the taverns there, because they say he goes there every day with wild, unruly companions, the kind, they say, who wait in narrow alleys to beat up our watchmen and rob travellers; while he, a spoiled, soft young man, makes it a point of honour to support such a disgraceful gang."},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["My lord, some two days since I saw the prince,","And told him of those triumphs held at Oxford."],"id":599,"m":"My lord, about two days ago I saw the prince and told him about the tournament being held at Oxford."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["And what said the gallant?"],"id":600,"m":"And what did the young gallant say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["His answer was: he would unto the stews,","And from the common'st creature pluck a glove","And wear it as a favour; and with that","He would unhorse the lustiest challenger."],"id":601,"m":"He answered that he would go to the brothels, take a glove from the commonest whore there and wear it as a lady's token, and with that he would knock the strongest challenger off his horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["As dissolute as desperate; yet through both","I see some sparks of better hope, which elder years","May happily bring forth. But who comes here?"],"id":602,"m":"As wild as he is reckless. And yet through both I see some sparks of better hope, which older age may luckily bring out. But who is this?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter AUMERLE","id":603},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Where is the King?"],"id":604,"m":"Where is the King?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["What means our cousin that he stares and looks","So wildly?"],"id":605,"m":"What does our cousin mean by staring and looking so wild?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["God save your Grace! I do beseech your majesty,","To have some conference with your Grace alone."],"id":606,"m":"God save your Grace! I beg your majesty to let me talk with your Grace alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Withdraw yourselves, and leave us here alone."],"id":607,"m":"Leave us, and let us be alone here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt HENRY PERCY and LORDS","id":608},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","cont":1,"lines":["What is the matter with our cousin now?"],"id":609,"m":"What's the matter with our cousin now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["[Kneels] For ever may my knees grow to the earth,","My tongue cleave to my roof within my mouth,","Unless a pardon ere I rise or speak."],"id":610,"m":"May my knees grow into the earth for ever, and my tongue stick to the roof of my mouth, unless I have a pardon before I get up or speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Intended or committed was this fault?","If on the first, how heinous e'er it be,","To win thy after-love I pardon thee."],"id":611,"m":"Was this crime planned or carried out? If only planned, however terrible it is, I pardon you to win your love afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Then give me leave that I may turn the key,","That no man enter till my tale be done."],"id":612,"m":"Then let me turn the key, so that no one comes in until I have finished my story."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Have thy desire."],"id":613,"m":"Do as you wish."},{"t":"sd","text":"AUMERLE locks the door","id":614},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["[Within] My liege, beware! look to thyself;","Thou hast a traitor in thy presence there."],"id":615,"m":"My lord, beware! Look after yourself. You have a traitor there with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["[Drawing] Villain, I'll make thee safe."],"id":616,"m":"Villain, I'll make sure you can do no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Stay thy revengeful hand; thou hast no cause to fear."],"id":617,"m":"Hold back your revengeful hand. You have no reason to be afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["[Within] Open the door, secure, foolhardy king:","Shall I, for love, speak treason to thy face?","Open the door, or I will break it open."],"id":618,"m":"Open the door, you careless, foolhardy king. Must I, out of love, speak treason to your face? Open the door, or I'll break it open."},{"t":"sd","text":"BOLINGBROKE unlocks the door; and afterwards, relocks it","id":619},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter YORK","id":620},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["What is the matter, uncle? speak;","Recover breath; tell us how near is danger,","That we may arm us to encounter it."],"id":621,"m":"What's the matter, uncle? Speak. Get your breath back. Tell us how near the danger is, so that we can arm ourselves to meet it."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Peruse this writing here, and thou shalt know","The treason that my haste forbids me show."],"id":622,"m":"Read this paper here, and you will know about the treason that I am too out of breath to explain."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Remember, as thou read'st, thy promise pass'd:","I do repent me; read not my name there;","My heart is not confederate with my hand."],"id":623,"m":"Remember your promise as you read. I repent. Don't read my name there. My heart isn't partner to my hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["It was, villain, ere thy hand did set it down.","I tore it from the traitor's bosom, king;","Fear, and not love, begets his penitence.","Forget to pity him, lest thy pity prove","A serpent that will sting thee to the heart."],"id":624,"m":"It was, villain, before your hand wrote it down. I tore it out of the traitor's coat, King. Fear, not love, makes him sorry. Forget to pity him, or your pity may turn out to be a snake that stings you to the heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["O heinous, strong, and bold conspiracy!","O loyal father of a treacherous son!","Thou sheer, immaculate, and silver fountain,","From whence this stream through muddy passages","Hath held his current and defil'd himself!","Thy overflow of good converts to bad;","And thy abundant goodness shall excuse","This deadly blot in thy digressing son."],"id":625,"m":"Oh, terrible, powerful and bold conspiracy! Oh, loyal father of a treacherous son! You pure, spotless, silver spring, from which this stream has run through muddy channels and dirtied itself! Your overflowing goodness turns into bad in him, and your great goodness will excuse this deadly stain in your straying son."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["So shall my virtue be his vice's bawd,","And he shall spend mine honour with his shame,","As thriftless sons their scraping fathers' gold.","Mine honour lives when his dishonour dies,","Or my sham'd life in his dishonour lies:","Thou kill'st me in his life; giving him breath,","The traitor lives, the true man's put to death."],"id":626,"m":"Then my virtue will be the pimp for his vice, and he will spend my honour on his shame, as wasteful sons spend their stingy fathers' gold. My honour lives when his dishonour dies, or else my shamed life lies in his dishonour. You kill me by letting him live. If you give him breath, the traitor lives and the loyal man is put to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["[Within] What ho! my liege, for God's sake, let me in."],"id":627,"m":"Hello there! My lord, for God's sake, let me in."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["What shrill-voic'd suppliant makes this eager cry?"],"id":628,"m":"What shrill-voiced beggar is crying out so eagerly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["[Within] A woman, and thine aunt, great king; 'tis I.","Speak with me, pity me, open the door:","A beggar begs that never begg'd before."],"id":629,"m":"A woman, and your aunt, great king. It's me. Speak to me, pity me, open the door. A beggar is begging who has never begged before."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Our scene is alter'd from a serious thing,","And now chang'd to 'The Beggar and the King.'","My dangerous cousin, let your mother in:","I know she's come to pray for your foul sin."],"id":630,"m":"Our play has changed from a serious drama into 'The Beggar and the King'. My dangerous cousin, let your mother in. I know she has come to plead for your foul sin."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["If thou do pardon, whosoever pray,","More sins for this forgiveness prosper may.","This fester'd joint cut off, the rest rest sound;","This let alone will all the rest confound."],"id":631,"m":"If you pardon him, whoever pleads, more sins may grow because of this forgiveness. Cut off this infected limb, and the rest stays healthy. Leave it alone, and it will destroy all the rest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter DUCHESS","id":632},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["O King, believe not this hard-hearted man:","Love, loving not itself, none other can."],"id":633,"m":"O King, don't believe this hard-hearted man. Someone who doesn't love his own flesh and blood can't love anyone else."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Thou frantic woman, what dost thou make here?","Shall thy old dugs once more a traitor rear?"],"id":634,"m":"You crazy woman, what are you doing here? Will your old breasts feed a traitor once again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Sweet York, be patient. [Kneels] Hear me, gentle liege."],"id":635,"m":"Sweet York, be patient. Hear me, gentle lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Rise up, good aunt."],"id":636,"m":"Get up, good aunt."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Not yet, I thee beseech.","For ever will I walk upon my knees,","And never see day that the happy sees,","Till thou give joy: until thou bid me joy","By pardoning Rutland, my transgressing boy."],"id":637,"m":"Not yet, I beg you. I will walk on my knees for ever and never see a happy day until you give me joy, by pardoning Rutland, my sinful boy."},{"t":"sp","who":"AUMERLE","lines":["Unto my mother's prayers I bend my knee."],"id":638,"m":"I kneel to support my mother's prayers."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels","id":639},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Against them both, my true joints bended be."],"id":640,"m":"And I bend my loyal knees against them both."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneels","id":641},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","cont":1,"lines":["Ill mayst thou thrive, if thou grant any grace!"],"id":642,"m":"May bad luck come to you if you grant any mercy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Pleads he in earnest? Look upon his face;","His eyes do drop no tears, his prayers are in jest;","His words come from his mouth, ours from our breast;","He prays but faintly and would be denied;","We pray with heart and soul, and all beside:","His weary joints would gladly rise, I know;","Our knees still kneel till to the ground they grow:","His prayers are full of false hypocrisy;","Ours of true zeal and deep integrity.","Our prayers do out-pray his; then let them have","That mercy which true prayer ought to have."],"id":643,"m":"Does he mean it? Look at his face. His eyes shed no tears; his prayers are a joke. His words come from his mouth, ours from our hearts. He prays weakly and wants to be refused. We pray with heart and soul and everything else. His tired joints would happily get up, I know. Our knees will keep kneeling until they grow into the ground. His prayers are full of false hypocrisy, ours of true passion and deep honesty. Our prayers outpray his, so let them have the mercy that true prayer deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Good aunt, stand up."],"id":644,"m":"Good aunt, stand up."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Nay, do not say 'stand up';","Say 'pardon' first, and afterwards 'stand up'.","An if I were thy nurse, thy tongue to teach,","'Pardon' should be the first word of thy speech.","I never long'd to hear a word till now;","Say 'pardon,' king; let pity teach thee how:","The word is short, but not so short as sweet;","No word like 'pardon' for kings' mouths so meet."],"id":645,"m":"No, don't say 'stand up'. Say 'pardon' first, and 'stand up' afterwards. If I were your nurse, teaching you to speak, 'pardon' would be the first word you said. I have never longed to hear a word until now. Say 'pardon', king. Let pity teach you how. The word is short, but even more sweet than short. No word is so fitting for kings' mouths as 'pardon'."},{"t":"sp","who":"YORK","lines":["Speak it in French, King, say 'pardonne moy.'"],"id":646,"m":"Say it in French, King: say 'pardonne-moi', the polite way of saying no."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Dost thou teach pardon pardon to destroy?","Ah! my sour husband, my hard-hearted lord,","That sett'st the word itself against the word.","Speak 'pardon' as 'tis current in our land;","The chopping French we do not understand.","Thine eye begins to speak, set thy tongue there,","Or in thy piteous heart plant thou thine ear,","That hearing how our plaints and prayers do pierce,","Pity may move thee pardon to rehearse."],"id":647,"m":"Are you teaching him to use the word 'pardon' to destroy pardon? Ah, my sour husband, my hard-hearted lord, who sets the word against itself! Say 'pardon' the way we use it in our country. We don't understand clipped French. Your eyes are beginning to speak; let your tongue say what they say. Or put your ear into your pitying heart, so that, hearing how our pleas and prayers pierce it, pity may move you to say 'pardon'."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Good aunt, stand up."],"id":648,"m":"Good aunt, stand up."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["I do not sue to stand;","Pardon is all the suit I have in hand."],"id":649,"m":"I'm not begging to stand. Pardon is the only thing I'm asking for."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["I pardon him, as God shall pardon me."],"id":650,"m":"I pardon him, as God will pardon me."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["O happy vantage of a kneeling knee!","Yet am I sick for fear: speak it again;","Twice saying 'pardon' doth not pardon twain,"],"id":651,"m":"Oh, what a happy result from kneeling! But I am still sick with fear. Say it again. Saying 'pardon' twice doesn't pardon two people,"},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","cont":1,"lines":["But makes one pardon strong."],"id":652,"m":"but it makes one pardon strong."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["With all my heart","I pardon him."],"id":653,"m":"With all my heart I pardon him."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["A god on earth thou art."],"id":654,"m":"You are a god on earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["But for our trusty brother-in-law and the abbot,","With all the rest of that consorted crew,","Destruction straight shall dog them at the heels.","Good uncle, help to order several powers","To Oxford, or where'er these traitors are:","They shall not live within this world, I swear,","But I will have them, if I once know where.","Uncle, farewell: and, cousin, adieu:","Your mother well hath pray'd, and prove you true."],"id":655,"m":"But as for our trusty brother-in-law, the abbot and all the rest of that gang of conspirators, destruction will follow at their heels at once. Good uncle, help send several forces to Oxford or wherever these traitors are. I swear they won't stay alive in this world; I'll get them, once I know where they are. Uncle, goodbye. And, cousin, farewell. Your mother has prayed well; now prove yourself loyal."},{"t":"sp","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","lines":["Come, my old son: I pray God make thee new."],"id":656,"m":"Come, my old son. I pray God makes you new."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":657},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another room in the Castle.","id":658},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EXTON and a Servant","id":659},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["Didst thou not mark the king, what words he spake?","'Have I no friend will rid me of this living fear?'","Was it not so?"],"id":660,"m":"Didn't you notice what the king said? 'Have I no friend who will rid me of this living fear?' Wasn't that it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["These were his very words."],"id":661,"m":"Those were his exact words."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["'Have I no friend?' quoth he: he spake it twice","And urg'd it twice together, did he not?"],"id":662,"m":"'Have I no friend?' he said. He said it twice and pressed it twice in a row, didn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He did."],"id":663,"m":"He did."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["And, speaking it, he wistly looked on me,","As who should say 'I would thou wert the man","That would divorce this terror from my heart';","Meaning the king at Pomfret. Come, let's go.","I am the king's friend, and will rid his foe."],"id":664,"m":"And as he said it, he looked at me meaningfully, as if to say, 'I wish you were the man who would free my heart of this terror', meaning the king at Pomfret. Come, let's go. I am the king's friend, and I'll get rid of his enemy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":665},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Pomfret. The dungeon of the Castle.","id":666},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter KING RICHARD","id":667},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["I have been studying how I may compare","This prison where I live unto the world","And for because the world is populous,","And here is not a creature but myself,","I cannot do it; yet I'll hammer it out.","My brain I'll prove the female to my soul;","My soul the father: and these two beget","A generation of still-breeding thoughts,","And these same thoughts people this little world,","In humours like the people of this world,","For no thought is contented. The better sort,","As thoughts of things divine, are intermix'd","With scruples, and do set the word itself","Against the word:","As thus: 'Come, little ones'; and then again,","'It is as hard to come as for a camel","To thread the postern of a needle's eye.'","Thoughts tending to ambition, they do plot","Unlikely wonders; how these vain weak nails","May tear a passage through the flinty ribs","Of this hard world, my ragged prison walls;","And, for they cannot, die in their own pride.","Thoughts tending to content flatter themselves","That they are not the first of fortune's slaves,","Nor shall not be the last; like silly beggars","Who sitting in the stocks refuge their shame,","That many have and others must sit there:","And in this thought they find a kind of ease,","Bearing their own misfortunes on the back","Of such as have before endur'd the like.","Thus play I in one person many people,","And none contented: sometimes am I king;","Then treasons make me wish myself a beggar,","And so I am: then crushing penury","Persuades me I was better when a king;","Then am I king'd again; and by and by","Think that I am unking'd by Bolingbroke,","And straight am nothing: but whate'er I be,","Nor I, nor any man that but man is","With nothing shall be pleas'd till he be eas'd","With being nothing.","Music do I hear? [Music]","Ha, ha! keep time. How sour sweet music is","When time is broke and no proportion kept!","So is it in the music of men's lives.","And here have I the daintiness of ear","To check time broke in a disorder'd string;","But, for the concord of my state and time,","Had not an ear to hear my true time broke.","I wasted time, and now doth time waste me;","For now hath time made me his numbering clock:","My thoughts are minutes; and with sighs they jar","Their watches on unto mine eyes, the outward watch,","Whereto my finger, like a dial's point,","Is pointing still, in cleansing them from tears.","Now sir, the sound that tells what hour it is","Are clamorous groans, which strike upon my heart,","Which is the bell: so sighs and tears and groans","Show minutes, times, and hours; but my time","Runs posting on in Bolingbroke's proud joy,","While I stand fooling here, his Jack o' the clock.","This music mads me; let it sound no more;","For though it have holp madmen to their wits,","In me it seems it will make wise men mad.","Yet blessing on his heart that gives it me!","For 'tis a sign of love; and love to Richard","Is a strange brooch in this all-hating world."],"id":668,"m":"I have been working out how I can compare this prison where I live to the world. And because the world is full of people, and here there is no creature but me, I can't do it. But I'll hammer it out. I'll make my brain the mother and my soul the father, and the two of them will produce a family of thoughts that keep on breeding. And these same thoughts fill this little world, with moods like the people of this world, because no thought is satisfied. The better kind, like thoughts about religion, are mixed with doubts, and set one part of the Bible against another, like this: 'Come, little children', and then again, 'It is as hard to come to heaven as it is for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle.' Ambitious thoughts plan impossible wonders: how these useless, weak fingernails could tear a way through the flinty ribs of this hard world, my rough prison walls; and because they can't, they die in their own pride. Thoughts that tend towards contentment flatter themselves that they are not the first slaves of fortune and won't be the last, like simple beggars sitting in the stocks who comfort their shame by thinking that many have sat there before and others will have to. And in this thought they find a kind of relief, putting their own bad luck on the backs of those who have suffered the same before. So I play many people in one person, and none of them is content. Sometimes I am a king. Then treasons make me wish I were a beggar, and so I am. Then crushing poverty convinces me I was better off as a king; then I am king again; and soon I think that Bolingbroke has unkinged me, and at once I am nothing. But whatever I am, neither I nor any other man who is only a man will be pleased with anything until he is relieved by being nothing. Do I hear music? Ha, ha! Keep time. How sour sweet music is when the rhythm is broken and the beat isn't kept! It's the same in the music of men's lives. And here I have a fine enough ear to correct a broken rhythm on an out-of-tune string; but for the harmony of my kingship and my time, I had no ear to hear my true time breaking. I wasted time, and now time wastes me, because now time has made me his clock. My thoughts are minutes, and with sighs they tick them on to my eyes, the outer clock face, where my finger, like the hand of a clock, keeps pointing as it wipes away tears. Now, sir, the sound that tells the hour is loud groans that strike my heart, which is the bell. So sighs, tears and groans show minutes, quarters and hours. But my time runs on fast in Bolingbroke's proud joy, while I stand here playing the fool, his little figure that strikes the clock. This music is driving me mad. Let it play no more. Though it has helped madmen back to their senses, in me it seems it will make wise men mad. Yet bless the heart of the person who plays it for me, because it is a sign of love, and love for Richard is a rare jewel in this all-hating world."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Groom of the stable","id":669},{"t":"sp","who":"GROOM","lines":["Hail, royal Prince!"],"id":670,"m":"Hail, royal Prince!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Thanks, noble peer;","The cheapest of us is ten groats too dear.","What art thou? and how comest thou hither, man,","Where no man never comes but that sad dog","That brings me food to make misfortune live?"],"id":671,"m":"Thank you, noble peer. The cheaper of us is overpriced by ten groats. Who are you? And how have you got here, man, where no one ever comes except that miserable dog who brings me food to keep misfortune alive?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GROOM","lines":["I was a poor groom of thy stable, king,","When thou wert king; who, travelling towards York,","With much ado at length have gotten leave","To look upon my sometimes royal master's face.","O! how it yearn'd my heart when I beheld,","In London streets, that coronation day,","When Bolingbroke rode on roan Barbary,","That horse that thou so often hast bestrid,","That horse that I so carefully have dress'd."],"id":672,"m":"I was a poor groom in your stable, king, when you were king. I am travelling to York, and with a lot of trouble I have at last got permission to see the face of my former royal master. Oh, how it hurt my heart to see, in the streets of London, on that coronation day, Bolingbroke riding on roan Barbary, the horse you rode so often, the horse I groomed so carefully!"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Rode he on Barbary? Tell me, gentle friend,","How went he under him?"],"id":673,"m":"Did he ride on Barbary? Tell me, gentle friend, how did the horse go under him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GROOM","lines":["So proudly as if he disdain'd the ground."],"id":674,"m":"As proudly as if he scorned the ground."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["So proud that Bolingbroke was on his back!","That jade hath eat bread from my royal hand;","This hand hath made him proud with clapping him.","Would he not stumble? would he not fall down,—","Since pride must have a fall,—and break the neck","Of that proud man that did usurp his back?","Forgiveness, horse! Why do I rail on thee,","Since thou, created to be aw'd by man,","Wast born to bear? I was not made a horse;","And yet I bear a burden like an ass,","Spur-gall'd and tir'd by jauncing Bolingbroke."],"id":675,"m":"So proud that Bolingbroke was on his back! That nag has eaten bread from my royal hand. This hand made him proud by patting him. Wouldn't he stumble? Wouldn't he fall down, since pride must have a fall, and break the neck of that proud man who stole his back? Forgive me, horse! Why do I abuse you, when you were created to be ruled by man and born to carry? I was not made a horse, and yet I carry a load like a donkey, rubbed raw by spurs and worn out by Bolingbroke bouncing on my back."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Keeper, with a dish","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["[To the Groom] Fellow, give place; here is no longer stay."],"id":677,"m":"You, make way. You can't stay here any longer."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["If thou love me, 'tis time thou wert away."],"id":678,"m":"If you love me, it's time you went."},{"t":"sp","who":"GROOM","lines":["My tongue dares not, that my heart shall say."],"id":679,"m":"What my tongue doesn't dare say, my heart will."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":680},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["My lord, will't please you to fall to?"],"id":681,"m":"My lord, would you like to start eating?"},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["Taste of it first as thou art wont to do."],"id":682,"m":"Taste it first, as you usually do."},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["My lord, I dare not: Sir Pierce of Exton,","Who lately came from the king, commands the contrary."],"id":683,"m":"My lord, I don't dare. Sir Pierce of Exton, who has just come from the king, orders me not to."},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["The devil take Henry of Lancaster and thee!","Patience is stale, and I am weary of it."],"id":684,"m":"The devil take Henry of Lancaster, and you! Patience has gone stale, and I'm tired of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes the Keeper","id":685},{"t":"sp","who":"KEEPER","lines":["Help, help, help!"],"id":686,"m":"Help, help, help!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EXTON and Servants, armed","id":687},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","lines":["How now! What means death in this rude assault?","Villain, thy own hand yields thy death's instrument."],"id":688,"m":"What's this! What does death mean by this violent attack? Villain, your own hand gives me the weapon for your death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Snatching a weapon and killing one","id":689},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["Go thou and fill another room in hell."],"id":690,"m":"Go and fill another room in hell."},{"t":"sd","text":"He kills another, then EXTON strikes him down","id":691},{"t":"sp","who":"RICHARD","cont":1,"lines":["That hand shall burn in never-quenching fire","That staggers thus my person. Exton, thy fierce hand","Hath with the king's blood stain'd the king's own land.","Mount, mount, my soul! thy seat is up on high;","Whilst my gross flesh sinks downward, here to die."],"id":692,"m":"The hand that knocks me down like this will burn in a fire that never goes out. Exton, your fierce hand has stained the king's own land with the king's blood. Rise, rise, my soul! Your seat is up high, while my heavy flesh sinks down to die here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies","id":693},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["As full of valour as of royal blood:","Both have I spilt; O! would the deed were good;","For now the devil, that told me I did well,","Says that this deed is chronicled in hell.","This dead king to the living king I'll bear.","Take hence the rest, and give them burial here."],"id":694,"m":"As full of courage as of royal blood. I have spilled both. Oh, I wish the deed were good! Because now the devil, who told me I did well, says that this deed is recorded in hell. I'll take this dead king to the living king. Take the rest away and bury them here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":695},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"VI","loc":"Windsor. An Apartment in the Castle.","id":696},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter BOLINGBROKE and YORK, with Lords and Attendants","id":697},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Kind uncle York, the latest news we hear","Is that the rebels have consum'd with fire","Our town of Cicester in Gloucestershire;","But whether they be ta'en or slain we hear not."],"id":698,"m":"Kind uncle York, the latest news we have is that the rebels have burned down our town of Cirencester in Gloucestershire; but we haven't heard whether they have been captured or killed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter NORTHUMBERLAND","id":699},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","cont":1,"lines":["Welcome, my lord. What is the news?"],"id":700,"m":"Welcome, my lord. What is the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","lines":["First, to thy sacred state wish I all happiness.","The next news is: I have to London sent","The heads of Salisbury, Spencer, Blunt, and Kent.","The manner of their taking may appear","At large discoursed in this paper here."],"id":701,"m":"First, I wish your sacred majesty every happiness. The next news is that I have sent the heads of Salisbury, Spencer, Blunt and Kent to London. How they were captured is described in full in this paper here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["We thank thee, gentle Percy, for thy pains;","And to thy worth will add right worthy gains."],"id":702,"m":"Thank you, noble Percy, for your trouble. I will reward your worth with gifts that match it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FITZWATER","id":703},{"t":"sp","who":"FITZWATER","lines":["My lord, I have from Oxford sent to London","The heads of Brocas and Sir Bennet Seely,","Two of the dangerous consorted traitors","That sought at Oxford thy dire overthrow."],"id":704,"m":"My lord, I have sent from Oxford to London the heads of Brocas and Sir Bennet Seely, two of the dangerous conspirators who planned your terrible downfall at Oxford."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Thy pains, Fitzwater, shall not be forgot;","Right noble is thy merit, well I wot."],"id":705,"m":"Your efforts won't be forgotten, Fitzwater. Your service is truly noble, I know."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter HENRY PERCY, With the BISHOP OF CARLISLE","id":706},{"t":"sp","who":"PERCY","lines":["The grand conspirator, Abbot of Westminster,","With clog of conscience and sour melancholy,","Hath yielded up his body to the grave;","But here is Carlisle living, to abide","Thy kingly doom, and sentence of his pride."],"id":707,"m":"The chief conspirator, the Abbot of Westminster, weighed down by his conscience and bitter depression, has given up his body to the grave. But here is Carlisle, alive, to wait for your royal judgement and the sentence his pride deserves."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Carlisle, this is your doom:","Choose out some secret place, some reverend room,","More than thou hast, and with it joy thy life;","So as thou livest in peace, die free from strife;","For though mine enemy thou hast ever been,","High sparks of honour in thee have I seen."],"id":708,"m":"Carlisle, this is your sentence: choose some quiet place, some religious house, holier than the one you have, and enjoy your life there. As long as you live in peace, you can die free from trouble. Though you have always been my enemy, I have seen high sparks of honour in you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter EXTON, with attendants, bearing a coffin","id":709},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["Great king, within this coffin I present","Thy buried fear: herein all breathless lies","The mightiest of thy greatest enemies,","Richard of Bordeaux, by me hither brought."],"id":710,"m":"Great king, in this coffin I present your buried fear. Here lies, without breath, the mightiest of your great enemies, Richard of Bordeaux, brought here by me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["Exton, I thank thee not; for thou hast wrought","A deed of slander with thy fatal hand","Upon my head and all this famous land."],"id":711,"m":"Exton, I don't thank you, because with your deadly hand you have done a deed that will bring disgrace on my head and on all this famous country."},{"t":"sp","who":"EXTON","lines":["From your own mouth, my lord, did I this deed."],"id":712,"m":"I did this deed on the orders of your own mouth, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOLINGBROKE","lines":["They love not poison that do poison need,","Nor do I thee: though I did wish him dead,","I hate the murderer, love him murdered.","The guilt of conscience take thou for thy labour,","But neither my good word nor princely favour:","With Cain go wander thorough shade of night,","And never show thy head by day nor light.","Lords, I protest my soul is full of woe,","That blood should sprinkle me to make me grow:","Come, mourn with me for what I do lament,","And put on sullen black incontinent.","I'll make a voyage to the Holy Land,","To wash this blood off from my guilty hand.","March sadly after; grace my mournings here,","In weeping after this untimely bier."],"id":713,"m":"Those who need poison don't love it, and I don't love you. Though I wished him dead, I hate the murderer and love the murdered man. Take a guilty conscience as your reward for your work, but not my good word or royal favour. Go and wander with Cain through the shadows of night, and never show your face by day or in light. Lords, I swear my soul is full of sorrow that blood should be sprinkled on me to make me grow. Come, mourn with me for what I mourn, and put on gloomy black at once. I'll make a voyage to the Holy Land to wash this blood off my guilty hand. March sadly after me. Honour my mourning here by weeping as you follow this coffin of a man who died before his time."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":714}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Richard","group":"The royal family","desc":"King Richard the Second, grandson of Edward III and son of the Black Prince. Graceful, eloquent and sure that God made him king, he rules carelessly: he has his uncle Gloucester killed, banishes Bolingbroke, leases out the kingdom and seizes Gaunt's lands. Once his power collapses he becomes a poet of his own fall, gives up the crown in Westminster Hall and is murdered at Pomfret."},{"name":"Bolingbroke","group":"The royal family","desc":"Henry Bolingbroke, Duke of Hereford, Gaunt's son and Richard's cousin. Banished after his quarrel with Mowbray, he returns to claim his inheritance as Duke of Lancaster and ends up as King Henry IV. He says little about his aims, is popular with the people and acts decisively. He wishes Richard dead, then rejects the man who kills him and vows a crusade to wash off the guilt."},{"name":"Gaunt","group":"The royal family","desc":"John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, Richard's uncle and Bolingbroke's father. Loyal to the idea of a king chosen by God, he will not avenge his brother Gloucester and agrees to his own son's exile. Dying, he praises England as 'this sceptred isle' and tells Richard he is 'landlord of England', not king."},{"name":"York","group":"The royal family","desc":"Edmund of Langley, Duke of York, the last of Edward III's sons and Richard's uncle. Made governor of England while Richard is in Ireland, he cannot resist Bolingbroke and declares himself neutral. He describes Richard's humiliating entry into London and, loyal to the new king, denounces his own son for treason."},{"name":"Duchess of York","group":"The royal family","desc":"York's wife and Aumerle's mother. When York discovers their son's plot, she fights for Aumerle's life, rides after her husband and kneels before Bolingbroke until he grants a pardon."},{"name":"Aumerle","group":"The royal family","desc":"York's son and Richard's loyal cousin. Accused in Westminster Hall of helping to kill Gloucester, he loses his title (he becomes Earl of Rutland) and joins a plot to kill the new king. His father exposes him; his mother wins his pardon."},{"name":"Queen","group":"The royal family","desc":"Richard's French-born wife. Full of a sorrow she cannot name before the bad news comes, she hears the truth from a gardener, curses him and travels to London. In their parting she wants Richard to rage like a dying lion; he sends her to France."},{"name":"Duchess of Gloucester","group":"The royal family","desc":"The widow of Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester, murdered on Richard's orders. She begs Gaunt for revenge, then goes home to Plashy, where she dies of grief offstage; York learns of it when he sends there for money."},{"name":"Mowbray","group":"The nobles","desc":"Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk. Bolingbroke accuses him of stealing soldiers' pay and murdering Gloucester; he admits only to neglecting his duty. Banished for life, he mourns the loss of his English tongue and warns that the king will regret it. He dies in Venice after fighting as a crusader."},{"name":"Northumberland","group":"The rebel lords","desc":"Henry Percy, Earl of Northumberland, the first great lord to join Bolingbroke, and brother of the Earl of Worcester. He is blunt, efficient and unmoved: he drops Richard's title, demands he read out his crimes and separates him from the Queen. Richard predicts he and Bolingbroke will turn on each other."},{"name":"Percy","group":"The rebel lords","desc":"Harry Percy, Northumberland's son (Hotspur in the next plays). Young and eager, he offers his service to Bolingbroke and later brings news of the rebels' defeat."},{"name":"Ross","group":"The rebel lords","desc":"A nobleman who complains of Richard's taxes and rides with Northumberland to join Bolingbroke."},{"name":"Willoughby","group":"The rebel lords","desc":"A nobleman who joins Ross and Northumberland in supporting Bolingbroke."},{"name":"Worcester","group":"The rebel lords","desc":"Thomas Percy, Earl of Worcester, Northumberland's brother and Richard's household steward. He never appears: in 2.2 news comes that he has broken his staff of office and taken the royal servants over to Bolingbroke."},{"name":"Bushy","group":"Richard's favourites","desc":"One of Richard's favourites. He tries to cheer the Queen with clever talk about shadows of grief, then flees to Bristol, where Bolingbroke has him executed."},{"name":"Bagot","group":"Richard's favourites","desc":"One of Richard's favourites. He escapes to Ireland but is taken; brought out as a prisoner in Westminster Hall, he accuses Aumerle of plotting Gloucester's death."},{"name":"Green","group":"Richard's favourites","desc":"One of Richard's favourites. He brings the Queen the news of Bolingbroke's landing and is executed with Bushy at Bristol."},{"name":"Carlisle","group":"Richard's loyal followers","desc":"The Bishop of Carlisle. He urges Richard to act rather than despair and, in Westminster Hall, boldly declares that no subject can judge a king and prophesies civil war. Arrested and joined to the plot, he is spared by Bolingbroke for his honour."},{"name":"Scroop","group":"Richard's loyal followers","desc":"Sir Stephen Scroop, who brings Richard the worst news in 3.2: the whole country has risen, his favourites are dead and York has joined Bolingbroke."},{"name":"Salisbury","group":"Richard's loyal followers","desc":"The Earl of Salisbury, who waits in vain with the Welsh army and arrives one day too late. He is later executed as a rebel against Henry."},{"name":"Abbot","group":"Richard's loyal followers","desc":"The Abbot of Westminster, who begins the plot against Bolingbroke after the deposition. He dies before he can be punished."},{"name":"Exton","group":"Others","desc":"Sir Pierce of Exton, who takes Bolingbroke's hint and murders Richard at Pomfret, then finds himself rejected and banished."},{"name":"Fitzwater","group":"Others","desc":"A lord who accuses Aumerle in Westminster Hall and later sends rebels' heads to London."},{"name":"Surrey","group":"Others","desc":"A lord who defends Aumerle against Fitzwater in Westminster Hall."},{"name":"Gardener","group":"Others","desc":"The Duke of York's head gardener at Langley. He compares good government to pruning and weeding, gives the Queen the news of Richard's capture and plants rue where she wept."},{"name":"Groom","group":"Others","desc":"A poor groom from Richard's stables who visits him in prison and tells him Bolingbroke rode his horse, roan Barbary, at the coronation."},{"name":"Keeper","group":"Others","desc":"Richard's jailer at Pomfret, who will not taste his food on Exton's orders."},{"name":"Marshal","group":"Others","desc":"The Lord Marshal, who runs the ceremony of the combat at Coventry."},{"name":"Berkeley","group":"Others","desc":"A lord sent by York to ask why Bolingbroke has come back armed."},{"name":"Captain","group":"Others","desc":"The captain of the Welsh army, who disbands his men because he believes Richard is dead."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In his London palace, King Richard hears Henry Bolingbroke accuse Thomas Mowbray of embezzling soldiers' pay, of plotting every treason for eighteen years and of murdering the Duke of Gloucester. Mowbray denies it and calls Bolingbroke a liar. Both throw down their gloves. Richard and Gaunt fail to make them pick the challenges back up, so Richard orders them to settle it by combat at Coventry on St Lambert's Day.","key":["Bolingbroke's charge about Gloucester's death points, unspoken, at Richard himself, who ordered it.","Honour is everything to these noblemen: Mowbray says without 'spotless reputation' men are 'gilded loam'.","Richard cannot make his subjects obey him: 'We were not born to sue, but to command' is said after they refuse."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Bolingbroke accuse Mowbray of?","o":["Plotting with the French","Stealing soldiers' pay and murdering Gloucester","Insulting the Queen","Leaving his post at Calais"],"a":1},{"q":"How do the two men accept each other's challenge?","o":["By shaking hands","By throwing down their gloves (gages)","By drawing swords","By signing a paper"],"a":1},{"q":"Where must the combat take place?","o":["London","Windsor","Coventry","Calais"],"a":2}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"The widowed Duchess of Gloucester begs John of Gaunt to avenge his brother's murder. Gaunt refuses: the king, God's anointed deputy, caused the death, so only God may punish it. The Duchess hopes Bolingbroke will kill Mowbray at Coventry, then says a long farewell and goes home to die of grief.","key":["The scene tells us plainly that Richard was behind Gloucester's murder.","Gaunt states the doctrine of divine right: a subject must never raise 'an angry arm' against God's minister.","The Duchess calls Gaunt's patience 'pale cold cowardice', a charge that hangs over every loyal subject in the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why won't Gaunt take revenge for Gloucester?","o":["He thinks Gloucester was guilty","The king caused the death, and only God can punish a king","He is too ill","He fears Mowbray"],"a":1},{"q":"What image does the Duchess use for Edward III's seven sons?","o":["Seven stars","Seven vials of blood and seven branches","Seven swords","Seven towers"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Duchess say she will do?","o":["Go to Coventry","Raise an army","Go home and die of grief","Go to France"],"a":2}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"At Coventry the two champions swear their cases, say farewell and prepare to charge. At the last moment Richard throws down his staff and stops the fight. He banishes Bolingbroke for ten years and Mowbray for life. Seeing Gaunt's grief, he cuts Bolingbroke's sentence to six years. Mowbray warns that the king will regret it. Gaunt tries to comfort his son, but Bolingbroke leaves as 'a true-born Englishman'.","key":["Richard's sudden stop of the combat is theatrical: he grabs the spotlight and avoids a verdict that might expose him.","'Such is the breath of kings': one word from Richard cuts four years from an exile.","Bolingbroke refuses Gaunt's comfort; imagination cannot cure real pain."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Richard stop the combat?","o":["He shouts 'Hold!'","He throws down his warder (staff)","He sends soldiers in","He leaves the lists"],"a":1},{"q":"How long is Mowbray banished?","o":["Six years","Ten years","For life","Two years"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Richard shorten Bolingbroke's exile?","o":["Bolingbroke apologises","He sees Gaunt's grief","The nobles demand it","He needs Bolingbroke in Ireland"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Mowbray miss most in exile?","o":["His lands","His wife","His native English language","His horses"],"a":2}]},"a1s4":{"sum":"Back at court Richard mocks Bolingbroke's popularity with the common people. He announces he will fight the Irish rebels in person, paying for the war by leasing out the kingdom and forcing the rich to sign blank charters. When news comes that Gaunt is dying, Richard hopes he dies quickly so he can seize his wealth.","key":["Richard has noticed that Bolingbroke is courting the people, but does nothing about it.","Leasing the realm and 'blank charters' show a king exploiting his own country.","Richard's wish for Gaunt's quick death is the coldest moment in the first act."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Bolingbroke do as he left England?","o":["Cursed the king","Courted the common people","Hid his face","Took ships to Ireland"],"a":1},{"q":"How will Richard pay for the Irish war?","o":["Selling the crown jewels","Leasing the realm and using blank charters","Borrowing from France","Taxing the church"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard pray for when he hears Gaunt is ill?","o":["His recovery","His quick death","His forgiveness","A last meeting with his son"],"a":1}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"The dying Gaunt hopes to warn Richard. He praises England as 'this sceptred isle' but mourns that it is now 'leased out'. When Richard arrives, Gaunt tells him he is 'landlord of England', not king. Richard rages; Gaunt is carried off and dies. Richard seizes all Gaunt's property. York protests that this attacks the very law of inheritance that makes Richard king, then leaves. Richard makes York governor and sails for Ireland. Northumberland, Ross and Willoughby agree that Bolingbroke is already on his way back with an army, and ride to join him.","key":["Gaunt's speech is the play's great picture of England, and his warning comes true.","Seizing Bolingbroke's inheritance is Richard's fatal mistake: as York says, it undoes the principle of 'fair sequence and succession'.","The nobles' grievances (taxes, fines, flatterers) show how far support for Richard has fallen."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Gaunt call Richard?","o":["Tyrant of England","Landlord of England","Lord of misrule","Shepherd of England"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard do as soon as Gaunt dies?","o":["Calls Bolingbroke home","Seizes Gaunt's goods and lands","Declares a day of mourning","Pardons Mowbray"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is made governor of England while Richard is in Ireland?","o":["Northumberland","Bushy","York","Aumerle"],"a":2},{"q":"Where is Bolingbroke said to be landing?","o":["Dover","Ravenspurgh","Bristol","Milford Haven"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"The Queen feels a nameless grief. Bushy tries to explain it away, but Green brings news that Bolingbroke has landed and the northern lords have joined him. York arrives, overwhelmed. A servant tells him the Duchess of Gloucester has died. York does not know whether to serve the king or help his wronged nephew. Bushy, Bagot and Green split up to save themselves, fearing they will never meet again.","key":["The Queen's foreboding 'nothing' turns into terrible 'something' within the scene.","York is torn between duty to the king and conscience for Bolingbroke: 'Both are my kinsmen'.","The favourites run: the king's friends are friends only in good times."],"quiz":[{"q":"What news does Green bring?","o":["Richard has won in Ireland","Bolingbroke has landed and lords have joined him","The Queen must go to France","York has rebelled"],"a":1},{"q":"Who has died, according to York's servant?","o":["Gaunt","The Duchess of Gloucester","Mowbray","The Earl of Wiltshire"],"a":1},{"q":"Where do Bushy and Green go?","o":["Ireland","Bristol Castle","Flint Castle","France"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Bolingbroke marches through Gloucestershire with Northumberland, who flatters him. Young Harry Percy pledges his service, and Ross and Willoughby arrive. York confronts his nephew and calls him a traitor, but Bolingbroke insists he has come only for his inheritance as Duke of Lancaster. York admits he cannot resist and declares himself neutral, inviting them to rest at Berkeley Castle. Bolingbroke vows to root out Bushy, Bagot and the other 'caterpillars of the commonwealth'.","key":["Bolingbroke's careful claim: 'I come for Lancaster', not for the crown.","York's neutrality is really surrender; the king's last defender at home gives way.","Bolingbroke is already acting like a ruler, promising rewards and punishing the king's friends."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who pledges his service to Bolingbroke for the first time?","o":["Aumerle","Harry Percy","Scroop","Bagot"],"a":1},{"q":"What reason does Bolingbroke give for returning?","o":["To be king","To claim his rights as Duke of Lancaster","To fight Mowbray","To rescue the Queen"],"a":1},{"q":"What does York decide?","o":["To fight Bolingbroke","To flee to Ireland","To stay neutral","To arrest Northumberland"],"a":2}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"In Wales the Earl of Salisbury begs a Welsh captain to wait one more day for the king. The captain refuses: omens show the king is dead, and his men have already gone. Salisbury sees Richard's glory falling like a shooting star.","key":["A delay of one day costs Richard his Welsh army.","Omens (withered bay trees, meteors, a bloody moon) frame the fall as cosmic.","Salisbury's shooting star begins the play's pattern of Richard as a setting sun."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the Welsh soldiers leave?","o":["They are not paid","They believe Richard is dead","Bolingbroke bribes them","Salisbury dismisses them"],"a":1},{"q":"Which is NOT an omen the captain mentions?","o":["Withered bay trees","A bloody moon","A comet over London","Meteors frightening the stars"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Salisbury compare Richard's glory to?","o":["A falling star","A broken sword","A sinking ship","A burnt-out candle"],"a":0}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"At Bristol, Bolingbroke sentences Bushy and Green to death. He lists their crimes: misleading the king, coming between Richard and the Queen, and feeding on Bolingbroke's own estates while he was in exile. They are led out to execution. Bolingbroke sends kind greetings to the Queen, then marches against Glendower.","key":["Bolingbroke acts as judge and executioner, with no royal authority.","He blames the favourites rather than Richard, keeping up the appearance of loyalty.","His personal grievance (his woods felled, his coat of arms torn down) shows identity is bound up with land and name."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who are executed at Bristol?","o":["Bagot and Green","Bushy and Green","Bushy and Bagot","Wiltshire and Scroop"],"a":1},{"q":"Which charge does Bolingbroke bring against them?","o":["Poisoning Gaunt","Coming between the king and queen","Murdering Gloucester","Selling the crown jewels"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Bolingbroke march against next?","o":["Richard","Glendower","Mowbray","The French"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Richard lands in Wales and greets the earth of England. He claims that no rebel can depose an anointed king and that God sends angels to fight for him. Then bad news falls blow by blow: the Welsh have gone, Bushy, Green and Wiltshire are executed, York has joined Bolingbroke. Richard swings between confidence and despair, sits on the ground to 'tell sad stories of the death of kings', and finally dismisses his army and goes to Flint Castle.","key":["Richard's belief in divine right is total, and it does not save him.","His moods swing wildly with each piece of news, which is both moving and a sign of weakness as a leader.","The 'hollow crown' speech shows Richard discovering he is a mortal man like his subjects."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Richard land?","o":["Dover","The coast of Wales","Bristol","Ravenspurgh"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard say God provides for every rebel soldier?","o":["A plague","A glorious angel","A thunderbolt","A storm at sea"],"a":1},{"q":"Who sits in the hollow crown, according to Richard?","o":["Bolingbroke","Death","Fortune","God"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Richard go at the end of the scene?","o":["Ireland","London","Flint Castle","Pomfret"],"a":2}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Bolingbroke's army reaches Flint Castle. He sends Northumberland to tell Richard he will kneel and lay down his arms if his banishment is lifted and his lands returned. Richard appears on the walls, still majestic, and warns of the bloodshed his overthrow will bring, but then agrees to everything. Alone with Aumerle he imagines giving up his kingdom for a little grave. He comes down to the 'base court', where Bolingbroke kneels, and agrees to go with him to London.","key":["Bolingbroke still kneels and asks only for his own, but his army does the talking.","Richard gives in before he is asked: he names deposition himself.","'Down, down I come': the king's physical descent from the walls stages his fall."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Bolingbroke say he wants?","o":["The crown","His banishment ended and his lands restored","Richard's head","Marriage to the Queen"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Richard first appear?","o":["At the gate","On the castle walls","In the courtyard","On a ship"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard compare himself to as he comes down?","o":["Icarus","Phaethon","A falling leaf","A sunset"],"a":1}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"At York's house in Langley, the Queen tries to distract herself with her ladies. She hides when gardeners arrive. The head gardener tells his men to trim the overgrown plants and root out the weeds, as Richard should have done with his kingdom. He reveals that Bolingbroke has seized the king and that Bushy and Green are dead. The Queen bursts out, curses the gardener and leaves for London. The gardener plants rue where her tear fell.","key":["The garden is England in miniature: good government means pruning, weeding and order.","Ordinary working men know the news before the Queen does.","The gardener answers her curse with pity: rue, the 'herb of grace'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the Queen refuse to play bowls?","o":["It is too hot","The 'rubs' remind her of her bad fortune","She is too tired","Her ladies cheat"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the gardener say Richard failed to do?","o":["Water his garden","Prune and weed his kingdom","Plant new trees","Pay his gardeners"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the gardener plant where the Queen wept?","o":["Roses","Rue","Rosemary","Lilies"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"In Westminster Hall nobles hurl accusations and gloves over Gloucester's murder, with Aumerle at the centre. Carlisle reports that Mowbray has died abroad. York announces that Richard will hand over the crown, and Bolingbroke agrees to take it. Carlisle protests that subjects cannot judge a king and prophesies civil war; he is arrested. Richard is brought in. He hands over the crown, 'undoes' himself, refuses to read the list of his crimes, calls for a mirror and smashes it. He is sent to the Tower. The Abbot, Carlisle and Aumerle begin a plot against the new king.","key":["The deposition scene was cut from the early printed texts: deposing a monarch was dangerous to stage.","Carlisle's prophecy of Golgotha looks ahead to the Wars of the Roses.","Richard takes control of his own fall, turning the ceremony into a performance only he can direct."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who prophesies that England will become 'the field of Golgotha'?","o":["York","Carlisle","Aumerle","The Abbot"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens to Carlisle after his speech?","o":["He is banished","He is arrested for treason","He is made Archbishop","He leaves the hall"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard do with the mirror?","o":["Gives it to Bolingbroke","Hides it","Smashes it on the ground","Asks for another"],"a":2},{"q":"What do the Abbot and Aumerle plan at the end?","o":["To flee to France","A plot against the new king","To free Mowbray","To crown Aumerle"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"The Queen waits in the street for Richard to pass on his way to the Tower. She is shocked at how meekly he accepts his fate. He tells her to go to France and tell his sad story. Northumberland arrives: Richard is to go to Pomfret instead, and the Queen to France. Richard warns Northumberland that he and Bolingbroke will turn on each other. The couple say a last farewell with two kisses.","key":["The Queen wants Richard to fight back like a dying lion; he has accepted 'grim Necessity'.","Richard's prophecy about Northumberland will come true in the next plays.","The farewell, full of rhyme and wordplay, is the emotional heart of the last act."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where was Richard first being taken?","o":["Pomfret","The Tower","Windsor","Flint"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Richard tell the Queen to do in France?","o":["Raise an army","Remarry","Tell his sad story by the fire","Forget him"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Richard predict about Northumberland?","o":["He will become king","He and Bolingbroke will turn against each other","He will die in battle","He will be banished"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"York tells his wife how Bolingbroke rode into London cheered by crowds while dust was thrown on Richard's head. Their son Aumerle, now demoted to Rutland, arrives with a sealed paper. York snatches it and finds a plot to kill the new king at Oxford. York calls for his boots to ride and denounce him; the Duchess begs him to protect their only son, then sends Aumerle to reach the king first and follows herself.","key":["York's account turns the crowd's switch of loyalty into a theatre audience bored by the second actor.","York chooses loyalty to the new king over his own son.","The scene is close to comedy, with boots, a frantic chase and a struggling servant, before a deadly serious scene."],"quiz":[{"q":"How did the London crowd treat Richard?","o":["They cheered him","They threw dust on his head","They tried to rescue him","They ignored him completely"],"a":1},{"q":"What does York find in the paper?","o":["A love letter","A debt for fine clothes","A plot to kill the king at Oxford","A pardon"],"a":2},{"q":"What is Aumerle's new title?","o":["Rutland","Exeter","Albemarle","Surrey"],"a":0}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Bolingbroke, now King Henry, worries about his wild son, Prince Hal. Aumerle rushes in, locks the door and begs a pardon. York hammers on the door and warns the king. The Duchess arrives and the family kneel: York asking for punishment, the Duchess and Aumerle for mercy. Bolingbroke pardons Aumerle but orders death for the other conspirators.","key":["The new king already has a troubled heir: the 'unthrifty son' is the future Henry V.","Bolingbroke calls the scene 'The Beggar and the King', a joke that lightens a dangerous moment.","Mercy to Aumerle and death to the rest show Bolingbroke as a shrewd ruler."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Bolingbroke complain about at the start?","o":["Northumberland","His unthrifty son","Carlisle","Exton"],"a":1},{"q":"Who kneels against the pardon?","o":["The Duchess","Percy","York","Aumerle"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Bolingbroke decide?","o":["Pardons everyone","Pardons Aumerle but orders death for the other plotters","Banishes Aumerle","Executes Aumerle"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Sir Pierce of Exton tells a servant that the king twice said 'Have I no friend will rid me of this living fear?' and looked at him meaningfully. Exton decides to go to Pomfret and kill Richard.","key":["Bolingbroke never gives a clear order; the hint is enough.","The short scene echoes Richard's own guilt over Gloucester's death.","Exton thinks he is the king's 'friend', but he will be rejected."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did the king say twice?","o":["'Bring me Richard's head'","'Have I no friend will rid me of this living fear?'","'Let Richard live'","'Go to Pomfret'"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is Richard held?","o":["The Tower","Windsor","Pomfret","Flint"],"a":2}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Alone in his cell at Pomfret, Richard tries to compare his prison to the world, peopling it with his thoughts. Music plays and makes him think of wasted time. A former stable groom visits and tells him Bolingbroke rode Richard's horse, roan Barbary, at the coronation. The keeper brings food but will not taste it. Richard beats him; Exton and his men burst in. Richard kills two of them before Exton strikes him down. Dying, he says his soul will rise. Exton already regrets the deed.","key":["The prison soliloquy is Richard's deepest self-examination: 'Thus play I in one person many people'.","'I wasted time, and now doth time waste me' is his clearest admission of failure.","Richard dies fighting, more like a king than at any earlier point."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Richard try to compare his prison to?","o":["A ship","The world","A garden","A theatre"],"a":1},{"q":"What did Bolingbroke ride at his coronation?","o":["A white horse","Richard's horse, roan Barbary","A borrowed mare","He walked"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Richard die?","o":["Poisoned","Struck down by Exton after killing two men","Starved","He kills himself"],"a":1}]},"a5s6":{"sum":"At Windsor, news arrives that the rebels have been crushed and their heads sent to London. The Abbot of Westminster has died; Bolingbroke spares Carlisle. Exton brings in Richard's coffin. Bolingbroke refuses to thank him, banishes him like Cain and vows to go on crusade to wash the blood from his hands. The court follows the coffin in mourning.","key":["Bolingbroke rejects the murder he wished for: 'I hate the murderer, love him murdered'.","Exton is sent off 'with Cain', echoing Abel's blood in the first scene: the play has come full circle.","The promised crusade looks ahead to Henry IV's guilt and the troubles of his reign."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to Carlisle?","o":["He is executed","He is spared and sent to a religious house","He is banished","He becomes Abbot"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Bolingbroke reward Exton?","o":["With land","With a title","He refuses to thank him and banishes him","He knights him"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Bolingbroke vow to do?","o":["Rebuild Pomfret","Go to the Holy Land","Abdicate","Marry again"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens with ceremony: Richard sits in judgement as God's deputy. Notice how formal and full the titles are.","11":"Bolingbroke's rhyming couplets make his accusation sound like a public performance of honour.","16":"Gloucester's death is the secret at the centre of the play. Richard was behind it, so Bolingbroke's charge is aimed, indirectly, at the king.","19":"Richard promises impartial justice, but he has his own reasons for wanting this quarrel ended quietly.","20":"Mowbray's answer is careful: he did not kill Gloucester but 'neglected my sworn duty', a hint that he was ordered to let it happen.","21":"Richard plays doctor: 'Our doctors say this is no month to bleed.' The joke is that bloodletting will soon be national.","28":"Honour, reputation and 'name' matter more than life to these men.","36":"Woodstock is Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester, the murdered uncle. Gaunt knows who is guilty but leaves it to heaven.","38":"Divine right: the king is 'God's substitute', so even a guilty king must not be resisted. This is Tudor orthodoxy.","43":"Plashy (Pleshey) in Essex was Gloucester's house. Her empty house is a picture of a ruined family.","63":"Richard comes down from his throne to embrace Bolingbroke: an early gesture of descent.","78":"The warder is the king's staff of office. Throwing it down stops the combat.","81":"Richard's long, elaborate sentence about peace in its 'cradle' is typical of his style: beautiful but evasive.","84":"Mowbray's exile is almost a death: his tongue will be imprisoned behind his teeth.","94":"Richard can change years of a man's life with a word, which impresses Bolingbroke.","100":"Gaunt was on the council that agreed to banish his own son; he now regrets it.","115":"Gaunt's advice is to imagine exile is a pleasure trip. Bolingbroke, a practical man, refuses.","128":"Richard has noticed that Bolingbroke 'wooed' the common people. Bolingbroke's popularity will win him the crown.","130":"Farming the realm (leasing out the right to collect taxes) and blank charters were real grievances against Richard.","136":"Richard's cruelty here shocks even an audience that has seen him banish two men.","145":"Gaunt's speech is one long sentence: the subject ('This royal throne... this England') is held off for about twenty lines before the verb 'is now leased out'.","150":"Gaunt plays on his name: gaunt means thin and haggard.","158":"A deposition is foreseen here: Gaunt says Edward III would have deposed Richard before he was born.","160":"The pelican was believed to feed its young with its own blood; Richard is the greedy chick drinking his family's blood.","170":"Kerns were lightly armed Irish foot soldiers. Ireland was said to have no snakes, thanks to St Patrick.","173":"York's argument is the political key: if Richard ignores inheritance, he destroys the principle that makes him king.","177":"Richard leaves York as governor, the man who has just warned him most strongly.","200":"Ravenspurgh was a port on the Humber estuary in Yorkshire. The news comes so soon after Gaunt dies that Bolingbroke must have been preparing before Richard seized his lands.","207":"The Queen's 'nothing' grief is a foreboding: it soon becomes real news.","208":"Perspectives were trick pictures that look distorted head-on and clear from the side.","226":"York's 'Comfort's in heaven' sets the tone of his weary half-loyalty.","235":"York cannot decide between his oath to the king and his conscience. His confused orders show it.","253":"Northumberland's flattery shows how Bolingbroke attracts supporters.","263":"Harry Percy is the Hotspur of Henry IV Part 1, who will one day rebel against Bolingbroke.","285":"York's rebuke is stern, but by the end of the scene he has given in.","288":"Bolingbroke uses the argument York used against Richard: inheritance must be respected.","295":"Caterpillars eat away a plant from within: the favourites are pests in England's garden.","302":"Bay (laurel) trees withering was thought to be an omen of death.","308":"Bolingbroke behaves like a king, sentencing men to death, though he has no legal power to.","321":"Richard talks to the earth and stones. His love for the land is real, though he has abused it.","324":"The balm is the holy oil used to anoint a king at his coronation.","343":"The 'hollow crown' gave its name to a BBC series of the history plays.","358":"York warns Northumberland that leaving out Richard's title would once have cost him his head.","368":"Bolingbroke's message is humble in words and threatening in force: a man kneeling with an army behind him.","372":"On the walls Richard is magnificent, but the threat of plague and bloodshed is all he can offer.","380":"Richard talks himself into deposition before anyone has asked. 'You make a leg': you bow.","382":"Phaethon, son of the sun god, drove the sun's chariot, lost control and was struck down. The base court is the lower courtyard.","414":"The garden is a 'model' of the state, an idea common in Elizabethan writing about government.","417":"A good gardener cuts the bark to slow over-rich growth: Richard should have checked his over-mighty nobles.","426":"Rue was called herb of grace and stood for repentance and pity (ruth).","430":"Act 4 opens with a second appeal scene that mirrors 1.1: accusations about Gloucester, glove after glove.","453":"Mowbray's death abroad means the truth about Gloucester will never be tested.","460":"Carlisle's speech was dangerous on the Elizabethan stage: the deposition section of this scene was left out of the first printed texts.","467":"Richard's 'God save the King! Will no man say amen?' turns the ceremony into a church service with no congregation.","475":"An inverted coronation: Richard removes each sign of kingship in turn.","490":"The mirror shows only a face; Richard wants to see what lies under the title. Breaking it is his last royal act of theatre.","504":"To convey also meant to steal.","515":"Julius Caesar was said to have built the Tower of London.","520":"Richard turns his own story into a tale for winter nights: he is already writing his legend.","523":"Richard's prophecy comes true in the next plays, when the Percys rebel against Henry IV.","547":"The 'well-graced actor' image sums up a play full of theatre and performance.","598":"The 'unthrifty son' is Prince Hal, the future Henry V, hero of the next plays.","630":"'The Beggar and the King' alludes to a ballad, King Cophetua and the Beggar Maid.","646":"'Pardonne-moi' is the French polite refusal; York plays on the word to mean 'no'.","660":"Exton has heard a hint and decided it is an order: compare Henry II and Becket, 'Will no one rid me of this turbulent priest?'","668":"Richard's only true soliloquy. Alone at last, he turns his mind into a crowd of people.","672":"Roan Barbary: a reddish horse, perhaps of Barbary (North African) breed.","692":"Richard dies fighting, for the first time showing physical courage.","713":"Bolingbroke's crusade is never made; in Henry IV he dies in a room called Jerusalem."},"gloss":{"liege":"lord, sovereign","appeal":"formal accusation of treason","appellant":"accuser","sounded":"questioned, tested","inveterate":"long-standing","high-stomach'd":"proud, haughty","ire":"anger","hap":"fortune, luck","miscreant":"villain","gage":"glove thrown down as a challenge","pawn":"pledge, the gage","nobles":"gold coins","lendings":"advance pay","lewd":"wicked, improper","complotted":"plotted together","chastisement":"punishment","pitch":"height a falcon flies to","partialize":"make biased","receipt":"money received","recreant":"coward, traitor","choler":"anger","boot":"use, help","baffled":"publicly disgraced","impeach'd":"accused","sue":"plead, beg","atone":"reconcile","arbitrate":"settle, decide","Woodstock":"Thomas of Woodstock, Duke of Gloucester","vials":"small bottles","vaded":"faded","caitiff":"wretch","career":"charge at a tournament","courser":"war horse","lists":"enclosed ground for a combat","sprightfully":"spiritedly","habiliments":"clothes, equipment","depose":"take an oath from; also remove from the throne","casque":"helmet","amazing":"stunning, bewildering","enfranchisement":"freedom","jocund":"cheerful","warder":"staff of office","dateless":"endless","viol":"stringed instrument","portcullis'd":"barred with a portcullis (a castle gate)","compassionate":"full of self-pity","louring":"dark, threatening","sepulchre":"tomb","party-verdict":"share of the verdict","foil":"metal setting that shows off a jewel","journeyman":"trained workman (no longer an apprentice)","strew'd":"scattered with rushes or flowers","gnarling":"snarling","lanceth":"cuts open","rheum":"watery discharge from the eyes","oyster-wench":"woman who sells oysters","draymen":"cart drivers","reversion":"right to inherit later","expedient":"swift","coffers":"chests of money","largess":"generous gifts","farm":"lease out for rent","blank charters":"signed documents with the amount left blank","unstaid":"uncontrolled","glose":"flatter","lascivious":"lustful","apish":"copycat","cormorant":"greedy sea bird","Mars":"Roman god of war","demi-paradise":"half paradise","Jewry":"Judea","pelting":"paltry, worthless","tenement":"rented land or house","Neptune":"Roman god of the sea","ague":"fever","admonition":"warning","kerns":"Irish foot soldiers","rug-headed":"shaggy-haired","moveables":"personal goods","letters-patents":"royal documents granting rights","livery":"legal possession of an inheritance","homage":"oath of loyalty","bereft":"robbed","gelded":"stripped","patrimony":"inheritance","pill'd":"robbed, stripped","exactions":"forced payments","benevolences":"forced 'gifts' of money to the king","wot":"know","wrack":"wreck, ruin","imp":"repair (a falcon's wing with new feathers)","broking":"pawnbroker's","Ravenspurgh":"port in Yorkshire where Bolingbroke landed","perspectives":"trick pictures","conceit":"fancy, imagination","cozening":"cheating","sirrah":"term for a servant or inferior","posts":"messengers","muster":"gather (troops)","levy":"raise (an army)","delectable":"delightful","beguil'd":"made pass pleasantly","estimate":"reputation","exchequer":"treasury","raze":"scratch out","pricks":"spurs, urges","palsy":"trembling illness","braving":"defiant","unthrifts":"spendthrifts","distrain'd":"seized for debt","abet":"help, encourage","attach":"arrest","neuter":"neutral","complices":"accomplices","caterpillars":"parasites","meteors":"comets, shooting stars","ruffians":"thugs","forerun":"come before, foretell","firmament":"sky, heavens","unfold":"reveal","lineaments":"features","signories":"estates","dispark'd":"turned private parks into open land","impress":"emblem, badge","Glendower":"Owen Glendower, Welsh leader","brooks":"likes, endures","fondly":"tenderly; also foolishly","conjuration":"solemn appeal","remiss":"careless","security":"over-confidence","Antipodes":"the other side of the world","balm":"holy oil used at a coronation","press'd":"forced into military service","shrewd":"harmful","beadsmen":"men paid to pray for someone","yew":"wood used for bows","distaff-women":"women who spin","bills":"long-handled weapons","Judases":"traitors, like Judas","executors":"people who carry out a will","antick":"clown, jester","monarchize":"play the king","humour'd":"amused","beshrew":"curse","parley":"trumpet call for talks","brazen":"made of brass","totter'd":"tattered, crumbling","appointments":"equipment","lime":"mortar","occident":"the west","purple":"blood-red","testament":"will","barbed":"armoured","sooth":"flattery","almsman":"man who lives on charity","palmer":"pilgrim","Phaethon":"son of the sun god who could not control the sun's chariot","manage":"control (of horses)","jades":"worn-out or unruly horses","base court":"lower courtyard","redoubted":"feared, respected","rubs":"obstacles (in bowls)","bias":"weight that curves a bowl's path","apricocks":"apricots","sire":"father","noisome":"harmful","knots":"patterned flower beds","rue":"bitter herb, symbol of pity","ruth":"pity","Golgotha":"the place where Christ was crucified, 'place of a skull'","appeach":"accuse publicly","sureties":"guarantees for a person's appearance","haught":"haughty","sterling":"genuine, of value","plume-pluck'd":"stripped of honours","Pilate":"governor who washed his hands of Christ's death","ravel out":"unravel","pageant":"show","blot":"stain","Hallowmas":"All Saints' Day, 1 November","pines":"wastes, starves","fealty":"loyalty","justs":"jousts","triumphs":"public festivities","dugs":"breasts","teeming date":"time for bearing children","stews":"brothels","wanton":"spoiled, playful","effeminate":"soft, unmanly","bawd":"pimp","digressing":"straying","suppliant":"one who begs","fester'd":"infected","consorted":"conspiring together","wistly":"meaningfully","groats":"small coins","dial":"clock face","jar":"tick","Jack o' the clock":"figure that strikes a clock bell","brooch":"jewel","yearn'd":"grieved","roan":"reddish-brown (horse)","bestrid":"rode","jauncing":"bouncing","Cicester":"Cirencester","doom":"sentence, judgement","incontinent":"at once","bier":"coffin stand","Pomfret":"Pontefract Castle in Yorkshire","Plashy":"Pleshey, Gloucester's house in Essex","Barkloughly":"a castle on the Welsh coast","Flint":"castle in North Wales","Calais":"English port in France","Coventry":"town where the combat is held","grav'd":"buried"}};
const PARTS={"11":[[0,4,"First, and may heaven witness what I say, I come here to accuse him before my prince out of a subject's loving duty, out of care for my king's precious safety, and free of any other, unworthy hatred."],[5,8,"Now, Thomas Mowbray, I turn to you. Note well how I greet you, because what I say my body will prove here on earth, or my immortal soul will answer for it in heaven."],[9,12,"You are a traitor and a villain, too noble to be one and too wicked to live. The clearer and brighter the sky, the uglier the clouds that fly across it look."],[13,16,"And to stress the charge, I'll stuff the name of foul traitor down your throat once more. With my king's permission, before I leave here I want my drawn sword to prove what my tongue says."]],"12":[[0,4,"Don't judge my loyalty by how coolly I speak. A shouting match like two women quarrelling, two bitter, eager tongues, can't settle this matter between us. Hot blood must be spilled and cooled to do that."],[5,10,"But I can't boast of such tame patience as to keep quiet and say nothing at all. First, my respect for your Highness holds me back from giving my speech free rein; otherwise it would gallop on until it had driven these charges of treason back down his throat twice over."],[11,19,"Setting aside his royal blood, and pretending he is no relation of my king, I defy him and spit at him. I call him a slandering coward and a villain. To prove it I would give him the advantage and meet him even if I had to run on foot to the frozen ridges of the Alps, or any other place where no one can live, anywhere an Englishman ever dared to set foot."],[20,21,"Meanwhile, let this defend my loyalty: by all my hopes, he lies completely."]],"13":[[0,3,"Pale, trembling coward, there I throw down my glove. I give up any claim to be the king's relative and put aside my royal blood, which you use as an excuse out of fear, not respect."],[4,8,"If guilty fear has left you strength enough to pick up the pledge of my honour, then bend down. By that glove, and by all the other rituals of knighthood, I will prove against you in single combat what I have said, or anything worse you can think up."]],"16":[[0,4,"Listen: what I say, my life will prove true. Mowbray has received eight thousand gold coins, supposedly as pay for your Highness's soldiers, and has kept them for wicked purposes, like a false traitor and harmful villain."],[5,10,"Besides that, I say, and will prove in battle here or anywhere else as far as any English eye has ever seen, that all the treasons plotted and invented in this country in the last eighteen years started with false Mowbray."],[11,16,"And I say further, and will prove it on his wicked life, that he plotted the Duke of Gloucester's death, stirred up the Duke's too-trusting enemies, and so, like a traitor and coward, let out his innocent soul in streams of blood."],[17,21,"That blood, like the blood of Abel the sacrificer, cries out to me from the silent caves of the earth for justice and harsh punishment. And by my glorious ancestry, this arm will deliver it, or I will die trying."]],"19":[[0,6,"Mowbray, my eyes and ears are fair to both sides. If he were my brother, or even heir to my kingdom, and he is only my uncle's son, I swear by my royal sceptre that such closeness to my sacred blood would not give him any privilege or bend my firm, upright judgment."],[7,8,"He is my subject, Mowbray, and so are you. I allow you to speak freely and without fear."]],"20":[[0,7,"Then, Bolingbroke, you lie, right down your lying throat to your heart. Of the money I received for Calais, I duly paid three quarters to his Highness's soldiers. The rest I kept back with the King's agreement, because he owed me money left over from a large debt from when I last went to France to bring back his queen."],[8,10,"So swallow that lie. As for Gloucester's death, I did not kill him; but to my shame I failed in my sworn duty in that matter."],[11,17,"As for you, my noble Lord of Lancaster, honourable father of my enemy: I did once set an ambush to kill you, a crime that troubles my grieving soul. But before I last took communion I confessed it and begged your Grace's pardon, and I hope I received it."],[18,25,"That is my fault. The rest of the charges come from the hatred of a villain, a coward and a most degenerate traitor. I will boldly defend myself against them, and in return I throw my glove down at this arrogant traitor's foot, to prove myself a loyal gentleman by spilling the best blood in his heart."],[26,27,"To hurry that on, I beg your Highness with all my heart to set the day of our combat."]],"26":[[0,4,"I throw myself at your feet, my feared king. You may command my life, but not my shame. My life I owe to you as my duty; but my good name, which will live on my grave in spite of death, you will not have for dark dishonour."],[5,8,"I am disgraced, accused and humiliated here, stabbed to the soul with the poisoned spear of slander. No medicine can cure it except the blood from the heart of the man who breathed out this poison."]],"28":[[0,4,"Yes, but they don't change their spots. Take away my shame and I'll give up the glove. My dear, dear lord, the purest treasure this life offers is a spotless reputation. Without it, men are just gilded mud or painted clay."],[5,8,"A bold spirit in a loyal heart is a jewel in a chest locked ten times over. My honour is my life; the two grow together as one. Take my honour away and my life is over."],[9,10,"So, my dear lord, let me put my honour to the test. I live for it, and I will die for it."]],"30":[[0,3,"Oh, God keep my soul from such a terrible sin! Should I look defeated in my father's sight, or lower my rank with cowardly fear in front of this beaten coward?"],[4,8,"Before my tongue wounds my honour with such weakness or calls such a shameful truce, my teeth will tear out the cowardly tongue that would take it back and spit it, bleeding, at the place where shame lives: in Mowbray's face."]],"32":[[0,7,"I was not born to plead, but to command. Since I can't make you friends, be ready, on pain of death, at Coventry on St Lambert's Day. There your swords and lances will settle the swelling difference of your fixed hatred. Since I cannot reconcile you, we'll see justice declare the winner by knightly combat."],[8,9,"Lord Marshal, order my heralds to be ready to manage this quarrel at home."]],"37":[[0,6,"Doesn't being his brother spur you on more sharply than that? Is there no living fire of love in your old blood? Edward's seven sons, and you are one of them, were like seven bottles of his sacred blood, or seven fine branches growing from one root. Some of the seven have dried up naturally; some of the branches have been cut by fate."],[7,12,"But Thomas, my dear husband, my life, my Gloucester, one bottle full of Edward's sacred blood, one flourishing branch of his royal root, has been cracked and all its precious liquid spilled, has been hacked down and all his summer leaves faded, by the hand of malice and the bloody axe of murder."],[13,19,"Ah, Gaunt, his blood was yours! The same bed, the same womb, the same metal and the same mould that made you made him a man. Though you live and breathe, you are killed in him. You partly agree to your own father's death by watching your poor brother die, who was the image of your father's life."],[20,25,"Don't call it patience, Gaunt; it is despair. By letting your brother be slaughtered like this, you show the open road to your own life and teach cruel murder how to butcher you. What we call patience in common men is pale, cold cowardice in noble ones."],[26,27,"What more can I say? The best way to protect your own life is to avenge my Gloucester's death."]],"41":[[0,4,"Then I will. Goodbye, old Gaunt. You are going to Coventry to watch our cousin Hereford and cruel Mowbray fight. Oh, may my husband's wrongs ride on Hereford's spear, so that it goes into butcher Mowbray's heart!"],[5,9,"Or if bad luck makes him miss at the first charge, may Mowbray's sins be so heavy in his heart that they break his foaming horse's back and throw the rider headlong into the arena, a cowardly wretch at my cousin Hereford's mercy!"],[10,11,"Goodbye, old Gaunt. Your dead brother's wife must end her life with her only companion, grief."]],"43":[[0,3,"One more word. Grief bounces back where it falls, not because it is hollow but because it is heavy. I'm saying goodbye before I've even begun, because sorrow doesn't end when it seems to be over."],[4,8,"Give my regards to your brother, Edmund of York. There, that's all. No, don't leave yet; even if that's all, don't go so quickly. I'll remember more. Tell him... ah, what? To visit me at Plashy as soon as he can."],[9,14,"Alas, and what will good old York see there but empty rooms and bare walls, deserted servants' quarters and stones nobody walks on? And what welcome will he hear there but my groans? So give him my regards, but don't let him come there looking for sorrow; it is everywhere."],[15,16,"Desolate, desolate, I will go away and die. My weeping eye takes its last look at you."]],"54":[[0,5,"My name is Thomas Mowbray, Duke of Norfolk. I come here bound by my oath, which God forbid a knight should break, to defend my loyalty and faithfulness to God, my King and my descendants against the Duke of Hereford, who accuses me."],[6,9,"And by the grace of God and this arm of mine, I come to prove, by defending myself, that he is a traitor to my God, my King and me. And as I fight honestly, may heaven defend me!"]],"64":[[0,3,"Oh, let no noble eye waste a tear on me if I am pierced by Mowbray's spear. I fight Mowbray as confidently as a falcon flies at a bird."],[4,7,"My loving lord, I say goodbye to you; and to you, my noble cousin, Lord Aumerle. I am not sick, though I am dealing with death, but strong, young and breathing cheerfully."],[8,9,"Look, as at English feasts, I greet the sweetest dish last, to make the end the best."],[10,18,"Oh, you, my father on earth, whose young spirit, reborn in me, lifts me up with double strength to reach for a victory above my head: strengthen my armour with your prayers, and harden my lance's point with your blessings, so that it pierces Mowbray's armour as if it were wax and polishes up the name of John of Gaunt again through the vigorous deeds of his son."]],"68":[[0,2,"However God or fortune decides my fate, there lives or dies here a loyal, just and honest gentleman, faithful to King Richard's throne."],[3,7,"No prisoner ever threw off his chains and welcomed his golden, unlimited freedom with a happier heart than my dancing soul feels as it celebrates this feast of battle with my enemy."],[8,11,"Most mighty lord, and my fellow noblemen, accept my wish that you may have many happy years. I go to fight as calmly and cheerfully as if I were going to a game. A clear conscience has a peaceful heart."]],"81":[[0,1,"Come closer, and listen to what my council and I have decided."],[2,5,"Because the soil of my kingdom should not be stained with the precious blood it has brought up, and because my eyes hate the terrible sight of wounds of civil war, ploughed open by neighbours' swords,"],[6,15,"and because I think that the proud, high-flying ambition that aims at the sky, together with jealous rivalry, set you on to wake up peace, which lies in our country's cradle breathing the sweet breath of a sleeping baby; and that peace, woken by loud, tuneless drums, the harsh, dreadful blare of trumpets and the grating clash of angry weapons, might be frightened out of our quiet land and make us wade in the blood of our own relatives:"],[16,20,"for these reasons I banish you from my lands. You, cousin Hereford, on pain of death, shall not see my fair kingdom again until ten summers have ripened our fields, but shall walk the foreign roads of exile."]],"84":[[0,4,"A heavy sentence, my most royal lord, and completely unexpected from your Highness's mouth. I have deserved a better reward from your Highness than such a deep wound as to be thrown out into the open world."],[5,11,"Now I must give up the language I have learned for forty years, my native English. My tongue is now no more use to me than a viol or harp without strings, or a fine instrument shut in its case, or, if it is opened, put into the hands of someone who has no idea how to play it."],[12,15,"You have imprisoned my tongue inside my mouth, behind the double gate of my teeth and lips, and dull, unfeeling, empty ignorance has been made my jailer."],[16,19,"I am too old to start learning from a nurse again, too old to be a schoolboy now. So what is your sentence but a speechless death, since it stops my tongue speaking its native language?"]],"88":[[0,4,"Come back, and take an oath with you. Lay your banished hands on my royal sword. Swear by the duty you owe to God (the duty you owe me goes into exile with you) that you will keep the oath I now give you:"],[5,12,"that you will never, so help you truth and God, become friends in exile, nor ever look at each other's faces, nor ever write, greet each other or settle this dark storm of homegrown hatred, nor ever meet on purpose to plot, plan or scheme any harm against me, my government, my subjects or my country."]],"96":[[0,2,"I thank my lord for shortening my son's exile by four years for my sake. But I won't get much benefit from it."],[3,8,"Before the six years he has to spend abroad can change their moons and come round, my lamp, dried of oil, and my light, worn out by time, will be put out by age and endless night. My last inch of candle will burn down, and blindfolded death won't let me see my son."]],"100":[[0,2,"Things that taste sweet turn sour when you digest them. You asked me to speak as a judge, but I would rather you had told me to argue like a father."],[3,6,"Oh, if it had been a stranger and not my child, I would have been gentler and played down his fault. I tried to avoid being accused of favouring my son, and by my sentence I destroyed my own life."],[7,10,"Alas, I kept waiting for one of you to say I was too strict in sending away my own son. But you allowed my unwilling tongue to do me this harm against my will."]],"115":[[0,3,"To a wise man, every place the sun shines on is a port and a happy harbour. Teach yourself in your hardship to think like this: there is no virtue like accepting what you must."],[4,6,"Don't think the King banished you; think that you banished the King. Sorrow sits more heavily on someone who bears it weakly."],[7,12,"Go, and say I sent you away to win honour, and the King didn't exile you. Or imagine a deadly plague hanging in our air, and you are escaping to a healthier country. Whatever your soul holds dear, imagine it lies in the direction you are going, not the place you came from."],[13,18,"Imagine the singing birds are musicians, the grass you walk on is a royal hall strewn with rushes, the flowers are beautiful ladies and your steps are just a graceful dance. Snarling sorrow has less power to bite a man who laughs at it and takes it lightly."]],"116":[[0,5,"Oh, who can hold fire in his hand by thinking of the frosty Caucasus mountains? Or satisfy the sharp edge of hunger just by imagining a feast? Or roll naked in December snow by thinking of imaginary summer heat?"],[6,9,"Oh, no! Thinking about good things only makes you feel the bad ones more. Sorrow's cruel tooth never hurts more than when it bites without opening the wound to let it heal."]],"127":[[0,4,"'Farewell.' And since my heart refused to let my tongue insult that word by saying it, it taught me the trick of pretending to be so overcome with grief that my words seemed buried in the grave of my sorrow."],[5,8,"Honestly, if the word 'farewell' could have lengthened the hours and added years to his short exile, I would have given him a whole book of farewells. But since it couldn't, he got none from me."]],"128":[[0,2,"He is our cousin, cousin. But when time calls him home from exile, it's doubtful whether our relative will come back to see his friends."],[3,10,"I myself, and Bushy, Bagot and Green here, watched him courting the common people: how he seemed to dive into their hearts with humble, friendly politeness, how much respect he wasted on lowly people, winning over poor workmen with crafty smiles and with the patient way he bore his bad luck, as if to take their love into exile with him."],[11,16,"Off comes his cap to an oyster-seller. A couple of cart drivers wished him God speed, and got a bow of his bending knee and 'Thank you, my countrymen, my loving friends', as if our England would be his after me, and he were next in line in my subjects' hopes."]],"130":[[0,5,"I will go to this war in person. And because my treasury has got rather light from keeping too large a court and giving generous gifts, I am forced to lease out my royal kingdom for rent. The income from it will pay for the business in hand."],[6,10,"If that is not enough, my deputies at home shall have blank documents. When they find out which men are rich, they will fill in those men's names for large sums of gold and send the money after me to supply what I need. I am leaving for Ireland at once."]],"143":[[0,3,"Oh, but they say the words of dying men command attention like solemn music. When words are few, they are rarely wasted, because those who speak in pain speak the truth."],[4,9,"A man who will soon say nothing more is listened to more than those whom youth and comfort have taught to flatter. People notice how men end more than how they lived before. The setting sun, and music as it closes, like the last taste of something sweet, are sweetest at the end and stay in the memory longer than things long past."],[10,11,"Though Richard would not listen to my advice while I lived, my sad words as I die may still unblock his ears."]],"144":[[0,6,"No. His ears are stuffed with other, flattering sounds, like praise of his greatness. Then there are silly, lustful poems, whose poisonous sound young ears are always open to, and reports of the fashions in proud Italy, whose manners our slow, copycat nation limps after in cheap imitation."],[7,11,"Where in the world is there a new vanity, and if it's new, nobody cares how vile it is, that isn't quickly buzzed into his ears? Advice comes much too late to be heard when desire rebels against reason."],[12,13,"Don't try to steer someone who insists on choosing his own way. You are short of breath, and you will lose what breath you have."]],"145":[[0,8,"I feel I am a prophet newly inspired, and as I breathe my last I predict this about him: his reckless, fierce blaze of wild living cannot last, because violent fires soon burn themselves out. Light showers last a long time, but sudden storms are short. A man who spurs his horse too fast too early soon tires. Eating too greedily chokes the eater. Empty vanity, that greedy bird that is never full, soon eats itself once it has used up everything else."],[9,18,"This royal throne of kings, this island ruled by a sceptre, this land of majesty, this home of the god of war, this second Eden, this half-paradise, this fortress that Nature built for herself against disease and the hand of war, this happy race of men, this little world, this precious stone set in the silver sea, which acts as a wall for it, or as a defensive moat around a house, against the envy of less fortunate countries;"],[19,27,"this blessed plot of ground, this earth, this kingdom, this England, this nurse, this fertile womb of royal kings, feared because of their ancestry and famous for their birth, renowned for their deeds as far from home, in Christian service and true knighthood, as the tomb, in stubborn Judea, of the world's saviour, blessed Mary's Son; this land of such dear souls, this dear, dear land, dear for its reputation throughout the world,"],[28,35,"is now leased out, and I die as I say it, like a rented house or a miserable farm. England, surrounded by the triumphant sea, whose rocky shore beats back the jealous attacks of the sea god Neptune, is now surrounded by shame, by inky blots and rotten paper contracts. That England, which used to conquer others, has made a shameful conquest of itself."],[36,37,"Ah, if the disgrace would vanish when I die, how happy my coming death would be!"]],"150":[[0,5,"Oh, how well that name suits my condition! Old Gaunt indeed, and gaunt from being old. Grief has kept a long fast inside me, and who goes without food and isn't gaunt? I have kept watch over sleeping England for a long time. Staying awake makes you thin, and thinness is all gaunt."],[6,8,"The pleasure some fathers feed on, I mean the sight of their children, I have been strictly denied. And by making me fast from that, you have made me gaunt."],[9,10,"I am gaunt and ready for the grave, gaunt as a grave, whose hollow belly holds nothing but bones."]],"158":[[0,6,"Now, God who made me knows I see you are ill. I am ill myself, and ill to see you doing wrong. Your deathbed is nothing less than your whole country, where your reputation lies sick. And you, careless patient that you are, hand your anointed body over to be cured by the same doctors who wounded you in the first place."],[7,10,"A thousand flatterers sit inside your crown, whose circle is no bigger than your head. And yet, though they are caged in such a small space, the damage they do is as big as your whole country."],[11,15,"Oh, if your grandfather had seen with a prophet's eye how his son's son would destroy his sons, he would have put your shame out of your reach by deposing you before you inherited, you who are now possessed by madness to depose yourself."],[16,22,"Why, cousin, if you ruled the whole world, it would be shameful to lease out this land. But since this land is the only world you have, isn't it worse than shameful to shame it like this? You are England's landlord now, not its king. Your legal position makes you a slave to the law, and..."]],"159":[[0,4,"And you, a mad, half-witted fool, taking advantage of the privilege a fever gives you, dare to make my cheeks pale with your frozen warning, chasing the royal blood from its natural home with anger."],[5,8,"Now, by the royal majesty of my throne, if you weren't the brother of great Edward's son, this tongue that runs so freely in your head would run your head right off your disrespectful shoulders."]],"160":[[0,7,"Oh, don't spare me, my brother Edward's son, just because I was his father Edward's son. Like the pelican chick that feeds on its mother's blood, you have already drained that blood and drunk it greedily. My brother Gloucester, a simple, well-meaning soul (may he be happy in heaven among blessed souls!), is a good example and proof that you don't care about spilling Edward's blood."],[8,12,"Join forces with the sickness I have now, and let your cruelty, like bent old age, cut down at once a flower that has been withered too long. Live with your shame, but don't let your shame die with you! May these words torment you from now on!"],[13,14,"Carry me to my bed, then to my grave. Let those who have love and honour wish to live."]],"170":[[0,2,"The ripest fruit falls first, and so does he. His time is over; our journey through life must go on. So much for that. Now for our Irish wars."],[3,5,"We must get rid of those rough, shaggy-haired Irish foot soldiers, who live like poison in a country where nothing else poisonous is allowed to live."],[6,9,"And because these great matters cost money, to help pay for them I take for myself the silver plate, coins, income and possessions that belonged to our uncle Gaunt."]],"171":[[0,7,"How long shall I be patient? Ah, how long will my gentle sense of duty make me put up with wrong? Not Gloucester's death, not Hereford's exile, not Gaunt's humiliations, not England's private wrongs, not the way poor Bolingbroke's marriage abroad was blocked, not my own disgrace, has ever made me sour my patient face or frown even once at my king."],[8,12,"I am the last of noble Edward's sons, of whom your father, the Prince of Wales, was the eldest. In war no lion ever raged more fiercely, and in peace no gentle lamb was ever milder, than that young prince."],[13,20,"You have his face; he looked just like you when he was your age. But when he frowned, it was against the French, not against his friends. His noble hand won what he spent, and did not spend what his victorious father's hand had won. His hands were guilty of no relative's blood, but bloody with the blood of his family's enemies."],[21,22,"Oh, Richard! York is too overcome with grief, or he would never make this comparison."]],"173":[[0,2,"Oh, my lord, forgive me if you want to. If not, I'm happy not to be forgiven."],[3,8,"Are you trying to seize and grab into your hands the royal grants and rights of banished Hereford? Isn't Gaunt dead, and isn't Hereford alive? Wasn't Gaunt honourable, and isn't Harry loyal? Didn't the one deserve to have an heir? Isn't his heir a deserving son?"],[9,13,"Take away Hereford's rights, and you take away Time's legal charters and customary rights. Then don't let tomorrow follow today. Don't be yourself; for how are you a king except by proper order and succession?"],[14,22,"Now, before God, and God forbid I'm right, if you wrongfully seize Hereford's rights, cancel the royal letters that allow his lawyers to claim his inheritance, and refuse his offer of loyalty, you will pull a thousand dangers down on your head, lose a thousand well-meaning hearts, and push my gentle patience towards thoughts that a man of honour and loyalty cannot think."]],"177":[[0,6,"Bushy, go to the Earl of Wiltshire at once. Tell him to come to me at Ely House to deal with this business. Tomorrow I leave for Ireland, and it's high time, I think. While I am away I make my uncle York governor of England, because he is fair and has always loved me."],[7,8,"Come on, my queen. Tomorrow we must part. Be cheerful, because our time together is short."]],"200":[[0,10,"Then here it is. I have had news from Port Blanc, a bay in Brittany, that Harry Duke of Hereford, Rainold Lord Cobham, the son of the Earl of Arundel who recently escaped from the Duke of Exeter, his brother the former Archbishop of Canterbury, Sir Thomas Erpingham, Sir John Ramston, Sir John Norbery, Sir Robert Waterton and Francis Quoint, all well equipped by the Duke of Brittany with eight large ships and three thousand soldiers, are heading here as fast as they can and plan to land on our northern coast soon."],[11,12,"Perhaps they would have landed already, but they are waiting for the king to leave for Ireland first."],[13,20,"So if we are going to throw off our slavery, repair our drooping country's broken wing, buy back the stained crown from the pawnbroker, wipe off the dust that hides the gold of our sceptre and make royal majesty look like itself again, come with me at full speed to Ravenspurgh. But if you lose your nerve and are afraid to, stay here and keep it secret, and I will go myself."]],"207":[[0,4,"I promised to please the King. To please myself I cannot do it. And yet I know no reason why I should welcome such a guest as grief, except that I have said goodbye to such a sweet guest as my sweet Richard."],[5,8,"And yet I feel that some sorrow not yet born, ripe in the womb of fortune, is coming towards me, and my inner soul trembles at nothing. It grieves at something more than parting from my husband the king."]],"208":[[0,6,"Every real grief casts twenty shadows that look like grief itself but are not. Sorrow's eye, glazed with blinding tears, splits one whole thing into many, like those trick pictures which show nothing but a blur when you look at them straight on, but show a clear shape when you look at them from the side."],[7,13,"In the same way, your sweet Majesty, looking at your husband's departure from the wrong angle, finds more shapes of grief in it than the departure itself; and those, seen as they really are, are only shadows of what is not there. So, most gracious Queen, weep for nothing more than your husband's departure. There is nothing more to see; or if there is, it is seen with sorrow's false eye, which weeps for imaginary things as if they were real."]],"226":[[0,2,"If I did, I would be lying about my thoughts. Comfort is in heaven, and we are on earth, where there is nothing but trouble, worry and grief."],[3,8,"Your husband has gone far away to save one land, while others come to make him lose this one at home. I am left here to prop up his country, when I am so weak with age I can't support myself. Now comes the sickness that his excesses have caused. Now he will find out what his flattering friends are worth."]],"233":[[0,4,"God have mercy! What a flood of sorrows comes rushing over this sad country all at once! I don't know what to do. I wish to God, as long as it wasn't disloyalty in me that provoked him, that the king had cut off my head along with my brother's."],[5,9,"What! Haven't any messengers been sent to Ireland? How will we find money for these wars? Come, sister... cousin, I mean. Please forgive me. Go, man, go home, get some carts and bring away the armour that's there."]],"235":[[0,7,"Gentlemen, will you go and gather troops? If I know how or which way to manage these affairs that have been shoved into my hands in such a mess, never believe me again. Both are my relatives. One is my king, whom my oath and my duty both tell me to defend. The other is also my relative, whom the king has wronged, and whom my conscience and family feeling tell me to put right."],[8,13,"Well, we must do something. Come, cousin, I'll find a place for you. Gentlemen, go and gather your men and meet me straight away at Berkeley Castle. I ought to go to Plashy too, but there isn't time. Everything is uneven, and everything is in total confusion."]],"253":[[0,5,"Believe me, noble lord, I don't know my way around Gloucestershire. These high, wild hills and rough, bumpy roads stretch out our miles and make them tiring. And yet your pleasant conversation has been like sugar, making the hard road sweet and delightful."],[6,10,"But I think what a weary road Ross and Willoughby will find it from Ravenspurgh to the Cotswolds without your company, which, I swear, has made the dullness and length of my journey pass much more quickly."],[11,16,"But their journey is sweetened by the hope of having the benefit I have now; and hoping for joy is not much less joyful than joy itself. In this way the weary lords will make their road seem short, as mine has been made short by having what I have: your noble company."]],"285":[[0,3,"Tut, tut! Don't 'grace' me, and don't 'uncle' me. I am no traitor's uncle, and that word 'grace' is an insult to God in a mouth without grace."],[4,9,"Why have those banished, forbidden legs dared to touch even one speck of English dust? And more 'why': why have they dared to march so many miles over her peaceful breast, frightening her pale-faced villages with war and a display of hateful weapons?"],[10,12,"Have you come because the anointed king is away? Why, foolish boy, the king is left behind here, and his power lies in my loyal heart."],[13,19,"If I were now as young and hot-blooded as when brave Gaunt, your father, and I rescued the Black Prince, that young god of war among men, from the ranks of many thousand Frenchmen, oh, how quickly this arm of mine, now trembling with palsy, would punish you and correct your fault!"]],"288":[[0,3,"When I was banished, it was Hereford who was banished; but I come back as Lancaster. And, noble uncle, I beg your Grace to look at the wrongs done to me with an impartial eye."],[4,9,"You are my father, because I think I see old Gaunt alive in you. Oh, then, my father, will you let me be condemned to be a wandering beggar, my rights and titles snatched from my arms by force and given away to upstart spendthrifts? What was I born for?"],[10,15,"If my cousin is King of England, it must follow that I am Duke of Lancaster. You have a son, Aumerle, my noble cousin. If you had died first and he had been trampled on like this, he would have found a father in his uncle Gaunt, to rouse up his wrongs and hunt them down."],[16,23,"I am not allowed to claim my inheritance here, even though my royal letters give me the right. My father's goods have all been seized and sold, and all of it has been wrongly used. What do you want me to do? I am a subject and I appeal to the law. I am refused lawyers, and so I am claiming my lawful inheritance in person."]],"294":[[0,2,"Well, well, I see how this armed rising will end. I must admit I can't stop it, because my army is weak and has been badly left."],[3,9,"But if I could, by God who gave me life, I would arrest you all and make you bow to the royal mercy of the king. But since I can't, I tell you that I will stay neutral. So, goodbye, unless you would like to come into the castle and rest there for the night."]],"302":[[0,4,"People think the king is dead. We won't stay. The bay trees in our country have all withered, and comets frighten the fixed stars in heaven. The pale moon looks blood-red on the earth, and thin-faced prophets whisper about terrifying changes."],[5,8,"Rich men look sad, and thugs dance and jump about, the rich because they are afraid of losing what they have, the thugs because they hope to gain from violence and war. These signs come before the death or fall of kings."],[9,10,"Goodbye. Our countrymen have gone and run off, sure that Richard their king is dead."]],"308":[[0,6,"Bring these men out. Bushy and Green, I won't torment your souls, since they must leave your bodies very soon, by dwelling too much on your wicked lives; that would be unkind. But to wash your blood off my hands, I will set out here, in front of everyone, some of the reasons for your deaths."],[7,14,"You have misled a prince, a royal king, a fortunate gentleman in birth and looks, whom you made unfortunate and completely spoiled. With your sinful pastimes you have more or less divorced him from his queen, broken up a royal marriage bed and stained the beauty of a fair queen's cheeks with tears your foul wrongs drew from her eyes."],[15,26,"I myself, a prince by birth, close to the King in blood and close in love until you made him misjudge me, have bent my neck under the wrongs you did me and sighed my English breath into foreign clouds, eating the bitter bread of exile. Meanwhile you have fed on my estates, opened up my private parks and cut down my forests, torn my coat of arms out of my own windows and scratched out my emblem, leaving me no sign except people's opinion and my living blood to show the world I am a gentleman."],[27,29,"This, and much more, much more than twice all this, condemns you to death. See that they are handed over for execution and the hand of death."]],"321":[[0,7,"I must like it very much. I weep for joy to stand on my kingdom once again. Dear earth, I greet you with my hand, though rebels wound you with their horses' hooves. Like a mother long separated from her child, who weeps and smiles tenderly when they meet, I greet you, my earth, weeping and smiling, and touch you lovingly with my royal hands."],[8,13,"Don't feed your king's enemy, my gentle earth, and don't comfort his greedy appetite with your sweet things. Let your spiders, that suck up your poison, and slow, heavy toads lie in their path to trouble the treacherous feet that trample you with usurping steps."],[14,18,"Grow stinging nettles for my enemies; and when they pick a flower from your breast, guard it, I beg you, with a hidden adder, whose forked tongue can throw death on your king's enemies with a deadly touch."],[19,22,"Don't laugh at my appeal to things that can't feel, lords. This earth will come to have feelings, and these stones will turn into armed soldiers, before her rightful king is brought down by the weapons of foul rebellion."]],"324":[[0,10,"Gloomy cousin! Don't you know that when the searching eye of heaven, the sun, is hidden behind the earth and lights the world below, thieves and robbers wander about unseen, boldly committing murder and outrage here? But when he rises from under the earth, sets fire to the proud tops of the eastern pine trees and shoots his light into every guilty hole, then murders, treasons and hateful sins stand bare and naked, with the cloak of night pulled off their backs, trembling at themselves."],[11,17,"So when this thief, this traitor Bolingbroke, who has been partying in the night all this time while we were wandering on the other side of the world, sees us rising on our throne in the east, his treasons will blush in his face. He will not be able to bear the sight of day, but will tremble, terrified, at his own sin."],[18,21,"Not all the water in the rough, wild sea can wash the holy oil off an anointed king. The breath of mere earthly men cannot depose the deputy chosen by the Lord."],[22,26,"For every man Bolingbroke has forced to raise his sharp steel against our golden crown, God has a glorious angel in heaven's pay for his Richard. Then if angels fight, weak men must fall, because heaven always guards the right."]],"327":[[0,4,"No nearer and no further, my gracious lord, than this weak arm. Despair guides my tongue and tells me to speak of nothing but hopelessness. I fear, noble lord, that one day's delay has clouded all your happy days on earth."],[5,10,"Oh, call back yesterday, tell time to turn back, and you will have twelve thousand fighting men! Today, today, this unlucky day, too late, overthrows your joys, friends, fortune and power; because all the Welshmen, hearing you were dead, have gone over to Bolingbroke, or scattered and run away."]],"334":[[0,3,"My ear is open and my heart is ready. The worst you can tell me is a worldly loss. Tell me, is my kingdom lost? Well, it was my burden of care, and what loss is it to be rid of care?"],[4,10,"Is Bolingbroke trying to be as great as I am? He shall not be greater. If he serves God, I will serve God too, and so be his equal. Are my subjects rebelling? I can't put that right; they break their promises to God as well as to me. Cry woe, destruction, ruin, loss, decay. The worst is death, and death will have his day."]],"335":[[0,7,"I am glad your highness is so well prepared to hear news of disaster. Like a stormy day out of season, that makes the silver rivers flood their banks as if the world had melted into tears, Bolingbroke's rage swells high above its limits, covering your frightened land with hard, bright steel and hearts harder than steel."],[8,16,"White-bearded old men have put helmets on their thin, bald heads to fight your majesty. Boys with high voices try to speak like men and squeeze their soft limbs into stiff, heavy armour against your crown. Even the old men paid to pray for you learn to bend their bows of deadly yew against your rule. Even women who spin wool take up rusty spears against your throne. Young and old rebel, and everything is going worse than I can tell."]],"343":[[0,3,"It doesn't matter where. Let nobody speak of comfort. Let's talk about graves, worms and epitaphs. Let's make the dust our paper and write sorrow on the breast of the earth with our rainy eyes."],[4,10,"Let's choose executors and talk about wills. And yet, no: what can we leave except our deposed bodies to the ground? Our lands, our lives and everything belong to Bolingbroke, and we can call nothing our own except death and that small patch of barren earth that covers our bones like pastry over a pie."],[11,15,"For God's sake, let's sit on the ground and tell sad stories about the deaths of kings: how some have been deposed, some killed in war, some haunted by the ghosts of those they deposed, some poisoned by their wives, some killed in their sleep. All murdered."],[16,26,"Because inside the hollow crown that circles a king's mortal head, Death holds his court. There the clown sits, mocking the king's greatness and grinning at his ceremony, allowing him a breath, a short scene, to play the monarch, be feared and kill with a look, filling him with selfish, empty ideas, as if the flesh that walls in our life were unbreakable brass. And when he has amused himself like this, Death comes at last, and with a little pin he bores through the castle wall, and goodbye, king!"],[27,33,"Put your hats back on, and don't mock flesh and blood with solemn respect. Throw away respect, tradition, ceremony and formal duty, because you have misunderstood me all this time. I live on bread like you, feel need, taste grief, need friends. Since I am a subject to all these, how can you tell me I am a king?"]],"347":[[0,3,"Men judge what kind of day it will be by the colour of the sky. So you can judge by my dull, heavy eyes that my tongue only has a heavier story to tell."],[4,9,"I am playing the torturer, dragging out bit by bit the worst news, which must be told. Your uncle York has joined Bolingbroke, all your northern castles have surrendered and all your southern gentlemen have taken up arms on his side."]],"348":[[0,2,"You have said enough. Curse you, cousin, for leading me away from the sweet road to despair I was on!"],[3,7,"What do you say now? What comfort do we have now? By heaven, I will hate for ever anyone who tells me to take comfort again. Go to Flint Castle; I'll waste away there. A king who is sorrow's slave will obey royal sorrow."],[8,11,"Dismiss the army I have, and let them go and plough land that has some hope of growing something, because I have none. Let no one speak again to change this, because advice is useless."]],"368":[[0,3,"Noble lord, go to the rough walls of that ancient castle. Send the breath of a request to talk through a brass trumpet into its broken ears, and deliver this message:"],[4,10,"Henry Bolingbroke kisses King Richard's hand on both knees and sends loyalty and true faith of heart to his most royal person. I have come here to lay my weapons and my army at his feet, on condition that my banishment is cancelled and my lands are given back freely."],[11,17,"If not, I will use the advantage of my strength and settle the summer's dust with showers of blood rained from the wounds of slaughtered Englishmen. My humble kneeling will gently show how far it is from Bolingbroke's mind that such a crimson storm should soak the fresh green lap of fair King Richard's land."],[18,22,"Go and tell him this, while we march here on the grassy carpet of this plain. Let's march without the threatening noise of drums, so that our fine equipment can be clearly seen from the castle's crumbling walls."],[23,30,"I think King Richard and I should meet with no less terror than the elements of fire and water, when their thundering clash tears the cloudy cheeks of the sky. Let him be the fire, and I'll be the water that gives way. Let the rage be his, while I rain my waters on the earth; on the earth, not on him. March on, and watch how King Richard looks."]],"372":[[0,9,"I am astonished. I have stood here this long waiting to see you bend your knee in fear, because I thought I was your lawful king. And if I am, how dare your joints forget to pay their respectful duty to my presence? If I am not, show me the hand of God that has dismissed me from my office, because I know well that no hand of flesh and blood can grip the sacred handle of my sceptre without committing sacrilege, theft or usurpation."],[10,18,"And though you think that everyone has torn their souls, as you have, by turning them away from me, and that I am left bare and without friends, know this: my master, almighty God, is gathering armies of plague in his clouds on my behalf. They will strike your children, not yet born or even conceived, because you lift your servants' hands against my head and threaten the glory of my precious crown."],[19,28,"Tell Bolingbroke, for I think that's him standing there, that every step he takes on my land is dangerous treason. He has come to open the blood-red will of bleeding war. But before the crown he is after can live in peace, ten thousand bloody heads of mothers' sons will spoil the flower of England's face, change the pale, maiden colour of her peace to scarlet anger, and wet the grass of her fields with loyal English blood."]],"373":[[0,10,"The King of Heaven forbid that our lord the King should be attacked like this with weapons of civil war! Your thrice-noble cousin, Harry Bolingbroke, humbly kisses your hand. He swears by the honourable tomb that stands over your royal grandfather's bones, by the royal blood of you both, two streams springing from one most gracious source, by the buried hand of warlike Gaunt, and by his own worth and honour, which covers everything that can be sworn or said,"],[11,19,"that his only purpose in coming here is to claim his inherited titles and to beg on his knees to have his rights restored at once. Once your Majesty grants that, he will leave his glittering weapons to rust, his armoured horses to their stables and his heart to faithful service of your Majesty. He swears this is fair, on his word as a prince; and on my word as a gentleman, I believe him."]],"378":[[0,6,"O God, O God, that this tongue of mine, which passed the terrible sentence of exile on that proud man over there, should ever take it off again with flattering words! Oh, I wish I were as great as my grief, or less than my title, or that I could forget what I have been, or not remember what I must be now!"],[7,8,"Are you swelling, proud heart? I'll give you room to beat, since my enemies have room to beat both you and me."]],"380":[[0,3,"What must the King do now? Must he give in? The king will do it. Must he be deposed? The king will be content. Must he lose the name of king? In God's name, let it go."],[4,11,"I'll give my jewels for a string of prayer beads, my splendid palace for a hermit's cell, my fine clothes for a beggar's gown, my engraved goblets for a wooden bowl, my sceptre for a pilgrim's walking stick, my subjects for a pair of carved saints, and my large kingdom for a little grave, a little, little grave, an unknown grave."],[12,16,"Or I'll be buried in the king's highway, some busy road, where subjects' feet can trample on their king's head every hour; because they tread on my heart now while I'm alive, so once I'm buried, why not on my head?"],[17,20,"Aumerle, you're crying, my tender-hearted cousin! We'll make bad weather with our despised tears. Our sighs and tears together will flatten the summer corn and cause a famine in this rebellious country."],[21,28,"Or shall we play games with our sorrows and have a pretty contest in shedding tears? Like this: we drop them on the same spot until they have worn a pair of graves for us in the earth, with this carved there: 'Here lie two relatives who dug their graves with weeping eyes.' Wouldn't this misery work well? Well, well, I see I'm just talking nonsense, and you are laughing at me."],[29,32,"Most mighty prince, my Lord Northumberland, what does King Bolingbroke say? Will his Majesty give Richard permission to live until Richard dies? You bow, and that means Bolingbroke says yes."]],"392":[[0,3,"You deserve it well. Those who know the strongest and surest way to get things deserve to have them. Uncle, give me your hand. No, dry your eyes. Tears show love, but they can't cure anything."],[4,8,"Cousin, I am too young to be your father, though you are old enough to be my heir. What you want, I will give you, and willingly too, because we must do what force makes us do. Set off towards London. Cousin, is that right?"]],"413":[[0,3,"You, go and tie up those dangling apricots over there, which, like unruly children, make their father bend under their heavy, wasteful weight. Give some support to the bending branches."],[4,7,"You go, and like an executioner cut off the heads of the shoots that grow too fast and look too high in our little state. Everything must be level in our government."],[8,10,"While you do that, I will go and dig up the harmful weeds that uselessly suck the soil's goodness away from the healthy flowers."]],"417":[[0,6,"They are, and Bolingbroke has captured the wasteful King. Oh, what a pity it is that he didn't trim and tend his land as we do this garden! At the right time of year we cut the bark, the skin of our fruit trees, in case they get too full of sap and blood and destroy themselves with too much richness."],[7,12,"If he had done that to great men who were growing too powerful, they might have lived to bear fruit, and he to taste their fruits of loyalty. We cut off unnecessary branches so that fruit-bearing boughs can live. If he had done that, he would still be wearing the crown, which the waste of idle hours has completely thrown down."]],"423":[[0,5,"Forgive me, madam. I take little pleasure in telling this news, but what I say is true. King Richard is in Bolingbroke's powerful grip. Their fortunes have been weighed. In your husband's scale there is nothing but himself and a few vanities that make him light."],[6,10,"But in great Bolingbroke's scale, besides himself, there are all the English lords, and with that advantage he weighs King Richard down. Hurry to London and you will find it is true. I'm saying no more than everyone knows."]],"424":[[0,5,"Quick misfortune, so light on your feet, isn't your message meant for me, and am I the last to know it? Oh, you think you'll serve me last so that I keep your sorrow in my heart the longest. Come, ladies, let's go and meet London's king, sorrowful in London."],[6,9,"Was I born for this, so that my sad face should decorate great Bolingbroke's triumph? Gardener, for telling me this sad news, I pray God that the plants you graft may never grow!"]],"433":[[0,5,"My Lord Aumerle, I know your bold tongue is too proud to take back what it has once said. At that dark time when Gloucester's death was being plotted, I heard you say, 'Isn't my arm long enough to reach from the peaceful English court as far as Calais, to my uncle's head?'"],[6,11,"In a lot of other talk at that very time, I heard you say you would rather turn down a hundred thousand crowns than see Bolingbroke come back to England, and you added how blessed this country would be if your cousin died."]],"434":[[0,5,"Princes and noble lords, what answer shall I give this lowborn man? Shall I so dishonour my noble birth as to fight him as an equal? Either I must, or let my honour be stained by the accusation of his slandering lips."],[6,10,"There is my glove, the hand-sealed warrant of death that marks you out for hell. I say you lie, and I will prove that what you have said is false with your heart's blood, though it is much too lowly to stain my knightly sword."]],"450":[[0,5,"How foolishly you spur on a horse that is already eager! If I dare to eat or drink or breathe or live, I dare to meet Surrey in a wilderness and spit on him while I say he lies, and lies, and lies. There is my pledge of honour to bind you to the harsh punishment I'll give you."],[6,10,"As I hope to prosper in this new world, Aumerle is guilty of what I truly accuse him of. Besides, I heard the banished Norfolk say that you, Aumerle, sent two of your men to kill the noble duke at Calais."]],"453":[[0,4,"That honourable day will never be seen. Banished Norfolk fought many times for Jesus Christ on glorious Christian battlefields, flying the banner of the Christian cross against black pagans, Turks and Saracens."],[5,9,"Then, worn out by the labours of war, he retired to Italy, and there, at Venice, he gave his body to that pleasant country's earth and his pure soul to his captain, Christ, under whose flag he had fought for so long."]],"460":[[0,6,"God forbid! I may be the lowest in this royal company to speak, but I am the most fitting one to speak the truth. I wish to God anyone in this noble company were noble enough to be a fair judge of noble Richard! Then true nobility would teach him to hold back from such a foul wrong."],[7,17,"What subject can pass sentence on his king? And who sits here who is not Richard's subject? Thieves are not judged unless they are there to hear it, even when their guilt is obvious. And shall the image of God's majesty, his captain, steward and chosen deputy, anointed, crowned and established for many years, be judged by the voices of his subjects and inferiors, without even being present himself? Oh, God forbid that in a Christian country refined souls should commit such a hateful, black, disgusting deed!"],[18,21,"I am speaking to subjects, and a subject is speaking, stirred up by God to speak out like this for his king. My Lord of Hereford here, whom you call king, is a foul traitor to proud Hereford's king."],[22,30,"And if you crown him, let me prophesy: English blood will fertilise the ground, and future ages will groan because of this foul act. Peace will go to sleep among Turks and unbelievers, and in this home of peace, violent wars will set relatives against relatives and countrymen against countrymen. Disorder, horror, fear and rebellion will live here, and this country will be called Golgotha, the field of dead men's skulls."],[31,35,"Oh, if you raise this royal house against this house, it will be the most terrible division that ever fell on this cursed earth. Prevent it, resist it, don't let it happen, or your children and your children's children will cry 'woe' against you!"]],"467":[[0,5,"Alas, why am I sent for to come to a king before I have shaken off the royal thoughts I reigned with? I have hardly learned yet how to creep, flatter, bow and bend my knee. Give sorrow a little time to teach me this submission."],[6,9,"And yet I remember these men's faces well. Weren't they mine? Didn't they once cry 'All hail!' to me? So Judas did to Christ. But Christ, out of twelve, found all loyal but one; I, out of twelve thousand, found none."],[10,14,"God save the King! Won't anyone say amen? Am I both the priest and the clerk who answers him? Well then, amen. God save the King, although I am not him; and yet, amen, if heaven thinks he is me. What service have I been sent for to do here?"]],"469":[[0,2,"Give me the crown. Here, cousin, take hold of the crown. Here, cousin: my hand on this side, and yours on that."],[3,8,"Now this golden crown is like a deep well with two buckets, filling each other in turn, the emptier one always dancing in the air, the other one down below, unseen and full of water. I am that bucket, down and full of tears, drinking my griefs while you rise up high."]],"475":[[0,2,"Yes, no; no, yes. Because I must be nothing, I can't say no; I give it up to you. Now watch how I will undo myself."],[3,12,"I take this heavy weight off my head and this clumsy sceptre out of my hand, and the pride of royal power out of my heart. With my own tears I wash off my holy oil. With my own hands I give away my crown. With my own tongue I deny my sacred rank. With my own breath I release everyone from their duties. I give up all pomp and majesty. I give up my estates, rents and income. I cancel my acts, decrees and laws."],[13,21,"God forgive all the oaths that are broken to me! God keep unbroken all the vows that are made to you! Make me, who have nothing, not grieve about having nothing, and make you, who have gained everything, pleased with everything! Long may you live to sit on Richard's throne, and may Richard soon lie in a hole in the earth! God save King Henry, says Richard, no longer king, and send him many years of sunny days! What else is there?"]],"477":[[0,8,"Must I do that? Must I unpick the woven pattern of my follies? Gentle Northumberland, if your crimes were written down, wouldn't you be ashamed to read them out in front of such a fine company? If you did, you would find there one terrible charge: the deposing of a king and the breaking of the strong bond of an oath, marked with a black stain and damned in the book of heaven."],[9,14,"No, all of you who stand looking at me while my misery torments me, though some of you wash your hands like Pilate, showing pity on the outside, you Pilates have still handed me over to my bitter cross, and water cannot wash away your sin."]],"479":[[0,2,"My eyes are full of tears; I can't see. And yet the salt water doesn't blind them so much that they can't see a pack of traitors here."],[3,8,"No, if I turn my eyes on myself, I find I am a traitor along with the rest, because I have given my soul's agreement here to strip the splendid body of a king, to make glory lowly and royal power a slave, proud majesty a subject and kingship a peasant."]],"481":[[0,5,"I'm no lord of yours, you proud, insulting man, and no man's lord. I have no name, no title; no, not even the name I was given at my christening, because that has been taken from me. Alas, what a sad day, that I have lived through so many winters and don't know now what name to call myself!"],[6,8,"Oh, I wish I were a snowman king, standing in front of the sun of Bolingbroke to melt myself away in drops of water!"],[9,13,"Good king, great king, though not greatly good, if my word is still worth anything in England, let it order a mirror to be brought here at once, so that it can show me what my face looks like now that it has lost its majesty."]],"490":[[0,5,"Give me that mirror, and I will read in it. No deeper wrinkles yet? Has sorrow struck so many blows on this face of mine and made no deeper wounds? Oh, flattering mirror! You deceive me, like my followers when I was doing well."],[6,12,"Was this the face that every day fed ten thousand men under its roof? Was this the face that, like the sun, made people who looked at it blink? Is this the face that put a bold face on so many follies, and was at last outfaced by Bolingbroke? A fragile glory shines in this face. The face is as fragile as the glory."]],"494":[[0,5,"Say that again. The shadow of my sorrow! Ha! Let's see. It's very true. My grief is all inside, and these outward ways of mourning are only shadows of the unseen grief that swells in silence in my tortured soul."],[6,11,"That's where the real thing is. And I thank you, king, for your great generosity, which not only gives me reason to weep but teaches me how to mourn that reason. I'll ask one favour, and then go and trouble you no more. Shall I have it?"]],"511":[[0,4,"My lord, before I speak my mind freely on this, you must take communion and swear not only to keep my intentions secret, but also to carry out whatever I decide."],[5,8,"I can see your brows are full of discontent, your hearts full of sorrow and your eyes full of tears. Come home to supper with me. I will set out a plan that will bring us all a happy day."]],"517":[[0,3,"But wait, look, or rather don't look, at my fair rose withering. And yet look up, see him, so that you melt into dew out of pity and wash him fresh again with tears of true love."],[4,8,"Ah, you, the ruin where old Troy once stood, the outline of honour, King Richard's tomb and not King Richard himself, you most beautiful inn, why should ugly grief be lodged in you, when triumph has become a guest at a common alehouse?"]],"518":[[0,3,"Don't join forces with grief, fair woman, don't, to make my end too sudden. Learn, good soul, to think of our former greatness as a happy dream. Now that we have woken from it, the truth of what we are shows us only this."],[4,9,"I am sworn brother, sweet, to grim Necessity, and he and I will stay allied until death. Hurry to France and shut yourself away in some convent. Our holy lives must win a crown in the next world, which our wicked hours here have thrown down."]],"519":[[0,2,"What! Has my Richard been changed and weakened in both body and mind? Has Bolingbroke deposed your mind too? Has he been inside your heart?"],[3,8,"The lion, when he is dying, thrusts out his paw and wounds the earth, if nothing else, in rage at being overpowered. And will you, like a schoolboy, take your punishment meekly, kiss the cane and fawn on the anger against you with low humility, when you are a lion and the king of beasts?"]],"520":[[0,4,"King of beasts indeed. If they had been anything but beasts, I would still be a happy king of men. Good woman, once my queen, get ready to leave for France. Think I am dead, and that even here you are taking your last leave of me, as if at my deathbed."],[5,10,"On long winter nights, sit by the fire with good old people and let them tell you stories of sad times long ago. And before you say good night, to pay them back for their sad stories, tell them the sad story of me, and send your listeners weeping to their beds."],[11,15,"Because even the senseless logs will feel for the heavy tone of your moving voice and weep the fire out in pity, and some will mourn in ashes, some in coal-black, for the deposing of a rightful king."]],"523":[[0,4,"Northumberland, you ladder that the climbing Bolingbroke uses to reach my throne, it won't be many hours older than it is now before foul sin comes to a head and bursts like a boil."],[5,10,"You will think, even if he divides the kingdom and gives you half, that it is too little, since you helped him get all of it. And he will think that you, who know how to set up kings who have no right, will know another way, with very little pushing, to pull him headlong off the throne he stole."],[11,13,"The love of wicked men turns to fear, that fear to hate, and hate leads one or both of them to the danger and death they deserve."]],"525":[[0,4,"Divorced twice over! Wicked men, you break two marriages: between my crown and me, and then between me and my wife. Let me unkiss the vow between you and me. And yet no, because it was made with a kiss."],[5,9,"Separate us, Northumberland: me to the north, where shivering cold and sickness waste the land; my wife to France, where she came from in splendour, decked out like sweet May, and is sent back like dark November, the shortest day of the year."]],"545":[[0,4,"Then, as I said, the Duke, great Bolingbroke, riding a hot, fiery horse that seemed to know its ambitious rider, kept on his way at a slow but stately pace, while everyone shouted, 'God save you, Bolingbroke!'"],[5,10,"You would have thought the windows themselves were speaking, so many eager eyes of young and old darted their longing looks through them at his face; and that all the walls, hung with painted cloths, were saying at once, 'Jesus protect you! Welcome, Bolingbroke!'"],[11,14,"Meanwhile he, turning from one side to the other, with his head bare and bowed lower than his proud horse's neck, spoke to them like this: 'Thank you, countrymen.' And doing this all the way, he passed along."]],"547":[[0,7,"As in a theatre, when a popular actor leaves the stage, people's eyes turn carelessly to the one who comes on next, thinking his chatter boring; in the same way, or with much more contempt, people's eyes scowled at Richard. No one shouted 'God save him'. No joyful voice welcomed him home. Instead dust was thrown on his sacred head,"],[8,13,"which he shook off with such gentle sorrow, his face struggling between tears and smiles, the signs of his grief and his patience, that if God had not hardened men's hearts for some strong purpose, they would have had to melt, and even savages would have pitied him."],[14,17,"But heaven has a hand in these events, and we limit our wishes calmly to its high will. We are sworn subjects of Bolingbroke now, and I accept his power and honour for ever."]],"591":[[0,4,"If you had groaned in labour for him as I did, you would have more pity. But now I know what you think. You suspect that I have been unfaithful to you, and that he is a bastard, not your son."],[5,8,"Sweet York, sweet husband, don't think that. He is as like you as any man can be, not like me or any of my family. And yet I love him."]],"598":[[0,3,"Can nobody tell me anything about my wild son? It's three whole months since I last saw him. If any disaster is hanging over us, it's him. I wish to God, my lords, he could be found."],[4,11,"Ask in London, among the taverns there, because they say he goes there every day with wild, unruly companions, the kind, they say, who wait in narrow alleys to beat up our watchmen and rob travellers; while he, a spoiled, soft young man, makes it a point of honour to support such a disgraceful gang."]],"643":[[0,4,"Does he mean it? Look at his face. His eyes shed no tears; his prayers are a joke. His words come from his mouth, ours from our hearts. He prays weakly and wants to be refused. We pray with heart and soul and everything else."],[5,10,"His tired joints would happily get up, I know. Our knees will keep kneeling until they grow into the ground. His prayers are full of false hypocrisy, ours of true passion and deep honesty. Our prayers outpray his, so let them have the mercy that true prayer deserves."]],"647":[[0,4,"Are you teaching him to use the word 'pardon' to destroy pardon? Ah, my sour husband, my hard-hearted lord, who sets the word against itself! Say 'pardon' the way we use it in our country. We don't understand clipped French."],[5,8,"Your eyes are beginning to speak; let your tongue say what they say. Or put your ear into your pitying heart, so that, hearing how our pleas and prayers pierce it, pity may move you to say 'pardon'."]],"655":[[0,6,"But as for our trusty brother-in-law, the abbot and all the rest of that gang of conspirators, destruction will follow at their heels at once. Good uncle, help send several forces to Oxford or wherever these traitors are. I swear they won't stay alive in this world; I'll get them, once I know where they are."],[7,8,"Uncle, goodbye. And, cousin, farewell. Your mother has prayed well; now prove yourself loyal."]],"668":[[0,4,"I have been working out how I can compare this prison where I live to the world. And because the world is full of people, and here there is no creature but me, I can't do it. But I'll hammer it out."],[5,10,"I'll make my brain the mother and my soul the father, and the two of them will produce a family of thoughts that keep on breeding. And these same thoughts fill this little world, with moods like the people of this world, because no thought is satisfied."],[11,16,"The better kind, like thoughts about religion, are mixed with doubts, and set one part of the Bible against another, like this: 'Come, little children', and then again, 'It is as hard to come to heaven as it is for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle.'"],[17,21,"Ambitious thoughts plan impossible wonders: how these useless, weak fingernails could tear a way through the flinty ribs of this hard world, my rough prison walls; and because they can't, they die in their own pride."],[22,29,"Thoughts that tend towards contentment flatter themselves that they are not the first slaves of fortune and won't be the last, like simple beggars sitting in the stocks who comfort their shame by thinking that many have sat there before and others will have to. And in this thought they find a kind of relief, putting their own bad luck on the backs of those who have suffered the same before."],[30,40,"So I play many people in one person, and none of them is content. Sometimes I am a king. Then treasons make me wish I were a beggar, and so I am. Then crushing poverty convinces me I was better off as a king; then I am king again; and soon I think that Bolingbroke has unkinged me, and at once I am nothing. But whatever I am, neither I nor any other man who is only a man will be pleased with anything until he is relieved by being nothing."],[41,48,"Do I hear music? Ha, ha! Keep time. How sour sweet music is when the rhythm is broken and the beat isn't kept! It's the same in the music of men's lives. And here I have a fine enough ear to correct a broken rhythm on an out-of-tune string; but for the harmony of my kingship and my time, I had no ear to hear my true time breaking."],[49,60,"I wasted time, and now time wastes me, because now time has made me his clock. My thoughts are minutes, and with sighs they tick them on to my eyes, the outer clock face, where my finger, like the hand of a clock, keeps pointing as it wipes away tears. Now, sir, the sound that tells the hour is loud groans that strike my heart, which is the bell. So sighs, tears and groans show minutes, quarters and hours. But my time runs on fast in Bolingbroke's proud joy, while I stand here playing the fool, his little figure that strikes the clock."],[61,66,"This music is driving me mad. Let it play no more. Though it has helped madmen back to their senses, in me it seems it will make wise men mad. Yet bless the heart of the person who plays it for me, because it is a sign of love, and love for Richard is a rare jewel in this all-hating world."]],"672":[[0,3,"I was a poor groom in your stable, king, when you were king. I am travelling to York, and with a lot of trouble I have at last got permission to see the face of my former royal master."],[4,8,"Oh, how it hurt my heart to see, in the streets of London, on that coronation day, Bolingbroke riding on roan Barbary, the horse you rode so often, the horse I groomed so carefully!"]],"675":[[0,5,"So proud that Bolingbroke was on his back! That nag has eaten bread from my royal hand. This hand made him proud by patting him. Wouldn't he stumble? Wouldn't he fall down, since pride must have a fall, and break the neck of that proud man who stole his back?"],[6,10,"Forgive me, horse! Why do I abuse you, when you were created to be ruled by man and born to carry? I was not made a horse, and yet I carry a load like a donkey, rubbed raw by spurs and worn out by Bolingbroke bouncing on my back."]],"713":[[0,6,"Those who need poison don't love it, and I don't love you. Though I wished him dead, I hate the murderer and love the murdered man. Take a guilty conscience as your reward for your work, but not my good word or royal favour. Go and wander with Cain through the shadows of night, and never show your face by day or in light."],[7,10,"Lords, I swear my soul is full of sorrow that blood should be sprinkled on me to make me grow. Come, mourn with me for what I mourn, and put on gloomy black at once."],[11,14,"I'll make a voyage to the Holy Land to wash this blood off my guilty hand. March sadly after me. Honour my mourning here by weeping as you follow this coffin of a man who died before his time."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"kingship":["Kingship and divine right","The king as God's anointed deputy, and what happens when that belief meets real power."],"power":["Power and usurpation","How Bolingbroke rises and Richard falls, and the price of taking a crown."],"identity":["Identity and the self","Names, titles, mirrors and shadows: who is Richard when he is no longer king?"],"loyalty":["Loyalty and treason","Oaths, duty and betrayal, from Mowbray and York to Aumerle and Northumberland."],"england":["England and the land","The 'sceptred isle', the garden, the earth: England as a body that is wounded."],"grief":["Grief and sorrow","The Queen's 'nameless woe', Richard's tears, and grief as a way of seeing."],"language":["Words and breath","The power and weakness of words: royal sentences, curses, wordplay and silence."],"time":["Time and change","Delay, wasted time and the turning wheel of fortune."],"justice":["Blood, guilt and justice","Gloucester's murder, Abel's blood and Bolingbroke's guilty hands."]},"quotes":[{"id":"16","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"Which blood, like sacrificing Abel's, cries, Even from the tongueless caverns of the earth, To me for justice and rough chastisement;","themes":["justice"],"note":"Bolingbroke turns Gloucester's murder into Cain killing Abel."},{"id":"28","who":"MOWBRAY","text":"The purest treasure mortal times afford Is spotless reputation; that away, Men are but gilded loam or painted clay.","themes":["loyalty","identity"],"note":"Mowbray: without honour a man is only painted mud."},{"id":"32","who":"RICHARD","text":"We were not born to sue, but to command:","themes":["kingship","power"],"note":"Richard insists on his right to command, just after being disobeyed."},{"id":"38","who":"GAUNT","text":"God's is the quarrel; for God's substitute, His deputy anointed in his sight, Hath caus'd his death;","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Gaunt refuses revenge on God's 'deputy anointed'."},{"id":"37","who":"DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","text":"That which in mean men we entitle patience Is pale cold cowardice in noble breasts.","themes":["loyalty","justice"],"note":"The Duchess of Gloucester shames Gaunt for doing nothing."},{"id":"84","who":"MOWBRAY","text":"The language I have learn'd these forty years, My native English, now I must forgo;","themes":["language","england"],"note":"Mowbray's exile means losing his language."},{"id":"95","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"How long a time lies in one little word! Four lagging winters and four wanton springs End in a word: such is the breath of kings.","themes":["language","kingship","time"],"note":"Bolingbroke marvels that one word cut four years."},{"id":"116","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"O! who can hold a fire in his hand By thinking on the frosty Caucasus?","themes":["grief"],"note":"Bolingbroke rejects Gaunt's advice to imagine his exile away."},{"id":"118","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"Where'er I wander, boast of this I can, Though banish'd, yet a true-born Englishman.","themes":["england","identity"],"note":"Bolingbroke's parting words."},{"id":"92","who":"MOWBRAY","text":"But what thou art, God, thou, and I, do know; And all too soon, I fear, the king shall rue.","themes":["loyalty"],"note":"Mowbray's warning as he leaves for ever."},{"id":"128","who":"RICHARD","text":"Observ'd his courtship to the common people, How he did seem to dive into their hearts With humble and familiar courtesy,","themes":["power"],"note":"Richard sneers at Bolingbroke's popularity."},{"id":"136","who":"RICHARD","text":"Now put it, God, in his physician's mind To help him to his grave immediately!","themes":["kingship","justice"],"note":"Richard prays for his uncle's quick death."},{"id":"143","who":"GAUNT","text":"O! but they say the tongues of dying men Enforce attention like deep harmony:","themes":["language"],"note":"Gaunt hopes his dying words will be heard."},{"id":"145","who":"GAUNT","text":"This royal throne of kings, this scepter'd isle, This earth of majesty, this seat of Mars, This other Eden, demi-paradise,","themes":["england"],"note":"The start of Gaunt's great praise of England."},{"id":"145","who":"GAUNT","text":"This blessed plot, this earth, this realm, this England,","themes":["england"],"note":"Gaunt's list builds to the name itself."},{"id":"145","who":"GAUNT","text":"That England, that was wont to conquer others, Hath made a shameful conquest of itself.","themes":["england","power"],"note":"Gaunt's bitter turn at the end of the speech."},{"id":"158","who":"GAUNT","text":"A thousand flatterers sit within thy crown, Whose compass is no bigger than thy head;","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Gaunt pictures the crown full of flatterers."},{"id":"158","who":"GAUNT","text":"Landlord of England art thou now, not king:","themes":["kingship","england"],"note":"Gaunt's sharpest insult."},{"id":"173","who":"YORK","text":"Be not thyself; for how art thou a king But by fair sequence and succession?","themes":["kingship","identity"],"note":"York warns Richard against seizing Bolingbroke's inheritance."},{"id":"187","who":"NORTHUMBERLAND","text":"The king is not himself, but basely led By flatterers;","themes":["kingship","loyalty"],"note":"Northumberland justifies turning against Richard."},{"id":"208","who":"BUSHY","text":"Each substance of a grief hath twenty shadows,","themes":["grief","identity"],"note":"Bushy tries to explain away the Queen's sadness."},{"id":"235","who":"YORK","text":"Both are my kinsmen: T'one is my sovereign, whom both my oath And duty bids defend; the other again Is my kinsman, whom the king hath wrong'd, Whom conscience and my kindred bids to right.","themes":["loyalty"],"note":"York is torn between duty and conscience."},{"id":"285","who":"YORK","text":"Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle:","themes":["loyalty","language"],"note":"York refuses Bolingbroke's polite titles."},{"id":"288","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"As I was banish'd, I was banish'd Hereford; But as I come, I come for Lancaster.","themes":["identity","power"],"note":"Bolingbroke claims he has returned only as Duke of Lancaster."},{"id":"295","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"The caterpillars of the commonwealth,","themes":["england","power"],"note":"Bolingbroke's name for Bushy, Bagot and Green."},{"id":"304","who":"SALISBURY","text":"I see thy glory like a shooting star Fall to the base earth from the firmament.","themes":["kingship","time"],"note":"Salisbury pictures Richard's fall."},{"id":"321","who":"RICHARD","text":"Dear earth, I do salute thee with my hand,","themes":["england","kingship"],"note":"Richard greets the soil of England on landing."},{"id":"324","who":"RICHARD","text":"Not all the water in the rough rude sea Can wash the balm off from an anointed king; The breath of worldly men cannot depose The deputy elected by the Lord.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Richard's faith in divine right."},{"id":"324","who":"RICHARD","text":"God for his Richard hath in heavenly pay A glorious angel:","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Richard believes angels will fight for him."},{"id":"327","who":"SALISBURY","text":"O! call back yesterday, bid time return,","themes":["time"],"note":"Salisbury: one day too late."},{"id":"331","who":"RICHARD","text":"Is not the king's name twenty thousand names?","themes":["identity","kingship"],"note":"Richard tries to rally himself with his title."},{"id":"343","who":"RICHARD","text":"For God's sake let us sit upon the ground And tell sad stories of the death of kings:","themes":["grief","kingship"],"note":"Richard gives up and turns to storytelling."},{"id":"343","who":"RICHARD","text":"within the hollow crown That rounds the mortal temples of a king Keeps Death his court;","themes":["kingship","identity"],"note":"Death is the real king inside the crown."},{"id":"343","who":"RICHARD","text":"I live with bread like you, feel want, Taste grief, need friends: subjected thus, How can you say to me I am a king?","themes":["identity","kingship"],"note":"Richard discovers he is an ordinary man."},{"id":"350","who":"RICHARD","text":"From Richard's night to Bolingbroke's fair day.","themes":["power","time"],"note":"Richard dismisses his followers."},{"id":"368","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"Be he the fire, I'll be the yielding water;","themes":["power"],"note":"Bolingbroke pictures himself as gentle water."},{"id":"371","who":"YORK","text":"Yet he looks like a king:","themes":["kingship"],"note":"York on Richard on the walls of Flint."},{"id":"380","who":"RICHARD","text":"I'll give my jewels for a set of beads, My gorgeous palace for a hermitage,","themes":["grief","kingship"],"note":"Richard imagines swapping royal things for holy poverty."},{"id":"380","who":"RICHARD","text":"And my large kingdom for a little grave, A little little grave, an obscure grave;","themes":["grief","identity"],"note":"The list ends in a grave."},{"id":"382","who":"RICHARD","text":"Down, down I come; like glist'ring Phaethon, Wanting the manage of unruly jades.","themes":["kingship","power"],"note":"Richard comes down to Bolingbroke."},{"id":"392","who":"RICHARD","text":"For do we must what force will have us do.","themes":["power"],"note":"Richard accepts that force rules."},{"id":"414","who":"SERVANT","text":"When our sea-walled garden, the whole land, Is full of weeds;","themes":["england"],"note":"A servant compares the garden to England."},{"id":"417","who":"GARDENER","text":"superfluous branches We lop away, that bearing boughs may live:","themes":["kingship","england"],"note":"The gardener explains good pruning."},{"id":"426","who":"GARDENER","text":"Here did she fall a tear; here in this place I'll set a bank of rue, sour herb of grace.","themes":["grief"],"note":"The gardener plants rue for the Queen."},{"id":"460","who":"CARLISLE","text":"What subject can give sentence on his king?","themes":["kingship","loyalty"],"note":"Carlisle protests against the deposition."},{"id":"460","who":"CARLISLE","text":"and this land be call'd The field of Golgotha and dead men's skulls.","themes":["justice","england"],"note":"Carlisle prophesies civil war."},{"id":"467","who":"RICHARD","text":"So Judas did to Christ: but he, in twelve, Found truth in all but one; I, in twelve thousand, none.","themes":["loyalty","kingship"],"note":"Richard compares himself to Christ betrayed."},{"id":"469","who":"RICHARD","text":"Now is this golden crown like a deep well That owes two buckets, filling one another;","themes":["power","grief"],"note":"Richard's image as both men hold the crown."},{"id":"471","who":"RICHARD","text":"You may my glories and my state depose, But not my griefs; still am I king of those.","themes":["grief","identity"],"note":"Richard keeps the one kingdom left to him."},{"id":"475","who":"RICHARD","text":"Ay, no; no, ay; for I must nothing be;","themes":["identity","language"],"note":"Richard's answer to 'Are you contented to resign?'"},{"id":"475","who":"RICHARD","text":"With mine own tears I wash away my balm, With mine own hands I give away my crown,","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Richard undoes his own coronation."},{"id":"481","who":"RICHARD","text":"O! that I were a mockery king of snow, Standing before the sun of Bolingbroke To melt myself away in water-drops!","themes":["identity","power"],"note":"Richard wishes to melt away."},{"id":"490","who":"RICHARD","text":"Was this face the face That every day under his household roof Did keep ten thousand men?","themes":["identity","kingship"],"note":"Richard questions his face in the mirror."},{"id":"492","who":"RICHARD","text":"How soon my sorrow hath destroy'd my face.","themes":["grief","identity"],"note":"Richard after smashing the mirror."},{"id":"494","who":"RICHARD","text":"my grief lies all within; And these external manner of laments Are merely shadows to the unseen grief That swells with silence in the tortur'd soul.","themes":["grief","identity"],"note":"Richard answers Bolingbroke's 'shadow' remark."},{"id":"504","who":"RICHARD","text":"O, good! convey? conveyers are you all, That rise thus nimbly by a true king's fall.","themes":["language","power"],"note":"Richard puns on 'convey' (carry / steal)."},{"id":"518","who":"RICHARD","text":"I am sworn brother, sweet, To grim Necessity; and he and I Will keep a league till death.","themes":["grief","time"],"note":"Richard accepts his fate."},{"id":"523","who":"RICHARD","text":"The love of wicked men converts to fear; That fear to hate; and hate turns one or both To worthy danger and deserved death.","themes":["power","loyalty"],"note":"Richard warns Northumberland."},{"id":"547","who":"YORK","text":"As in a theatre, the eyes of men After a well-grac'd actor leaves the stage Are idly bent on him that enters next, Thinking his prattle to be tedious;","themes":["power","kingship"],"note":"York describes the crowd ignoring Richard."},{"id":"630","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"Our scene is alter'd from a serious thing, And now chang'd to 'The Beggar and the King.'","themes":["power","language"],"note":"Bolingbroke jokes as the Duchess begs."},{"id":"645","who":"DUCHESS OF YORK","text":"No word like 'pardon' for kings' mouths so meet.","themes":["justice","language"],"note":"The Duchess pleads for Aumerle."},{"id":"660","who":"EXTON","text":"'Have I no friend will rid me of this living fear?'","themes":["justice","power"],"note":"Exton repeats Bolingbroke's words."},{"id":"668","who":"RICHARD","text":"Thus play I in one person many people, And none contented:","themes":["identity"],"note":"Richard alone in prison."},{"id":"668","who":"RICHARD","text":"I wasted time, and now doth time waste me;","themes":["time"],"note":"Richard's prison realisation."},{"id":"668","who":"RICHARD","text":"Nor I, nor any man that but man is With nothing shall be pleas'd till he be eas'd With being nothing.","themes":["identity","grief"],"note":"Richard: only death will satisfy."},{"id":"692","who":"RICHARD","text":"Mount, mount, my soul! thy seat is up on high; Whilst my gross flesh sinks downward, here to die.","themes":["kingship"],"note":"Richard's last words."},{"id":"694","who":"EXTON","text":"As full of valour as of royal blood: Both have I spilt;","themes":["justice"],"note":"Exton over Richard's body."},{"id":"713","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"They love not poison that do poison need, Nor do I thee: though I did wish him dead, I hate the murderer, love him murdered.","themes":["justice","power"],"note":"Bolingbroke rejects Exton."},{"id":"713","who":"BOLINGBROKE","text":"I'll make a voyage to the Holy Land, To wash this blood off from my guilty hand.","themes":["justice","kingship"],"note":"Bolingbroke's last vow."}]};
const EX={"intro":["King Richard II has ruled England since he was a boy. He is graceful, eloquent and certain that God chose him, but he wastes money on favourites, leases out his kingdom and is secretly to blame for the murder of his uncle, the Duke of Gloucester.","His cousin Henry Bolingbroke accuses Thomas Mowbray of that murder. Richard banishes them both, then, when Bolingbroke's father John of Gaunt dies, seizes all his lands and leaves for a war in Ireland.","Bolingbroke comes back with an army to claim his inheritance. The nobles and the people join him, and Richard is forced to give up the crown."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"Bolingbroke accuses Mowbray of treason","id":11},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The Duchess of Gloucester begs Gaunt for revenge","id":37},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Richard stops the combat and banishes both men","id":81},{"s":"a1s3","t":"Mowbray banished for life; Bolingbroke for six years","id":94},{"s":"a1s4","t":"Richard hopes Gaunt dies quickly","id":136},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Gaunt's 'sceptred isle' speech","id":145},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Gaunt dies; Richard seizes his lands","id":170},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The lords ride to join Bolingbroke","id":200},{"s":"a2s2","t":"News comes that Bolingbroke has landed","id":215},{"s":"a2s3","t":"York gives way to Bolingbroke","id":294},{"s":"a2s4","t":"The Welsh army breaks up","id":302},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Bushy and Green are executed","id":308},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Richard lands in Wales to disaster after disaster","id":327},{"s":"a3s2","t":"'Let us sit upon the ground'","id":343},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Richard submits at Flint Castle","id":382},{"s":"a3s4","t":"A gardener tells the Queen the news","id":423},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Carlisle's prophecy of civil war","id":460},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Richard gives up the crown and breaks the mirror","id":490},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Richard and the Queen part for ever","id":533},{"s":"a5s2","t":"York discovers Aumerle's plot","id":567},{"s":"a5s3","t":"The Duchess wins Aumerle's pardon","id":650},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Exton takes the king's hint","id":664},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Richard is murdered at Pomfret","id":692},{"s":"a5s6","t":"Bolingbroke rejects Exton and vows a crusade","id":713}],"irony":{"19":"Richard swears to judge Gloucester's death impartially, though he ordered it himself.","63":"Richard embraces Bolingbroke as 'my blood' minutes before banishing him.","136":"Richard prays to arrive too late at Gaunt's bedside; soon his own army will arrive 'one day too late' for him.","177":"Richard makes York governor straight after York has openly warned him.","324":"Richard promises that angels will fight for him just before the news that his soldiers have gone.","331":"Richard is sure York has power enough to save him; York has already joined Bolingbroke.","389":"Bolingbroke says he has come 'but for mine own' while his army surrounds the king.","461":"Northumberland arrests Carlisle for treason for defending the anointed king.","552":"Aumerle says he does not care to be a 'violet' of the new spring while hiding a plot against the new king.","562":"The Duchess thinks the sealed paper is a bill for fancy clothes; it is a plan to kill the king.","598":"Bolingbroke despairs of his wild son, who will become the hero-king Henry V.","710":"Exton expects thanks for doing exactly what the king wished for."},"devices":{"sun":"The sun and the heavens","garden":"Gardens and growth","blood":"Blood and the earth","theatre":"Theatre and performance","updown":"Rising and falling","mirror":"Shadows and mirrors"},"dev":{"16":[["blood","Abel's blood cries out from the earth for revenge: Gloucester as the first murder victim.","like sacrificing Abel's"]],"37":[["blood","Edward III's sons as seven vials of one sacred blood.","seven vials of his sacred blood"],["garden","The royal family as branches from one root, one of them hacked down.","seven fair branches"]],"82":[["sun","The sun that warms Richard will shine on Bolingbroke too: the first hint of a rival sun.","That sun that warms you here"]],"145":[["garden","England as Eden, a 'demi-paradise', now leased out.","This other Eden"]],"208":[["mirror","Grief's twenty shadows: things seen through tears look multiplied and false.","twenty shadows"]],"295":[["garden","The favourites as caterpillars eating the commonwealth.","The caterpillars of the commonwealth"]],"304":[["sun","Richard's glory as a shooting star and a sun setting in the west.","like a shooting star"],["updown","Falling from the sky to the base earth.","Fall to the base earth"]],"324":[["sun","Richard as the sun whose rising exposes the night-time traitor.","the searching eye of heaven"]],"343":[["theatre","Death lets the king play 'a little scene' before he bores through the castle wall.","a little scene"]],"368":[["sun","Fire and water: Bolingbroke will be 'the yielding water' to Richard's fire.","Be he the fire"]],"370":[["sun","Percy sees Richard on the walls as the blushing sun.","the blushing discontented sun"]],"372":[["blood","War will 'bedew' England's grass with English blood.","faithful English blood"]],"382":[["updown","Richard comes down from the walls: 'Down, down I come'.","Down, down I come"],["sun","Phaethon crashed the chariot of the sun.","glist'ring Phaethon"]],"413":[["garden","Over-heavy apricots must be propped up and over-tall shoots cut down.","dangling apricocks"]],"417":[["garden","Richard did not prune his 'great and growing men'.","superfluous branches"]],"460":[["blood","English blood will 'manure the ground' if Bolingbroke is crowned.","The blood of English shall manure the ground"]],"469":[["updown","The crown as a well with two buckets: one rises, one sinks.","like a deep well"]],"481":[["sun","Richard melts like a snow king before Bolingbroke's sun.","mockery king of snow"]],"490":[["mirror","Richard reads his face in a mirror instead of reading his crimes.","Give me that glass"]],"493":[["mirror","Bolingbroke: only the shadow of a face was destroyed by the shadow of sorrow.","The shadow of your sorrow"]],"547":[["theatre","The crowd looks at Richard like an audience bored by the actor after the star.","As in a theatre"]],"630":[["theatre","Bolingbroke names the scene like a ballad or play.","The Beggar and the King"]],"668":[["theatre","Richard plays 'many people' in one person.","Thus play I in one person many people"]],"692":[["updown","The soul rises as the body sinks.","Mount, mount, my soul"]],"713":[["blood","Bolingbroke must wash Richard's blood from his hands.","To wash this blood off"]]},"stage":{"palace":"A royal palace","lists":"The lists at Coventry","field":"Open country","coast":"The Welsh coast","castle":"Castle walls","garden":"The garden","hall":"Westminster Hall","street":"A London street","dungeon":"The dungeon"},"why":{"0":"The first mention of the blood that runs through the play, ending with Exton sent off 'with Cain'.","1":"Shows the noble code of honour that makes compromise impossible.","2":"The gap between what a king claims and what he can do.","3":"The clearest statement of divine right, and it confirms Richard's guilt.","4":"Raises the question every loyal subject faces: is obedience cowardice?","5":"Ties identity to English speech and land.","6":"Royal breath seems all-powerful; later 'the breath of worldly men' will depose a king.","7":"Bolingbroke is practical and literal; Richard will live inside imagination.","8":"He leaves as an Englishman, not a courtier: the people's man.","9":"An early prophecy that Richard will regret trusting Bolingbroke's silence.","10":"Richard sees the danger and does nothing about it.","11":"His greed makes it hard to pity him until much later.","12":"Words of truth against the flatterers' music.","13":"The most famous patriotic speech in Shakespeare, and it ends in shame.","14":"England as a 'plot' of land prepares for the garden scene.","15":"Self-conquest: civil war and self-ruin are the play's subject.","16":"Prepares for Richard's own image of Death sitting inside the hollow crown.","17":"Leasing the realm turns a king into a mere property owner.","18":"The key political argument: by breaking inheritance, Richard breaks his own claim.","19":"Blaming the flatterers lets the nobles rebel while seeming loyal.","20":"Substance and shadow return in the mirror scene.","21":"The dilemma at the centre of the play, put in the plainest words.","22":"Words are weapons: York will not let flattery hide treason.","23":"His careful choice of title hides how far he will go.","24":"Prepares for the gardener's caterpillars and weeds.","25":"Richard as a heavenly body falling begins here.","26":"Richard loves England as a poet; Gaunt loved it as a patriot.","27":"Within a scene, and then a play, the breath of worldly men does exactly that.","28":"The angels never come; real armies do.","29":"Time and delay ruin Richard; he will say 'I wasted time' in prison.","30":"He trusts a name instead of an army; he will later lose the name too.","31":"He becomes the poet of his own fall.","32":"Kingship is a show put on for a mortal body.","33":"Pun on 'subjected': a king subject to need is no different from his subjects.","34":"He hands over the sun image to his rival.","35":"Water 'yields' but it puts fire out.","36":"Richard still has the appearance of majesty, but not the power.","37":"He rehearses the deposition before anyone demands it.","38":"The repeated 'little' shrinks Richard from king to nothing.","39":"Phaethon could not control the sun's horses: a myth of a ruler who fails.","40":"The plainest admission that might, not right, now decides.","41":"Gaunt's 'sceptred isle' has become a neglected garden.","42":"Good rule cuts back the over-mighty; Richard failed to.","43":"A small act of pity from a common man.","44":"The question the whole of Act 4 tries to answer.","45":"Looks ahead to the Wars of the Roses, which Elizabethan audiences knew well.","46":"Self-pity, but also a claim to sacred kingship.","47":"One rises as the other sinks: the see-saw of fortune.","48":"Grief becomes his new crown.","49":"The pun on 'ay' and 'I' shows the self dissolving.","50":"Reverses 'Not all the water in the rough rude sea can wash the balm'.","51":"The sun image now belongs to Bolingbroke.","52":"Echoes Marlowe's Helen of Troy: 'Was this the face that launched a thousand ships?'","53":"He stages his fall as a moral show.","54":"Inner self against outward show: Hamlet will say the same.","55":"His wit survives his crown.","56":"His calm frustrates the Queen, who wants him to fight.","57":"A prophecy fulfilled in Henry IV, when the Percys rebel.","58":"Politics as theatre: the audience has moved on to the new star.","59":"He can see his own reign as a play, as Richard did.","60":"Bolingbroke's 'breath' now has a king's power.","61":"The new king's hint leads to murder, as Richard's did with Gloucester.","62":"He is an actor of many roles, and none satisfies him.","63":"The clearest confession of failure in the play.","64":"The play's long thread of 'nothing' ends here.","65":"The last 'up and down' image: the soul rises as the body falls.","66":"Instant regret, as with Bolingbroke.","67":"He wants the result but not the guilt.","68":"The play ends with guilt that the next three plays will not wash away."},"arc":{"Richard's power":[9,9,10,9,8,6,5,4,3,3,2,2,1,1,0,0,0,0,0],"Bolingbroke's power":[6,6,3,2,2,4,6,7,8,8,9,9,10,10,10,9,9,9,8]},"arcnote":"Richard's power is highest when he stops the combat at Coventry and falls steadily from the moment he seizes Gaunt's lands. Bolingbroke rises from exile to the throne, but in the last scenes plots, a murder and his own guilt start to weigh on the new king.","context":[["Date","Probably written in 1595, soon after Samuel Daniel's poem The Civil Wars, which Shakespeare seems to have used."],["Printing","Printed in 1597 and reprinted twice in 1598, which shows how popular it was. The deposition episode in 4.1 is missing from these early editions and first appears in the edition of 1608."],["Sources","Mainly Raphael Holinshed's Chronicles (1587), with details from Daniel's Civil Wars and probably other chronicles and an older play about Thomas of Woodstock."],["The real Richard","Richard II (1367–1400), son of the Black Prince, became king at ten. Gloucester died in custody at Calais in 1397; Bolingbroke landed in 1399 while Richard was in Ireland; Richard was deposed that year and died at Pontefract (Pomfret) in 1400."],["Divine right","Tudor teaching (in sermons read in every church) said a king was God's deputy and that rebellion was a sin. The play tests this: the king is anointed, but unfit to rule."],["The Essex rising","On the eve of the Earl of Essex's rebellion in February 1601, his supporters paid Shakespeare's company to perform a play about Richard II's deposition, almost certainly this one. Queen Elizabeth is reported to have said 'I am Richard II, know ye not that?'"],["Elizabeth and succession","Elizabeth was old, childless and had named no heir. A play about a monarch losing the crown to a cousin was politically sensitive."],["The Wars of the Roses","Bolingbroke's seizure of the crown began the struggle between the houses of Lancaster and York that Shakespeare had already dramatised in Henry VI and Richard III. Carlisle's prophecy points to it."],["The history cycle","Richard II begins a sequence with Henry IV Parts 1 and 2 and Henry V. Percy (Hotspur), Northumberland and the 'unthrifty son' Prince Hal all return."],["Chivalry","Trial by combat, gages and titles belong to a medieval world of honour. Richard's stopping of the combat in 1.3 marks the start of that world's collapse."],["Verse","The whole play is in verse, much of it rhymed, which is unusual for Shakespeare. The formal language suits a world of ceremony, and Richard's own poetry."],["The Queen","The real queen, Isabella of Valois, was a child of about ten in 1399. Shakespeare makes her a grown woman."]],"critics":{"kingship":[["Ernst Kantorowicz, The King's Two Bodies (1957)","Paraphrased: medieval kings were thought to have two bodies, a mortal natural one and an undying political one. The play shows the two bodies coming apart, most clearly when Richard breaks the mirror."],["Walter Pater, 'Shakespeare's English Kings' (1889)","Paraphrased: the deposition is like a coronation performed backwards, a ritual in which Richard himself is the poet and priest of his own unmaking."]],"power":[["E. M. W. Tillyard, Shakespeare's History Plays (1944)","Paraphrased: the play begins a great story of English history in which the sin of deposing a lawful king brings a curse of civil war that lasts until the Tudors."],["Jan Kott, Shakespeare Our Contemporary (1964)","Paraphrased: the history plays show a 'grand mechanism' or staircase of power: each ruler climbs it and is pushed off; Bolingbroke simply takes Richard's step."]],"identity":[["W. B. Yeats, 'At Stratford-on-Avon' (1901)","Paraphrased: Richard is a lyrical, imaginative man, a vessel of porcelain beside Bolingbroke's vessel of clay, and Shakespeare sympathises with the beauty of his failure."]],"language":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge, lectures on Shakespeare (1811–19)","Paraphrased: Richard turns every crisis into eloquent words and self-pity; his gift for language replaces action."]]},"insults":[{"id":13,"f":"Pale trembling coward","who":"Bolingbroke","n":"The challenge begins with a charge of cowardice."},{"id":12,"f":"Call him a slanderous coward and a villain","who":"Mowbray","n":"Mowbray answers in kind before the king."},{"id":158,"f":"Landlord of England art thou now, not king","who":"Gaunt","n":"Gaunt's insult is political: Richard has turned his kingdom into property."},{"id":159,"f":"a lunatic lean-witted fool","who":"Richard","n":"Richard to his dying uncle."},{"id":285,"f":"Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle","who":"York","n":"York refuses the polite titles that would cover treason."},{"id":338,"f":"O villains, vipers, damn'd without redemption!","who":"Richard","n":"Richard curses his favourites, wrongly believing they have changed sides."},{"id":338,"f":"Three Judases, each one thrice worse than Judas!","who":"Richard","n":"He casts himself as Christ betrayed."},{"id":422,"f":"Thou, old Adam's likeness","who":"Queen","n":"The gardener becomes a fallen Adam, bringing a second Fall."},{"id":449,"f":"Dishonourable boy!","who":"Surrey","n":"Surrey sneers at Fitzwater's youth."},{"id":481,"f":"thou haught insulting man","who":"Richard","n":"Richard to Northumberland, who keeps pressing him."},{"id":485,"f":"Fiend! thou torments me ere I come to hell.","who":"Richard","n":"Northumberland as a devil tormenting him early."},{"id":504,"f":"conveyers are you all","who":"Richard","n":"Richard calls the whole court thieves."},{"id":523,"f":"thou ladder wherewithal The mounting Bolingbroke ascends my throne","who":"Richard","n":"Northumberland is a tool that will be thrown away."},{"id":634,"f":"Thou frantic woman","who":"York","n":"York to his wife as she pleads for their son."},{"id":684,"f":"The devil take Henry of Lancaster and thee!","who":"Richard","n":"Richard refuses Bolingbroke the title of king."},{"id":713,"f":"With Cain go wander thorough shade of night","who":"Bolingbroke","n":"Exton is branded with the mark of the first murderer."}],"pronouns":[{"id":19,"f":"He is our subject, Mowbray; so art thou","n":"Richard speaks as 'we' and calls his subject 'thou': the voice of a king."},{"id":285,"f":"Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle","n":"York, angry, uses 'thee' and 'boy' to the nephew who has invaded."},{"id":388,"f":"Fair cousin, you debase your princely knee","n":"Powerless now, Richard uses careful, polite 'you' to Bolingbroke."},{"id":481,"f":"No lord of thine, thou haught insulting man","n":"Richard flings 'thou' and 'thine' at Northumberland as an insult."},{"id":518,"f":"Hie thee to France","n":"Richard uses tender 'thee' to his wife."},{"id":519,"f":"Hath Bolingbroke depos'd Thine intellect?","n":"The Queen's loving 'thine' becomes a challenge."},{"id":637,"f":"Not yet, I thee beseech","n":"The Duchess uses intimate 'thee' to the new king, her nephew."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["They fight there and then","Richard sets a trial by combat","Mowbray confesses"],"a1s2":["Gaunt avenges Gloucester","The combat goes ahead at Coventry","The Duchess goes to the king"],"a1s3":["Bolingbroke kills Mowbray","Richard stops the fight and banishes both","Mowbray wins"],"a1s4":["Richard makes peace with Gaunt","Richard seizes Gaunt's wealth","Richard calls Bolingbroke home"],"a2s1":["Richard cancels the Irish war","Bolingbroke lands with an army","Northumberland betrays the rebels"],"a2s2":["York fights Bolingbroke","York gives way to Bolingbroke","The Queen flees to France"],"a2s3":["The Welsh army fights for Richard","Richard's army melts away","Bolingbroke sails to Ireland"],"a2s4":["Bolingbroke pardons the favourites","Bolingbroke executes the favourites","The favourites escape to Ireland"],"a3s1":["Richard returns to a strong army","Richard returns to find his support gone","Richard stays in Ireland"],"a3s2":["Richard fights at Flint","Richard surrenders to Bolingbroke","Richard escapes by sea"],"a3s3":["The Queen hears the news from a gardener","The Queen rescues Richard","The Queen sails to France"],"a3s4":["Richard gives up the crown in public","Bolingbroke refuses the crown","Richard is killed at once"],"a4s1":["Richard is freed","Richard and the Queen part for ever","The plot succeeds"],"a5s1":["The plot is discovered","Aumerle becomes king","York joins the plot"],"a5s2":["Aumerle is executed","Aumerle is pardoned","York is arrested"],"a5s3":["Someone takes the king's hint to kill Richard","Richard is released","Bolingbroke visits Richard"],"a5s4":["Richard escapes","Richard is murdered","Richard is pardoned"],"a5s5":["Bolingbroke rewards Exton","Bolingbroke rejects Exton","Exton becomes king"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Richard and Bolingbroke","pts":["Richard talks in elaborate images; Bolingbroke says little and acts.","Richard trusts in God and his title; Bolingbroke trusts in armies and the people.","Richard grows more sympathetic as he falls; Bolingbroke more troubled as he rises."],"ids":[324,288]},{"t":"Gaunt and York","pts":["Both are Richard's uncles and believe in loyalty to the king.","Gaunt speaks his mind to Richard's face and dies before the rebellion.","York warns, wavers, gives way and then serves the new king as firmly as the old."],"ids":[158,235]},{"t":"The two appeal scenes (1.1 and 4.1)","pts":["Both open with accusations about Gloucester's death and gloves thrown down.","In 1.1 the king controls the quarrel; in 4.1 Bolingbroke does.","Neither scene ever reaches the truth about the murder."],"ids":[11,430]},{"t":"The Queen and the Duchess of York","pts":["Both are women pleading for the men they love.","The Queen can only grieve; the Duchess acts, rides and wins a pardon.","The Queen is sent away; the Duchess changes the king's mind."],"ids":[519,645]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Tragedy of King Richard the Second"],["Written","About 1595; first printed 1597 without the deposition episode, which was added in 1608"],["Genre","History play, printed as a tragedy; the first play of the sequence that continues with Henry IV and Henry V"],["Setting","England and Wales, 1398 to 1400: London, Coventry, Gloucestershire, the Welsh coast, Flint Castle, Westminster, Windsor and Pomfret"],["Language","Entirely in verse, with many rhyming couplets"],["Main plot","Richard banishes Bolingbroke and seizes his inheritance; Bolingbroke returns with an army and Richard is forced to give up the crown"],["Second plot","Aumerle's plot against the new king, exposed by his father and forgiven at his mother's plea"],["Turning point","2.1: Richard seizes Gaunt's lands, giving Bolingbroke his reason to return"],["Climax","4.1: the deposition in Westminster Hall and the broken mirror"],["Resolution","Richard is murdered at Pomfret; Bolingbroke rejects the murderer and vows a crusade"],["Key themes","Kingship and divine right, usurpation, identity, loyalty, England, grief, words, time, guilt"],["Famous for","'This sceptred isle', 'Let us sit upon the ground and tell sad stories of the death of kings', the mirror scene"]],"analysis":{"RICHARD":["At the start Richard looks every inch a king, presiding over a formal ceremony of justice. But he is hiding his own part in Gloucester's murder, and his decisions (stopping the combat, the unequal sentences, seizing Gaunt's lands) are theatrical and self-serving. He mocks the dying Gaunt and wishes him dead for his money.","He believes completely that God made him king and will protect him: 'The breath of worldly men cannot depose the deputy elected by the Lord.' When armies desert him, that belief turns into despair. His moods swing from defiance to collapse within a few lines.","As his power drains away, his language becomes richer. He sits on the ground and imagines Death in the hollow crown, gives his kingdom for 'a little little grave', comes 'down, down' like Phaethon and, at Westminster, turns his deposition into a performance he directs himself, ending with the broken mirror.","In prison, alone for the first time, he examines himself: 'I wasted time, and now doth time waste me.' He dies fighting, and the play ends with his enemies mourning him. Audiences who disliked the king in Act 1 often pity the man in Act 5."],"BOLINGBROKE":["Bolingbroke is a man of action and few words. He accuses Mowbray publicly, accepts his banishment without complaint and leaves as 'a true-born Englishman', having charmed the common people.","He returns saying he wants only his inheritance as Duke of Lancaster, and he kneels to Richard at Flint. But he also executes Bushy and Green without authority and his army does the talking. We never hear him say he wants the crown; he simply accepts it 'in God's name'.","As king he is shrewd: he settles quarrels, spares Carlisle and pardons Aumerle while crushing the other plotters. But his hint to Exton leads to Richard's murder, and he ends the play disowning the killer and promising a crusade to wash his hands, a guilt that will haunt him through Henry IV."],"GAUNT":["Gaunt is the voice of the old loyalty. He refuses to take revenge for his own brother because the king is God's deputy, and he agrees, as a councillor, to banish his own son, which breaks his heart.","On his deathbed he becomes a prophet. His great speech praises England as 'this sceptred isle' and 'this other Eden', then condemns Richard for leasing it out. He tells Richard to his face that he is 'landlord of England', not king.","His death removes the last restraint on Richard, and Richard's seizure of his estate sets the rebellion in motion."],"YORK":["York is an old, honest man caught between duty to the king and conscience. He warns Richard that seizing Bolingbroke's inheritance destroys the very idea of succession, then is left in charge of the country.","When Bolingbroke arrives he rebukes him ('Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle') but cannot resist and declares himself neutral. He is the first to tell the court that Richard will give up the crown.","His account of Richard's entry into London is full of pity. Yet once he has accepted Bolingbroke, he is fiercely loyal: he discovers his son's plot and begs the new king to punish him. His fussing over boots and horses gives his scenes a touch of comedy."],"NORTHUMBERLAND":["Northumberland is the first great lord to join Bolingbroke and the most ruthless. He gathers the other rebels, flatters Bolingbroke on the road and brings Richard his messages.","He drops Richard's title, presses him to read out his crimes in Westminster Hall and arrests Carlisle. He parts Richard from his Queen. Richard predicts that he and Bolingbroke will fall out, as they do in Henry IV."],"QUEEN":["The Queen feels grief before she has any reason, a 'nameless woe' that soon gets its name. She learns of Richard's fall from a gardener, and her curse on him is answered with pity.","In the street she is shocked that Richard accepts his fall meekly and urges him to fight like a dying lion. Their parting, in rhyme, is the most tender scene in the play."],"AUMERLE":["Aumerle is Richard's loyal cousin, who stays with him to the end at Flint. In Westminster Hall he is accused of helping to kill Gloucester and challenged by lord after lord.","He joins the Abbot's plot against the new king, but his father discovers it. He races to the king and begs a pardon, which his mother wins for him. He is weaker than his loyalty, and the scene treats him almost as a boy."],"CARLISLE":["The Bishop of Carlisle is the one man who speaks for divine right in public. He tells Richard to act rather than despair, and in Westminster Hall he declares that no subject can judge a king.","His prophecy that England will become 'the field of Golgotha' is arrested as treason. In the end Bolingbroke spares him, recognising 'high sparks of honour' in an enemy."]},"motifs":[["The sun","Richard is the sun of England, rising to expose traitors; by the end he is a snow king melting before 'the sun of Bolingbroke'.",[25,34,51]],["Rising and falling","Richard comes 'down, down' from Flint's walls, the crown is a well with two buckets and in death his soul rises while his body sinks.",[39,47,65]],["Gardens and the land","England is Eden in Gaunt's speech, a weed-choked garden in the gardener's, and the earth Richard greets with his hand.",[13,41,42]],["Blood","From Abel's blood crying from the earth to Bolingbroke washing Richard's blood from his hands, blood stands for murder, guilt and family.",[0,45,68]],["Names and titles","Gaunt puns on his name, Bolingbroke comes 'for Lancaster', Richard says the king's name is 'twenty thousand names' and later that he has none.",[30,23,49]],["Theatre","Richard turns his fall into scenes; York compares him to an actor leaving the stage; Bolingbroke calls one scene 'The Beggar and the King'.",[58,59,62]]],"symbols2":[["The crown","Kingship itself. Death sits inside it; the two cousins both hold it like a well with two buckets; Richard hands it over with his own hands."],["The mirror","Richard's face and identity. He asks for it instead of reading his crimes and smashes it: his glory is as brittle as glass."],["Gages","The gloves thrown down as challenges stand for honour and the medieval code of trial by combat, which never reaches the truth."],["The garden","England as a garden that needs pruning and weeding: the gardener's lesson in good government."],["Roan Barbary","Richard's horse, now ridden by Bolingbroke: loyalty that passes to whoever holds power."],["Balm","The holy oil of coronation. Richard says the sea cannot wash it off, then washes it away with his own tears."]],"bigq":[["Is Richard a bad king or a tragic hero?",["He was behind Gloucester's murder and rules for himself","He leases the kingdom and steals Gaunt's estate","He grows in self-knowledge as he loses power","His poetry and suffering win our sympathy at the end"],[17,11,63,65]],["Is Bolingbroke a usurper or a rescuer?",["He was wronged and asks only for his inheritance","England was badly ruled and the nobles welcome him","He never states his ambition but always gains","He wishes Richard dead and is left guilty"],[23,35,61,67]],["Does the play support divine right?",["Gaunt and Carlisle believe it absolutely","Richard trusts it, and it does not save him","Carlisle's prophecy suggests deposing a king brings a curse","The 'hollow crown' shows a king is just a man"],[3,27,44,33]],["Is York loyal or a turncoat?",["He warns Richard honestly","He gives way to Bolingbroke without a fight","He pities Richard but serves Henry","He puts loyalty to the king above his own son"],[18,21,58]],["Is Richard's language his strength or his weakness?",["His words create beauty and win sympathy","He talks instead of acting","He controls his deposition through performance","Bolingbroke wins with few words"],[31,32,49,55]],["Who is to blame for Richard's fall?",["Richard himself, for waste, greed and the seizure","The favourites, the 'caterpillars of the commonwealth'","Bolingbroke and Northumberland's ambition","Fortune and time, the turning wheel"],[42,24,57,29]]],"symbols":{"The crown":"\\bcrowns?\\b","The sun and day":"\\bsun\\b|\\bday's?\\b","Blood":"\\bblood","Earth and land":"\\bearth\\b|\\bland\\b|\\bsoil\\b|\\bground\\b","Tears and weeping":"\\btears?\\b|\\bweep","Names and titles":"\\bnames?\\b|\\btitles?\\b"},"stagerules":[["lists","lists"],["dungeon","dungeon"],["garden","garden"],["hall","hall"],["street","street"],["flint","castle"],["coast","coast"],["wolds|camp","field"],["palace|castle|room|apartment|house","palace"]],"solo":{"304":1,"668":1},"iago":[["a1s1","Accuses Mowbray of treason and of Gloucester's murder, a charge that also points at the king."],["a1s3","Leaves for exile as 'a true-born Englishman', having courted the common people."],["a2s1","Sets sail with soldiers from Brittany as soon as Richard leaves for Ireland (reported)."],["a2s3","Wins over Northumberland, Percy, Ross and Willoughby."],["a2s3","Tells York he has come only 'for Lancaster', and gains his neutrality."],["a3s1","Executes Bushy and Green without royal authority."],["a3s3","Kneels to Richard at Flint while his army surrounds the castle."],["a3s3","Takes Richard with him to London."],["a4s1","Accepts the crown: 'In God's name, I'll ascend the regal throne.'"],["a4s1","Sends Richard to the Tower and sets the date of his coronation."],["a5s3","Pardons Aumerle but orders death for the other plotters."],["a5s4","Lets Exton hear him wish for a 'friend' to rid him of Richard."],["a5s6","Rejects Exton and vows a crusade to wash off Richard's blood."]],"rel":{"1":[["BOLINGBROKE","MOWBRAY","hate"],["MOWBRAY","BOLINGBROKE","hate"],["RICHARD","BOLINGBROKE","deceive"],["GAUNT","RICHARD","trust"],["GAUNT","BOLINGBROKE","love"],["DUCHESS OF GLOUCESTER","MOWBRAY","hate"]],"2":[["GAUNT","RICHARD","hate"],["RICHARD","GAUNT","hate"],["YORK","RICHARD","trust"],["QUEEN","RICHARD","love"],["NORTHUMBERLAND","BOLINGBROKE","trust"],["PERCY","BOLINGBROKE","trust"],["BOLINGBROKE","BUSHY","hate"]],"3":[["BOLINGBROKE","RICHARD","deceive"],["NORTHUMBERLAND","RICHARD","hate"],["AUMERLE","RICHARD","love"],["CARLISLE","RICHARD","trust"],["YORK","BOLINGBROKE","trust"],["QUEEN","RICHARD","love"]],"4":[["CARLISLE","BOLINGBROKE","hate"],["BAGOT","AUMERLE","hate"],["AUMERLE","BOLINGBROKE","hate"],["NORTHUMBERLAND","RICHARD","hate"],["YORK","BOLINGBROKE","trust"],["RICHARD","BOLINGBROKE","hate"]],"5":[["QUEEN","RICHARD","love"],["AUMERLE","BOLINGBROKE","deceive"],["YORK","AUMERLE","hate"],["DUCHESS OF YORK","AUMERLE","love"],["BOLINGBROKE","AUMERLE","trust"],["EXTON","RICHARD","hate"],["BOLINGBROKE","EXTON","hate"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Richard as a king?",[2,11,27,32,33,63]],["How does Shakespeare present the character of Bolingbroke?",[7,8,23,35,61,67]],["Explore how Shakespeare presents ideas about England and the land.",[13,14,15,26,41,45]],["How does Shakespeare explore the idea of divine right?",[3,27,28,44,50]],["How is Richard's identity shown to change through the play?",[30,49,51,52,62,64]],["How does Shakespeare present loyalty and betrayal?",[1,21,22,46,57]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short, embedded quotations","Analysed key words and images, not just the meaning","Linked to context (divine right, Elizabeth and the succession, the Wars of the Roses)","Tracked change across the whole play: beginning, middle, end","Considered the audience and dramatic effect","Mentioned an alternative reading or critic","Wrote a conclusion that answers the question","Checked spelling of names: Bolingbroke, Aumerle, Northumberland, Gaunt"],"place":{"a1s1":"London","a1s2":"London","a1s3":"Coventry","a1s4":"London","a2s1":"Ely House, London","a2s2":"London","a2s3":"Gloucestershire","a2s4":"Wales","a3s1":"Bristol","a3s2":"Wales","a3s3":"Flint, Wales","a3s4":"Langley","a4s1":"Westminster","a5s1":"London","a5s2":"London","a5s3":"Windsor","a5s4":"Windsor","a5s5":"Pomfret","a5s6":"Windsor"},"models":[[27,"Richard is presented as a king who trusts too much in the sacred nature of his office.","\"Not all the water in the rough rude sea Can wash the balm off from an anointed king\" uses hyperbole to claim kingship cannot be undone.","The audience see the irony, as Richard is about to lose his throne.","Kings were anointed with holy oil at coronation, a sign of divine right.","Some critics see his faith as noble; others see it as an excuse for inaction."],[35,"Bolingbroke is presented as practical and quietly ambitious.","Richard's \"Be he the fire, I'll be the yielding water\" contrasts their natures: Bolingbroke is force, Richard is passive.","The audience see the transfer of power through these images.","Bolingbroke became Henry IV, founder of the Lancastrian line.","His guilt at the end suggests power has a cost even for the practical."],[13,"England is presented as a sacred garden, now spoiled by bad rule.","Gaunt's \"This royal throne of kings, this scepter'd isle\" is a long list of praise that builds England into a paradise.","The audience feel the loss as Gaunt then condemns Richard's misrule.","Shakespeare's audience would hear patriotic pride in the speech.","The speech ends in criticism, so its patriotism is mixed with anger."],[3,"Divine right is presented as a powerful belief that the play then tests.","Gaunt's phrase \"God's substitute, His deputy anointed\" refuses to act against Richard, since only God can judge a king.","The audience see why rebels hesitate to act.","Elizabethans were taught that rebellion against a king was a sin.","The play shows Bolingbroke succeeding, which questions the belief."],[49,"Richard's identity breaks down as he loses the crown.","\"Ay, no; no, ay; for I must nothing be\" uses wordplay on \"ay\" and \"I\" to show his self dissolving.","The audience feel his confusion as his identity depends on being king.","Kingship was seen as a role and a sacred self.","Some critics see this as self-pity; others as tragic insight."],[22,"Loyalty and betrayal are tested as nobles choose sides.","York's \"Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle\" rejects polite titles, showing his anger at Bolingbroke's return.","The audience see York torn between loyalty and practicality.","Family ties and loyalty to the crown often conflicted in the Wars of the Roses period.","York later supports Bolingbroke, so his loyalty is shown as flexible."]],"lang":{"un":[[382,"Down, down I come","I come down, down"],[668,"I wasted time, and now doth time waste me","I wasted time, and now time doth waste me"],[475,"With mine own tears I wash away my balm","I wash away my balm with mine own tears"],[343,"Of comfort no man speak","No man speak of comfort"]],"pun":[[150,"Old Gaunt indeed, and gaunt in being old","gaunt","pun","Gaunt is his name and means thin and worn. A dying man still plays with words, which Richard mocks."],[475,"Ay, no; no, ay; for I must nothing be","ay","pun","Ay (yes) sounds like I. Saying yes to giving up the crown means saying no to himself."],[285,"Grace me no grace, nor uncle me no uncle","uncle","shift","Grace and uncle become verbs: York refuses polite titles from a nephew who has broken the law."],[382,"In the base court? Base court, where kings grow base","base","pun","The base court is the lower courtyard; base also means shameful. Richard's coming down the stairs becomes coming down in worth."]],"st":[[668,"I wasted time, and now doth time waste me",[["I","Admits his own fault, at last."],["time","Makes time the real enemy."],["me","Shows him as the victim of what he did."]]],[343,"For God's sake, let us sit upon the ground",[["God's","An urgent appeal: kings were God's deputies."],["sit","A king giving up standing, the posture of power."],["ground","Brings the king down to earth, literally."]]],[382,"Down, down I come",[["Down","Dramatic, like a falling star."],["I","Self-pity: he is still the centre of the scene."],["come","Accepts defeat by moving to meet it."]]]]},"dir":[[324,"Not all the water in the rough rude sea","How does Richard speak this?",[["Grandly, sure of himself","His faith in kingship looks magnificent, and blind."],["Too loudly, nervous","He is trying to convince himself."],["With his men looking away","Others already doubt him."]]],[343,"For God's sake, let us sit upon the ground","Does Richard really sit down?",[["Yes, at once","A king choosing to fall; self-pity."],["His followers stay standing","He is already alone."],["He half-sits, then rises","He can't commit even to despair."]]],[382,"Down, down I come","How does Richard come down to Bolingbroke?",[["Slowly, with dignity","He keeps some royal grace."],["Rushing, almost falling","His fall from power is physical."],["Mocking, playing to the crowd","He turns his defeat into theatre."]]],[469,"Here, cousin, seize the crown","How do the two men hold the crown?",[["Both gripping it a moment","The struggle for power is shown physically."],["Richard dangling it, teasing","He controls the moment even as he loses."],["Bolingbroke refusing to take it","He wants it given, not seized; politics."]]],[668,"I wasted time, and now doth time waste me","Where is Richard in his last soliloquy?",[["Alone in a dark cell","Isolation sharpens his insight."],["Lit by a single window","Hope and despair mixed."],["Pacing, restless","His mind won't settle even now."]]],[713,"I'll make a voyage to the Holy Land","How does Bolingbroke end the play?",[["Guilty, troubled","Power has a cost; the next history plays begin."],["Coldly political","He uses guilt as a performance."],["Standing over Richard's coffin","The image of usurpation stays with the audience."]]]]};
